window.COURSE = {"course":"CS105 — Advanced Programming","student":"","brandcfg":{"key":"cs105.v1","brand":"Bytecode","code":"CS105","sub":"CS105 · Advanced Programming (Java)<br>study traveler","eyebrow":"Class file · 2026/27 Fall · Dr. K. Oladejo","hero":"CS105 Study Traveler","tagline":"Every chapter compiles. Read it, trace it, write the code, pass the tests.","testHint":"Code tracing is the core skill: for every snippet in the notes, predict the output before reading the answer."},"deadlines":[],"chapters":[{"id":"00","title":"Course Map","slides":"8 decks · weeks 1–7 · Savitch Absolute Java","md":"# CS105 — Advanced Programming (Course Map)\n\n_Ingested 15.11.2026 from 8 lecture decks (weeks 1–7, 481 slides) in `lectures/`. **Instructor: Dr. Kazeem Oladejo**, the same instructor as CS313. The slides are Pearson's Savitch slides (Rose Williams, Kenrick Mock), updated by Dr. Oladejo, September 2026. **The course requires coding exercises**, so every week has a set of Java exercises with tests (`study/exercises-java/`)._\n\n## Textbooks (Week 1, slide 3)\n\n- **Walter Savitch, *Absolute Java*, 6th ed. (Pearson)** — the main text. The decks follow its chapters.\n- Carrano & Henry, *Data Structures and Abstractions with Java*, 4th ed. — the second text (no deck uses it yet).\n\n## Assessment (from the decks' own \"Course Assessment\" slides)\n\nThe full grading table is only on e-campus (Week 1, slide 4 is just a link). The decks give these items:\n\n| Week | Item | Points | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 6 | **Graded Lab One**: Inheritance I (4 practical + 1 theory) | 5 | Week 6, slide 2 |\n| 7 | **Quiz 1**: in hour 2 of the exceptions class | 7 | Week 7 (exceptions), slide 2; Week 6, slide 70 |\n| **8** | **Midterm exam** (16–20.11.2026, exact date TBA) | ? | Week 7, slide 2 |\n| 9 | No lecture: lab on Inheritance II | — | Week 7, slide 2 |\n| 10 | Polymorphism & abstract classes + **Graded Lab 2** | 10 | Week 7, slide 2 |\n\n⚠️ **Copy the midterm and final weights and the midterm date from e-campus into this file.** Assumed midterm scope: weeks 1–7 (every deck in this folder).\n\n## Weeks and decks\n\n| Week | Deck | Savitch | Topic | Notes | Exercises |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Week1_Lecture1_Introduction (85 slides) | ch. 1–3 | Java basics: types, expressions, strings, console I/O, flow of control | `01-java-basics.md` | `exercises-java/w01.md` |\n| 2 | Week2_SavichJava_Chap4 (82) | ch. 4 | Defining classes I: instance variables, methods, encapsulation, constructors, overloading | `02-defining-classes-i.md` | `w02.md` |\n| 3 | Week3_SavichJava_chap5 (87) | ch. 5 | Defining classes II: static, wrapper classes, references, class parameters, privacy leaks, packages | `03-defining-classes-ii.md` | `w03.md` |\n| 4 | Week4_SavichJava_chap12_UML (47) | ch. 12 | UML class diagrams and design patterns | `04-uml-and-patterns.md` | `w04.md` |\n| 5 | Week5_SavichJava_CopyConstr (33) | ch. 5 | Copy constructors, deep vs shallow copy, privacy leaks | `05-copy-constructors.md` | `w05.md` |\n| 6 | Week6_SavichJava_chap7_Inheritance (70) | ch. 7 | Inheritance: derived classes, overriding, super, protected, Object | `06-inheritance.md` | `w06.md` |\n| 7 | Week7_Savich_chap9_Exception (41) | ch. 9 | Exception handling: try/catch/finally, throw, throws, custom exceptions | `07-exception-handling.md` | `w07.md` |\n| 7 | Week7_Savich_chap10_FileIO (36) | ch. 10 | File I/O: text files, Scanner, PrintWriter, binary files | `08-file-io.md` | `w08.md` |\n\n## How to use the Java exercises\n\nEach `exercises-java/wNN.md` has 6–8 exercises (warm → core → hard). Every exercise has a **starter** (compiles but fails), a **test** (a `Main` class) and a **reference solution** at the bottom of the file.\n\n1. Copy the test, then the starter, into one file `Main.java`.\n2. Fill in the starter until `java Main.java` prints only PASS lines.\n3. Compare your code with the reference solution.\n\n`python3 study/exercises-java/check_java.py` confirms that every reference solution passes and every starter fails (JDK 26 is installed on this Mac).\n\n## Study tools\n\n- **Study site:** `website/index.html` (chapters, flashcards, slide images, the exercises as chapters).\n- **Quiz Trainer:** `website/quiz.html` (A–D, Yes/No and True/False questions, many of them code tracing).\n- **Exam prep:** `exam-prep.md`.\n","lesson":"","cards":[],"figs":[],"exercises":[]},{"id":"ex","title":"★ Exam Prep","slides":"likely questions · code tracing · weak spots","md":"# CS105 — Exam Prep (Midterm, week 8)\n\n_Built 15.11.2026 from the eight digests. The midterm is in **week 8** (Week 7 deck, slide 2); the scope is assumed to be weeks 1–7. Every digest ends with a **\"Likely exam questions\"** section with answers. This page ranks them and lists the traps._\n\n## What the exam will most likely ask\n\nProgramming exams on Savitch have four question types. Practise each one on paper, with no IDE:\n\n1. **Trace the code.** \"What is printed?\" Know integer division, `++` prefix/postfix, string concatenation with numbers, `==` vs `equals`, reference aliasing, overriding (dynamic binding), and try/catch/finally flow. → Quiz Trainer (most questions are traces) and the digests' exam sections.\n2. **Find the bug.** A missing `;`, `=` instead of `==`, a privacy leak, a shallow copy, catch blocks in the wrong order, `this(...)`/`super(...)` not the first statement, an unclosed `PrintWriter`.\n3. **Write a class.** Instance variables (private), constructors including a **copy constructor**, accessors and mutators, `equals(Object)` / `toString()`, a derived class using `super`. → Java exercises w02, w03, w05, w06.\n4. **Short theory / UML.** Checked vs unchecked exceptions, static vs instance, overloading vs overriding, `protected`, the UML visibility symbols + − # ~, deep vs shallow copy.\n\n## Week-by-week focus (★ = highest yield)\n\n| Week | Digest | Must-know |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `01-java-basics.md` | types and casting, integer division, `%`, String methods, `printf`, `switch`, loops |\n| 2 ★ | `02-defining-classes-i.md` | encapsulation, constructors, `this`, overloading, `==` vs `equals`; his 7 \"Some Questions\" (slides 47–53) |\n| 3 ★ | `03-defining-classes-ii.md` | `static`, wrapper classes / autoboxing, reference vs primitive parameters, privacy leaks, packages; his Q1–Q4 |\n| 4 | `04-uml-and-patterns.md` | class diagrams with + − # ~, associations, the patterns (Singleton, MVC, sorting); quick-sort bug on slide 40 |\n| 5 ★ | `05-copy-constructors.md` | copy constructor, deep vs shallow copy, aliasing, privacy leaks via mutable fields |\n| 6 ★ | `06-inheritance.md` | `extends`, `super`, overriding vs overloading, `protected`, `final`, `Object.equals`/`toString`, `instanceof` vs `getClass()`; the Device/Door/Thermostat example |\n| 7 ★ | `07-exception-handling.md` | try/catch/finally order, `throw` vs `throws`, checked vs unchecked, custom exception classes, catch order (specific first) |\n| 7 | `08-file-io.md` | `PrintWriter` (create vs append), always `close()`, `Scanner(new File(...))` vs `Scanner(\"text\")`, `BufferedReader.readLine()` returns null at EOF, `FileNotFoundException` |\n\n## Traps found on the slides (full lists in each digest's errata section)\n\n- **Week 4, slide 40:** the quick-sort code recurses on `splitPoint` instead of `splitPoint - 1`, so any repeated value gives a StackOverflowError (exercise w04 E6 fixes it).\n- **Week 6, Display 7.7:** EnhancedStringTokenizer stores each token twice, because the JDK's `nextToken(String)` calls the overridden `nextToken()` (exercise w06 E6).\n- **Week 5, slide 24:** `copy = original` is **aliasing**, not a shallow copy, because no new object is made.\n- **Week 5, slide 29:** `final` on a reference does **not** make the object immutable.\n- **Week 7 exceptions, slide 17:** the root of all exceptions is `Throwable`, not `Exception`.\n- **Week 7 exceptions, slide 36:** `RuntimeException` and its subclasses are **unchecked**.\n- **Week 7 file I/O, slide 11:** an unclosed `PrintWriter` can leave the file **empty**.\n- **Week 7 file I/O, slide 25:** `new Scanner(\"data.txt\")` reads the text \"data.txt\", not the file.\n- **Week 2, slide 46:** `==` on objects compiles, but it compares references.\n- **Exam rule vs modern Java:** JDK 25+ lets statements come before `this(...)`/`super(...)`. The textbook rule (they must come first) is what the exam expects.\n\n## Plan for the days before the midterm\n\n1. **Day 1:** do the Quiz Trainer for W2, W3 and W5, then exercises w05 E1–E4 (copy constructors).\n2. **Day 2:** do the W6 and W7 quizzes, then exercises w06 E1–E4 and w07 E1–E4.\n3. **Day 3:** do the \"Likely exam questions\" sections of all eight digests on paper, then mark them. Redo every wrong one.\n4. **Night before:** the flashcards (site → Test bench) and the traps list above.\n","lesson":"","cards":[],"figs":[],"exercises":[]},{"id":"01","title":"W1 · Java Basics (ch. 1–3)","slides":"Week 1 · 85 slides","md":"# 01 — Week 1 · Getting Started: Java Basics, Strings, Flow of Control (Savitch ch. 1–3, 85 slides)\n\n_Ingested 15.11.2026 from lectures/Week1_Lecture1_Introduction.pptx (PDF copy in lectures/). Pearson originals by Rose Williams and Kenrick Mock, \"Updated by Dr. Kazeem Oladejo, September 2026\". All 85 slides read; the 17 image-only slides (4, 10, 28, 39, 45, 49, 54–61, 72, 75, 76) were rendered and read visually. Every code example below was compiled and run on JDK 26._\n\n**Instructor's own additions (not in the Pearson deck)** — these show what Dr. Oladejo cares about:\n\n| Slide | What he added |\n|---|---|\n| 2 | Class rules: phones silent, no food/drink, no disruptive talking (sanctioned per IUS rulebook), questions welcome |\n| 3 | Textbooks: Savitch *Absolute Java* 6e; Carrano & Henry *Data Structures and Abstractions with Java* 4e |\n| 4 | \"Course Assessment\" — only a link to the E-Campus page (no numbers on the slide) |\n| **13–14** | **In-class Q&A: \"Is Java bytecode a machine language?\" → No** (intermediate instruction set for the JVM) |\n| **15** | **Q&A: \"How does Java bytecode look?\"** — stack-based, constant pool; `iload_1, iload_2, iadd, ireturn` |\n| 20 | `javap`, the class-file disassembler |\n| 21 | Environment: NetBeans IDE and/or command prompt (`C:\\Program Files\\Apache NetBeans\\jdk\\bin`) |\n| 27, 46 | DEMO slides (live coding in class) |\n| **45** | **Mini-exercise: \"Is `int i = 5.5` correct?\" → No; `int i = (int)5.5` is correct** |\n| **49** | Step-by-step trace of `2 * (++n)` vs `2 * (n++)` |\n\n---\n\n## 1. What Java is (slides 5–9)\n\n- Java was created by Sun Microsystems, team led by **James Gosling, 1991**. Now owned by **Oracle** (slide 6).\n- First designed for **home appliances**. Appliances use many different processors, so the team built a **two-step translation** (source → bytecode → machine code).\n- Java is **object-oriented (OOP)**: a program is a set of **objects** that interact through actions called **methods**. Objects of the same kind belong to the same **class** (slide 7).\n- Two kinds of programs (slides 8–9):\n  - **Application** (\"regular\" program): a class with a method named `main`. The run-time system calls `main` automatically. Every application starts at `main`.\n  - **Applet**: runs from a web browser (or an applet viewer for debugging); always uses a windowing interface. Applications may use windows or console I/O. (Applets are obsolete: the applet viewer was dropped in JDK 11 and the `java.applet` package no longer exists in JDK 26 — but know the definition for the exam.)\n\nSample program (slide 10, Display 1.1):\n\n```java\npublic class FirstProgram\n{\n    public static void main(String[] args)\n    {\n        System.out.println(\"Hello reader.\");\n        System.out.println(\"Welcome to Java.\");\n        System.out.println(\"Let's demonstrate a simple calculation.\");\n        int answer;\n        answer = 2 + 2;\n        System.out.println(\"2 plus 2 is \" + answer);\n    }\n}\n```\nOutput: `Hello reader.` / `Welcome to Java.` / `Let's demonstrate a simple calculation.` / `2 plus 2 is 4`.\n\n## 2. Compiling and running: bytecode, JVM, JIT, class loader (slides 11–21)\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| High-level language | People can read and write it; must be translated before running |\n| Machine language | What the computer's CPU understands |\n| Low-level language | Machine language or anything close to it |\n| Compiler | Translates a high-level program into a low-level one (\"compiling\") |\n| **Bytecode** | Machine language of a *fictitious* computer, the **Java Virtual Machine (JVM)**. Makes Java **portable** |\n| **Interpreter** | Translates bytecode to real machine code and runs it, one instruction at a time |\n| **JIT compiler** | Compiles a *section* of bytecode at a time into machine code. Most Java runs this way today |\n| **Class loader** | Connects the bytecode of all classes a program needs. In C++ this job is done by the **linker** |\n| `javap` | JDK tool that disassembles `.class` files (use `javap -c` to see the bytecode instructions) |\n\n- Most languages compile straight to one CPU's machine code → one compiler per CPU. Java compiles once to bytecode; any computer with a JVM can run it (slide 12).\n- **Slide 14 (instructor Q&A):** bytecode is **not** a CPU machine language (not x86-64, not ARM). It is an intermediate instruction set for the JVM. The JVM interprets it or JIT-compiles it to the host CPU's machine code at run time.\n- **Slide 15:** bytecode is **stack-based** (push/pop on an operand stack) and uses a **constant pool** in the class file. For `int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }` the bytecode is `iload_1, iload_2, iadd, ireturn`. Verified with `javap -c`. Why slot 1 and not 0? In an *instance* method, slot 0 holds `this`. In a `static` version the same method gives `iload_0, iload_1, iadd, ireturn`.\n- Translating bytecode to machine code is relatively easy compared with the first compilation step (slide 16).\n\nCommands (slides 18–19):\n\n```\njavac FirstProgram.java     // compile → produces FirstProgram.class (bytecode)\njava FirstProgram           // run: class name only, NO .java and NO .class extension\n```\n- The class `FirstProgram` must be in a file named `FirstProgram.java` (applies to `public` classes).\n- Run only the class that contains `main`; the other classes are loaded automatically.\n- `main` heading: `public static void main(String[] args)`.\n\n## 3. Identifiers and naming (slides 22–24)\n\n- An identifier **must not start with a digit**; the rest may be letters, digits, underscore. (Java also allows `$`, which the slide omits; avoid it by convention.) Any length.\n- Java is **case-sensitive**: `Rate`, `rate`, `RATE` are three different variables.\n- **Keywords / reserved words** (`public class void static`) cannot be used as names.\n- **Predefined identifiers** (`System String println`) come from the libraries. They *can* be redefined, but that is confusing and dangerous.\n- Conventions: variables, methods, objects start lowercase, word boundaries uppercase — `topSpeed`, `bankRate1`, `timeOfArrival` (\"camelCase\"). Classes start uppercase — `FirstProgram`, `MyClass`, `String`.\n\n## 4. Primitive types (slide 28, Display 1.2)\n\n| Type | Kind | Size | Range |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `boolean` | `true`/`false` | 1 byte | n/a |\n| `char` | one Unicode character | 2 bytes | all Unicode chars |\n| `byte` | integer | 1 byte | −128 … 127 |\n| `short` | integer | 2 bytes | −32768 … 32767 |\n| `int` | integer | 4 bytes | −2147483648 … 2147483647 |\n| `long` | integer | 8 bytes | about ±9.22 × 10¹⁸ |\n| `float` | floating point | 4 bytes | about ±3.4 × 10³⁸ |\n| `double` | floating point | 8 bytes | about ±1.8 × 10³⁰⁸ |\n\nEight primitives. **`String` is not a primitive** — it is a class (slide 50).\n\n## 5. Variables, assignment, constants (slides 25–37)\n\n- Declare: `int answer;` Declare + initialise: `int count = 0;`, `double distance = 55 * .5;`, `char grade = 'A';`\n- Mixed: `int initialCount = 50, finalCount;` — only the first is initialised (slide 32).\n- `=` is **assignment**. The right side is evaluated first, then stored. `count = count + 2;` is legal.\n- Assignment runs **right to left**, so it can be chained: `number2 = number1 = 3;` (both become 3).\n- **Uninitialised variables**: some get a default value in certain cases (instance variables), but do not rely on it. A **local** variable must be assigned before use or the compiler refuses (`variable k might not have been initialized`).\n- Shorthand (slide 33): `count += 2;` `sum -= discount;` `bonus *= 2;` `time /= rushFactor;` `change %= 100;`\n  **Gotcha:** `amount *= count1 + count2;` means `amount = amount * (count1 + count2);` — the whole right side is evaluated first.\n- Literals (slides 36–37): integer literals have no decimal point (`10`); floating literals `367000.0`, `0.000589`, or scientific `3.67e5`, `5.89e-4`. `char` in single quotes `'Z'`; `String` in double quotes `\"Welcome to Java\"`; boolean only `true` and `false` (all lowercase).\n\n## 6. Assignment compatibility and type casting (slides 34–35, 43–45)\n\nWidening order (slide 35):\n\n```\nbyte → short → int → long → float → double\n                ↑\n              char\n```\n- A value can go to any type to its **right** automatically: `double d = 5;` (this automatic conversion is called **type coercion**, slide 44).\n- Going **left** needs an explicit **type cast**: `int i = (int)5.5;`\n- `int intVariable = 2.99;` is **illegal** (type mismatch).\n- `int` and `boolean` are never interchangeable in Java — in either direction.\n- Casting floating → integer **truncates**, it does not round: `(int)2.9` is `2`; `(int)-2.7` is `-2`.\n- To keep the fraction in an integer division, cast one operand **before** dividing: `double ans = n / (double)m;` The cast does not change the variable itself.\n- **Slide 45 (instructor mini-exercise):** \"Is `int i = 5.5` correct?\" → **No.** A `double` cannot be stored in an `int`. Correct: `int i = (int)5.5;` (i becomes 5).\n\n## 7. Arithmetic: precedence, division, % (slides 38–42, 76)\n\n- Precedence high → low (slide 39): unary `+ - ++ -- !` → `* / %` → `+ -`. Full table (slide 76): dot/`[]`/method call → postfix `++ --` → unary/prefix → casts → `* / %` → `+ -` → `< > <= >=` → `== !=` → `&` → `|` → `&&` → `||` → `?:` → assignment operators.\n- Equal precedence: **associativity** decides. Binary arithmetic is left-to-right; assignment and `?:` are right-to-left.\n- `base + rate * hours` = `base + (rate * hours)`.\n- **Integer division discards the fraction (no rounding):** `15/2` = `7`; `15.0/2` = `7.5`.\n- `%` gives the remainder: `15 % 2` = `1`. `n % 2 == 0` tests for even numbers.\n- With negatives, the sign of `%` follows the left operand: `-7 / 2` = `-3`, `-7 % 2` = `-1`, `7 % -2` = `1`.\n\n```java\nSystem.out.println(17/5 + \" \" + 17%5 + \" \" + 17.0/5 + \" \" + (double)(17/5));\n// 3 2 3.4 3.0    ← the cast in the last term happens AFTER the int division\n```\n\n## 8. Increment and decrement (slides 47–49)\n\n- `n++` / `++n` add 1; `n--` / `--n` subtract 1. As standalone statements they are identical.\n- **Inside an expression**: prefix (`++n`) changes first, then uses the new value; postfix (`n++`) uses the old value, then changes.\n\n```java\nint n = 2;\nint result = 2 * (++n);   // n = 3 first, result = 6, final n = 3\nn = 2;\nresult = 2 * (n++);       // uses 2, result = 4, then n = 3\n```\nMore traps (verified):\n```java\nint y = 2;  int z = y++ + y++;   // z = 2 + 3 = 5, y = 4\nint n = 5;  int r = 3 * n++ + --n; // 3*5 + 5 = 20, n = 5\nint k = 2;  k = k++;             // k stays 2 (old value is assigned back)\n```\n\n## 9. Strings (slides 50–61, Display 1.4 and 1.5)\n\n- `String` is a **class**, not a primitive. Any quoted text is a `String` constant.\n- `+` concatenates. A string plus almost anything gives a string: `\"The answer is \" + 42` → `\"The answer is 42\"`.\n- **Left-to-right gotcha:** `1 + 2 + \"3\" + 4 + 5` → `\"3345\"` (1+2 is added as numbers, then everything becomes text). `\"Sum: \" + 2 + 3` → `\"Sum: 23\"`.\n- `char` arithmetic: `'a' + 1` is the int `98`; `\"\" + 'a' + 1` is `\"a1\"`.\n- Call a method: object, dot, name, parentheses — `greeting.length()`.\n- **Indexes start at 0.** `\"Java is fun.\"` has 12 characters, indexes 0–11; spaces and the period count (slide 61).\n\n| Method | Returns | Example (from slides) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `length()` | int | `\"Hello!\".length()` → 6 |\n| `equals(s)` | boolean, case matters | `\"Hello\".equals(\"hello\")` → false |\n| `equalsIgnoreCase(s)` | boolean, ignores case | `\"mary!\".equalsIgnoreCase(\"Mary!\")` → true |\n| `toLowerCase()` / `toUpperCase()` | new String | `\"Hi Mary!\".toUpperCase()` → `\"HI MARY!\"` |\n| `trim()` | new String without leading/trailing whitespace | `\"   Hmm   \".trim()` → `\"Hmm\"` |\n| `charAt(i)` | char | `\"Hello!\".charAt(1)` → `'e'` |\n| `substring(start)` | from start to end | `\"AbcdefG\".substring(2)` → `\"cdefG\"` |\n| `substring(start, end)` | start **included**, end **excluded** | `\"AbcdefG\".substring(2, 5)` → `\"cde\"` |\n| `indexOf(s)` | first position, or **−1** | `\"Hi Mary!\".indexOf(\"Mary\")` → 3; `indexOf(\"Sally\")` → −1 |\n| `indexOf(s, start)` | first position at or after start | `\"Mary, Mary quite contrary\".indexOf(\"Mary\", 1)` → 6; `(…, 8)` → −1 |\n| `lastIndexOf(s)` | last position, or −1 | `\"Mary, Mary, Mary quite so\".lastIndexOf(\"Mary\")` → 12 |\n| `compareTo(s)` | negative / 0 / positive (lexicographic) | `\"adventure\".compareTo(\"zoo\")` < 0; `.compareTo(\"above\")` > 0 |\n\n- `compareTo` uses Unicode order: **all uppercase letters come before all lowercase**, so `\"Zebra\".compareTo(\"apple\") < 0`. For pure alphabetical order, convert case first.\n- Strings are **immutable**: `toUpperCase()`, `trim()` etc. return a *new* string; the original is unchanged.\n- `substring(start, end)` length = `end − start`.\n\n## 10. Packages, imports, comments (slides 62–64)\n\n- A **package** is a library of classes. Use `import`: `import java.text.NumberFormat;` (one class) or `import java.text.*;` (all classes in the package).\n- **`java.lang` is imported automatically** (that is why `String`, `System`, `Math` need no import).\n- `//` line comment; `/* … */` block comment; `/** … */` is read by **javadoc** to build documentation.\n- A well-written program is **self-documenting**: good names and indentation.\n\n## 11. Flow of control: branching (slides 65–76)\n\n- Branching: `if`, `if-else`, multiway `if-else`, `switch`. Loops: `while`, `do-while`, `for`. Most are controlled by **Boolean expressions** (true/false).\n- Compound statements need braces `{ }` (slide 67). Without braces only the next single statement belongs to the `if`.\n- Multiway if-else: conditions tested top to bottom; first true branch runs; final `else` catches the rest.\n- **`switch`** (slide 69): the controlling expression is compared with each `case` label. **Without `break`, execution falls through** into the next case.\n\n```java\nint x = 7;\nswitch (x % 3) {             // 7 % 3 = 1\n    case 0: System.out.print(\"A\");\n    case 1: System.out.print(\"B\");   // starts here\n    case 2: System.out.print(\"C\"); break;   // falls through, then stops\n    default: System.out.print(\"D\");\n}\n// prints BC\n```\n- **Conditional (ternary) operator** (slide 70): `max = (n1 > n2) ? n1 : n2;` — same as the if-else. Also called \"arithmetic if\".\n- Comparison operators (slide 72): `==  !=  >  >=  <  <=`. `=` assigns, `==` compares.\n- **Pitfall — `==` with Strings (slide 73):** `==` tests whether two references point to the **same object in memory**, not whether the text is equal. Use `s1.equals(s2)` or `s1.equalsIgnoreCase(s2)`.\n\n```java\nString a = \"java\";\nString b = new String(\"java\");\nSystem.out.println(a == b);       // false (two different objects)\nSystem.out.println(a.equals(b));  // true  (same characters)\n```\n- Boolean variables (slide 74): `boolean madeIt = (time < limit) && (limit < max);`\n- Truth tables (slide 75): `&&` true only if both true; `||` false only if both false; `!` flips.\n- **Short-circuit**: `&&` stops if the left side is false; `||` stops if the left side is true. So `a > 5 && b / 0 > 1` with `a = 3` gives `false` without dividing by zero.\n\n## 12. Loops (slides 77–82)\n\n- `while (cond) body` — tests first; body may run **zero** times.\n- `do body while (cond);` — body runs **at least once**; note the **semicolon** after `while (…)`.\n- `for (init; cond; update) body`.\n- **Nested loops** (slide 80): the inner loop runs completely for each outer iteration.\n\n```java\nfor (int rowNum = 1; rowNum <= 3; rowNum++) {\n    for (int columnNum = 1; columnNum <= 2; columnNum++)\n        System.out.print(\" row \" + rowNum + \" column \" + columnNum);\n    System.out.println();\n}\n// 3 lines, each with 2 \"row r column c\" pairs\n```\n- `break` ends the innermost loop or `switch`. **Labeled break** `break outer;` ends the loop labelled `outer:` even if it is not the innermost (slide 81).\n\n```java\nouter:\nfor (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++)\n    for (int j = 1; j <= 3; j++) {\n        if (i * j == 4) break outer;\n        System.out.print(i * j + \" \");\n    }\n// prints 1 2 3 2   (stops when i=2, j=2)\n```\n- `System.exit(0);` ends the **whole program** immediately. `0` = normal end by tradition (slide 82). `break` does not end the program.\n\n## 13. Debugging (slides 83–84)\n\n- Look at the whole system; try different test cases; comment out blocks to narrow the fault; check common pitfalls; take a break. **Never make random changes hoping they fix it.**\n- **Tracing variables**: watch values change, with the IDE debugger or temporary prints like `System.out.println(\"n = \" + n);` — comment them out when done.\n\n## Errata (slides vs correct Java)\n\n| Slide | Slide says | Correct | How verified |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 67 | `if (myScore > your Score)` | `if (myScore > yourScore)` — a space inside an identifier is a compile error | javac |\n| 55 | `String name = \"mary!\";` then `greeting.equalsIgnoreCase(\"Mary!\")` returns true | Should be `name.equalsIgnoreCase(\"Mary!\")` (Pearson typo; the result `true` is right) | ran it |\n| 59 | `String name = \"Mary, Mary, Mary quite so\";` then `greeting.indexOf(\"Mary\")` returns 0 | Should be `name.indexOf(\"Mary\")` → 0; `lastIndexOf(\"Mary\")` → 12 is correct | ran it |\n| 45 | `int i = 5.5` / `int i = (int)5.5` | Both lines are missing the `;`. The point (needs a cast) is correct | javac |\n| 54–60 | Titled \"Part 1 of 8\" … \"Part 7 of 8\" | Part 8 is missing from the deck (in Savitch Display 1.4 it continues with `compareToIgnoreCase`) | deck count |\n| 20 | `javap` \"breaks down .class files into bytecode\" | Plain `javap X` only lists members; use **`javap -c X`** to see the bytecode | javap |\n| 22 | Identifier characters: letters, digits, underscore | Also `$` is legal (`int $ok = 3;` compiles) — just not recommended | javac |\n| 14 | \"JIT (Just-In-Time complier)\" | spelling: compiler | — |\n\nThe bytecode on slide 15 (`iload_1 …`) is **correct** for an instance method (slot 0 is `this`).\n\n## Likely exam questions\n\n**Tracing — \"What is printed?\"** (all verified by running)\n\n1. `int n = 5; int r = 3 * n++ + --n; System.out.println(r + \" \" + n);` → **`20 5`**\n2. `System.out.println(17/5 + \" \" + 17%5 + \" \" + 17.0/5 + \" \" + (double)(17/5));` → **`3 2 3.4 3.0`**\n3. `String s = \"Hello World\"; System.out.println(s.substring(3,7) + \"|\" + s.indexOf(\"o\") + \"|\" + s.lastIndexOf(\"o\") + \"|\" + s.charAt(6) + \"|\" + s.length());` → **`lo W|4|7|W|11`**\n4. `System.out.println(\"Sum: \" + 2 + 3); System.out.println(2 + 3 + \" is sum\");` → **`Sum: 23`** then **`5 is sum`**\n5. The `switch (7 % 3)` example above → **`BC`** (fall-through)\n6. `int count = 0; for (int i=1;i<=3;i++) for (int j=i;j<=3;j++) count++;` → **`6`** (3+2+1)\n7. `int k = 10; do { k -= 4; } while (k > 5);` → **`2`** (10→6→2)\n8. `int c = 10; c += 5; c *= 2 + 1; c %= 7;` → **`3`** (15 → 45 → 3)\n9. `int w = 0; while (w < 10) w += 3;` → **`12`**\n10. `double d = 9.99; System.out.println((int)d + \" \" + (int)-2.7);` → **`9 -2`**\n\n**Find the bug**\n\n11. `if (name == \"Ana\")` compares references → use `name.equals(\"Ana\")`.\n12. `int avg = total / count;` where a fractional average is wanted → `double avg = (double)total / count;` (cast *before* dividing; `(double)(total / count)` is still wrong).\n13. `do { … } while (x > 0)` → missing `;` after the `while`.\n14. `switch` cases without `break` print several branches → add `break`.\n15. `int i = 5.5;` → needs `(int)` cast (slide 45).\n\n**Short theory**\n\n16. Is bytecode machine language? → No; it is the instruction set of the JVM, interpreted or JIT-compiled at run time (slide 14).\n17. Why is Java portable? → Compiled once to bytecode; any machine with a JVM runs it (slide 12).\n18. Command to compile / run `FirstProgram`? → `javac FirstProgram.java` / `java FirstProgram` (no extension).\n19. Difference `while` vs `do-while`? → `do-while` body runs at least once; `while` may run zero times.\n20. What does `System.exit(0)` do that `break` does not? → Ends the whole program; `break` only leaves a loop/switch.\n\n**Write code**\n\n21. Write `static boolean isEven(int n)` → `return n % 2 == 0;`\n22. Print a multiplication table 1–5 with nested `for` loops.\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"Who created Java, when, and who owns it now?","a":"A Sun Microsystems team led by James Gosling in 1991; now owned by Oracle."},{"q":"What was Java first designed for?","a":"Programming home appliances, which use many different processors."},{"q":"What is the difference between a Java application and an applet?","a":"An application is a class with a main method run by the run-time system; an applet runs from a web browser and always uses a windowing interface."},{"q":"What is Java bytecode?","a":"The machine language of a fictitious computer, the Java Virtual Machine; it makes compiled Java portable."},{"q":"Is Java bytecode a machine language (instructor's slide 13–14)?","a":"No — it is an intermediate instruction set for the JVM, which interprets it or JIT-compiles it to the real CPU's machine code at run time."},{"q":"Describe what bytecode looks like (slide 15).","a":"Stack-based instructions (push/pop on an operand stack) plus a constant pool; e.g. iload_1, iload_2, iadd, ireturn for return a + b."},{"q":"What does a JIT compiler do?","a":"Compiles a section of bytecode at a time into machine code while the program runs."},{"q":"What is the class loader, and what is its C++ equivalent?","a":"It connects the bytecode of all classes the program needs; in C++ this is the linker."},{"q":"What is javap?","a":"The JDK's class-file disassembler; javap -c shows the bytecode of a .class file."},{"q":"How do you compile and run the class FirstProgram?","a":"javac FirstProgram.java, then java FirstProgram (class name only, no extension)."},{"q":"What file does javac FirstProgram.java produce?","a":"FirstProgram.class, containing bytecode."},{"q":"What are the rules for a Java identifier?","a":"Must not start with a digit; letters, digits, underscore (and $); any length; case-sensitive."},{"q":"Name the eight primitive types.","a":"boolean, char, byte, short, int, long, float, double."},{"q":"Is String a primitive type?","a":"No, String is a predefined class (java.lang.String)."},{"q":"Give the widening (automatic) assignment order of numeric types.","a":"byte → short → int → long → float → double, and char → int."},{"q":"Is int i = 5.5; legal (slide 45)?","a":"No — a double cannot go into an int; write int i = (int)5.5; which gives 5."},{"q":"What is (int)2.9 and (int)-2.7?","a":"2 and -2 — casting truncates toward zero, it does not round."},{"q":"What is type coercion?","a":"The automatic conversion Java does when, e.g., an int is assigned to a double (double d = 5;)."},{"q":"What are 15/2, 15.0/2 and 15%2?","a":"7, 7.5 and 1."},{"q":"If n = 2, what are 2*(++n) and 2*(n++)?","a":"6 and 4; in both cases n ends at 3."},{"q":"What does amount *= count1 + count2; mean?","a":"amount = amount * (count1 + count2);"},{"q":"What does 1 + 2 + \"3\" + 4 + 5 evaluate to?","a":"\"3345\" — left to right, numbers add until a String appears, then everything concatenates."},{"q":"What does \"AbcdefG\".substring(2, 5) return?","a":"\"cde\" — start index included, end index excluded."},{"q":"What does indexOf return when the text is not found?","a":"-1."},{"q":"What does s1.compareTo(s2) return?","a":"A negative number if s1 comes first lexicographically, 0 if equal, positive if s2 comes first."},{"q":"Why is \"Zebra\".compareTo(\"apple\") negative?","a":"In Unicode order all uppercase letters come before all lowercase letters."},{"q":"Why must you not compare Strings with ==?","a":"== checks whether both references point to the same object in memory; use equals or equalsIgnoreCase to compare contents."},{"q":"Which package is imported automatically into every Java program?","a":"java.lang."},{"q":"What kind of comment does javadoc read?","a":"Block comments that open with /** ."},{"q":"What happens in a switch case that has no break?","a":"Execution falls through into the next case's statements."},{"q":"What is the difference between while and do-while?","a":"do-while tests after the body, so the body runs at least once; while may run zero times."},{"q":"What does a labeled break (break outer;) do?","a":"Exits the loop labelled outer:, even if it is not the innermost loop."},{"q":"What does System.exit(0) do?","a":"Ends the whole program immediately; 0 conventionally means a normal ending."},{"q":"What is short-circuit evaluation?","a":"&& skips the right side when the left is false; || skips it when the left is true."},{"q":"What is tracing variables?","a":"Watching variables change while the program runs, using a debugger or temporary println statements."}],"figs":[{"n":1,"src":"slides/W1s001.jpg"},{"n":2,"src":"slides/W1s002.jpg"},{"n":3,"src":"slides/W1s003.jpg"},{"n":4,"src":"slides/W1s004.jpg"},{"n":5,"src":"slides/W1s005.jpg"},{"n":6,"src":"slides/W1s006.jpg"},{"n":7,"src":"slides/W1s007.jpg"},{"n":8,"src":"slides/W1s008.jpg"},{"n":9,"src":"slides/W1s009.jpg"},{"n":10,"src":"slides/W1s010.jpg"},{"n":11,"src":"slides/W1s011.jpg"},{"n":12,"src":"slides/W1s012.jpg"},{"n":13,"src":"slides/W1s013.jpg"},{"n":14,"src":"slides/W1s014.jpg"},{"n":15,"src":"slides/W1s015.jpg"},{"n":16,"src":"slides/W1s016.jpg"},{"n":17,"src":"slides/W1s017.jpg"},{"n":18,"src":"slides/W1s018.jpg"},{"n":19,"src":"slides/W1s019.jpg"},{"n":20,"src":"slides/W1s020.jpg"},{"n":21,"src":"slides/W1s021.jpg"},{"n":22,"src":"slides/W1s022.jpg"},{"n":23,"src":"slides/W1s023.jpg"},{"n":24,"src":"slides/W1s024.jpg"},{"n":25,"src":"slides/W1s025.jpg"},{"n":26,"src":"slides/W1s026.jpg"},{"n":27,"src":"slides/W1s027.jpg"},{"n":28,"src":"slides/W1s028.jpg"},{"n":29,"src":"slides/W1s029.jpg"},{"n":30,"src":"slides/W1s030.jpg"},{"n":31,"src":"slides/W1s031.jpg"},{"n":32,"src":"slides/W1s032.jpg"},{"n":33,"src":"slides/W1s033.jpg"},{"n":34,"src":"slides/W1s034.jpg"},{"n":35,"src":"slides/W1s035.jpg"},{"n":36,"src":"slides/W1s036.jpg"},{"n":37,"src":"slides/W1s037.jpg"},{"n":38,"src":"slides/W1s038.jpg"},{"n":39,"src":"slides/W1s039.jpg"},{"n":40,"src":"slides/W1s040.jpg"},{"n":41,"src":"slides/W1s041.jpg"},{"n":42,"src":"slides/W1s042.jpg"},{"n":43,"src":"slides/W1s043.jpg"},{"n":44,"src":"slides/W1s044.jpg"},{"n":45,"src":"slides/W1s045.jpg"},{"n":46,"src":"slides/W1s046.jpg"},{"n":47,"src":"slides/W1s047.jpg"},{"n":48,"src":"slides/W1s048.jpg"},{"n":49,"src":"slides/W1s049.jpg"},{"n":50,"src":"slides/W1s050.jpg"},{"n":51,"src":"slides/W1s051.jpg"},{"n":52,"src":"slides/W1s052.jpg"},{"n":53,"src":"slides/W1s053.jpg"},{"n":54,"src":"slides/W1s054.jpg"},{"n":55,"src":"slides/W1s055.jpg"},{"n":56,"src":"slides/W1s056.jpg"},{"n":57,"src":"slides/W1s057.jpg"},{"n":58,"src":"slides/W1s058.jpg"},{"n":59,"src":"slides/W1s059.jpg"},{"n":60,"src":"slides/W1s060.jpg"},{"n":61,"src":"slides/W1s061.jpg"},{"n":62,"src":"slides/W1s062.jpg"},{"n":63,"src":"slides/W1s063.jpg"},{"n":64,"src":"slides/W1s064.jpg"},{"n":65,"src":"slides/W1s065.jpg"},{"n":66,"src":"slides/W1s066.jpg"},{"n":67,"src":"slides/W1s067.jpg"},{"n":68,"src":"slides/W1s068.jpg"},{"n":69,"src":"slides/W1s069.jpg"},{"n":70,"src":"slides/W1s070.jpg"},{"n":71,"src":"slides/W1s071.jpg"},{"n":72,"src":"slides/W1s072.jpg"},{"n":73,"src":"slides/W1s073.jpg"},{"n":74,"src":"slides/W1s074.jpg"},{"n":75,"src":"slides/W1s075.jpg"},{"n":76,"src":"slides/W1s076.jpg"},{"n":77,"src":"slides/W1s077.jpg"},{"n":78,"src":"slides/W1s078.jpg"},{"n":79,"src":"slides/W1s079.jpg"},{"n":80,"src":"slides/W1s080.jpg"},{"n":81,"src":"slides/W1s081.jpg"},{"n":82,"src":"slides/W1s082.jpg"},{"n":83,"src":"slides/W1s083.jpg"},{"n":84,"src":"slides/W1s084.jpg"},{"n":85,"src":"slides/W1s085.jpg"}],"exercises":[{"id":"e1","lang":"java","title":"Integer division, % and casts","level":"warm","blurb":"Slides 41–45. Write class `Arith` with four static methods:","prompt":"Slides 41–45. Write class `Arith` with four static methods:\n\n- `static int quotient(int a, int b)` — integer quotient, e.g. `quotient(15, 2)` → `7`\n- `static int remainder(int a, int b)` — e.g. `remainder(15, 2)` → `1`\n- `static double exactAverage(int total, int count)` — the average **with** its fraction: `exactAverage(7, 2)` → `3.5`. Cast *before* dividing.\n- `static int truncate(double d)` — cast to int: `truncate(2.9)` → `2`, `truncate(-2.7)` → `-2`","starter":"class Arith {\n    static int quotient(int a, int b) { return 0; }\n    static int remainder(int a, int b) { return 0; }\n    static double exactAverage(int total, int count) { return 0.0; }\n    static int truncate(double d) { return 0; }\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    static void check(boolean ok, String msg) { if (!ok) throw new AssertionError(msg); }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        check(Arith.quotient(15, 2) == 7, \"quotient(15,2) should be 7\");\n        check(Arith.quotient(-7, 2) == -3, \"quotient(-7,2) should be -3 (Java truncates toward zero)\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS quotient\");\n        check(Arith.remainder(15, 2) == 1, \"remainder(15,2) should be 1\");\n        check(Arith.remainder(-7, 2) == -1, \"remainder(-7,2) should be -1 (sign follows the left operand)\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS remainder\");\n        check(Math.abs(Arith.exactAverage(7, 2) - 3.5) < 1e-9, \"exactAverage(7,2) should be 3.5, not 3.0 - cast before dividing\");\n        check(Math.abs(Arith.exactAverage(10, 4) - 2.5) < 1e-9, \"exactAverage(10,4) should be 2.5\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS exactAverage\");\n        check(Arith.truncate(2.9) == 2, \"truncate(2.9) should be 2 (no rounding)\");\n        check(Arith.truncate(-2.7) == -2, \"truncate(-2.7) should be -2\");\n        check(Arith.truncate(5.0) == 5, \"truncate(5.0) should be 5\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS truncate\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Arith {\n    static int quotient(int a, int b) { return a / b; }\n    static int remainder(int a, int b) { return a % b; }\n    static double exactAverage(int total, int count) { return (double) total / count; }\n    static int truncate(double d) { return (int) d; }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e2","lang":"java","title":"Making change","level":"warm","blurb":"Use `/` and `%` (slide 42). Write `static String coins(int cents)` in class `Change` that splits an amount (0–99 cents) into quarters (25), dimes (10), nickels ","prompt":"Use `/` and `%` (slide 42). Write `static String coins(int cents)` in class `Change` that splits an amount (0–99 cents) into quarters (25), dimes (10), nickels (5) and pennies (1), using as many of the larger coins as possible. Return the format `\"<q>Q <d>D <n>N <p>P\"`.\n\nExamples: `coins(94)` → `\"3Q 1D 1N 4P\"`, `coins(0)` → `\"0Q 0D 0N 0P\"`, `coins(30)` → `\"1Q 0D 1N 0P\"`.","starter":"class Change {\n    static String coins(int cents) {\n        return \"\";\n    }\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    static void check(boolean ok, String msg) { if (!ok) throw new AssertionError(msg); }\n    static void eq(String got, String want) { check(want.equals(got), \"expected \\\"\" + want + \"\\\" but got \\\"\" + got + \"\\\"\"); }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        eq(Change.coins(94), \"3Q 1D 1N 4P\");\n        eq(Change.coins(0), \"0Q 0D 0N 0P\");\n        eq(Change.coins(30), \"1Q 0D 1N 0P\");\n        eq(Change.coins(99), \"3Q 2D 0N 4P\");\n        eq(Change.coins(4), \"0Q 0D 0N 4P\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS coins\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Change {\n    static String coins(int cents) {\n        int q = cents / 25;\n        cents = cents % 25;\n        int d = cents / 10;\n        cents %= 10;\n        int n = cents / 5;\n        int p = cents % 5;\n        return q + \"Q \" + d + \"D \" + n + \"N \" + p + \"P\";\n    }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e3","lang":"java","title":"String toolkit","level":"core","blurb":"Slides 53–61. Write class `Str` with:","prompt":"Slides 53–61. Write class `Str` with:\n\n- `static String initials(String fullName)` — the name has exactly two words separated by one space, possibly with spaces around it. Return the two first letters in upper case: `initials(\"  ada lovelace \")` → `\"AL\"`. Use `trim`, `indexOf`, `charAt`, `toUpperCase`.\n- `static int countChar(String s, char c)` — how many times `c` occurs: `countChar(\"banana\", 'a')` → `3`.\n- `static String middle(String s)` — for odd length, the middle character as a String; for even length, the middle two: `middle(\"abcde\")` → `\"c\"`, `middle(\"abcd\")` → `\"bc\"`. Use `substring`.","starter":"class Str {\n    static String initials(String fullName) { return \"\"; }\n    static int countChar(String s, char c) { return 0; }\n    static String middle(String s) { return \"\"; }\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    static void check(boolean ok, String msg) { if (!ok) throw new AssertionError(msg); }\n    static void eq(String got, String want, String what) { check(want.equals(got), what + \": expected \\\"\" + want + \"\\\" but got \\\"\" + got + \"\\\"\"); }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        eq(Str.initials(\"  ada lovelace \"), \"AL\", \"initials(\\\"  ada lovelace \\\")\");\n        eq(Str.initials(\"Alan Turing\"), \"AT\", \"initials(\\\"Alan Turing\\\")\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS initials\");\n        check(Str.countChar(\"banana\", 'a') == 3, \"countChar(banana,'a') should be 3\");\n        check(Str.countChar(\"banana\", 'z') == 0, \"countChar(banana,'z') should be 0\");\n        check(Str.countChar(\"\", 'a') == 0, \"countChar(\\\"\\\",'a') should be 0\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS countChar\");\n        eq(Str.middle(\"abcde\"), \"c\", \"middle(abcde)\");\n        eq(Str.middle(\"abcd\"), \"bc\", \"middle(abcd)\");\n        eq(Str.middle(\"x\"), \"x\", \"middle(x)\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS middle\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Str {\n    static String initials(String fullName) {\n        String s = fullName.trim();\n        int space = s.indexOf(\" \");\n        String two = \"\" + s.charAt(0) + s.charAt(space + 1);\n        return two.toUpperCase();\n    }\n    static int countChar(String s, char c) {\n        int count = 0;\n        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++)\n            if (s.charAt(i) == c) count++;\n        return count;\n    }\n    static String middle(String s) {\n        int mid = s.length() / 2;\n        if (s.length() % 2 == 1) return s.substring(mid, mid + 1);\n        return s.substring(mid - 1, mid + 1);\n    }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e4","lang":"java","title":"Letter grades: multiway if and switch","level":"core","blurb":"Slides 68–70. Class `Grades`:","prompt":"Slides 68–70. Class `Grades`:\n\n- `static char letter(int score)` — `'A'` for 90–100, `'B'` 80–89, `'C'` 70–79, `'D'` 60–69, otherwise `'F'`. Use a multiway if-else.\n- `static double points(char letter)` — use a **switch**: A → 4.0, B → 3.0, C → 2.0, D → 1.0, anything else → 0.0. Accept lower-case letters too (two case labels can share one branch: `case 'A': case 'a':`).\n- `static String passFail(int score)` — `\"pass\"` if score ≥ 60 else `\"fail\"`, written with the **conditional operator** `?:`.","starter":"class Grades {\n    static char letter(int score) { return ' '; }\n    static double points(char letter) { return -1; }\n    static String passFail(int score) { return \"\"; }\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    static void check(boolean ok, String msg) { if (!ok) throw new AssertionError(msg); }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[] s = {100, 90, 89, 80, 79, 70, 69, 60, 59, 0};\n        char[] w = {'A', 'A', 'B', 'B', 'C', 'C', 'D', 'D', 'F', 'F'};\n        for (int i = 0; i < s.length; i++)\n            check(Grades.letter(s[i]) == w[i], \"letter(\" + s[i] + \") should be \" + w[i] + \" but was \" + Grades.letter(s[i]));\n        System.out.println(\"PASS letter\");\n        check(Grades.points('A') == 4.0 && Grades.points('a') == 4.0, \"points A/a should be 4.0\");\n        check(Grades.points('B') == 3.0 && Grades.points('c') == 2.0 && Grades.points('D') == 1.0, \"points B=3, c=2, D=1\");\n        check(Grades.points('F') == 0.0 && Grades.points('?') == 0.0, \"points F and unknown should be 0.0\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS points\");\n        check(Grades.passFail(60).equals(\"pass\") && Grades.passFail(59).equals(\"fail\"), \"passFail boundary at 60\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS passFail\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Grades {\n    static char letter(int score) {\n        if (score >= 90) return 'A';\n        else if (score >= 80) return 'B';\n        else if (score >= 70) return 'C';\n        else if (score >= 60) return 'D';\n        else return 'F';\n    }\n    static double points(char letter) {\n        double p;\n        switch (letter) {\n            case 'A': case 'a': p = 4.0; break;\n            case 'B': case 'b': p = 3.0; break;\n            case 'C': case 'c': p = 2.0; break;\n            case 'D': case 'd': p = 1.0; break;\n            default: p = 0.0; break;\n        }\n        return p;\n    }\n    static String passFail(int score) {\n        return (score >= 60) ? \"pass\" : \"fail\";\n    }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e5","lang":"java","title":"Loop workouts","level":"core","blurb":"Slides 77–80. Class `Loops`:","prompt":"Slides 77–80. Class `Loops`:\n\n- `static int digitSum(int n)` — sum of the decimal digits of a non-negative `n`: `digitSum(4071)` → `12`. Use `% 10` and `/ 10` in a `while` loop.\n- `static int reverse(int n)` — digits reversed: `reverse(1230)` → `321`.\n- `static boolean isPrime(int n)` — `false` for n < 2; otherwise test divisors with a `for` loop.\n- `static int countPrimes(int max)` — how many primes are ≤ max: `countPrimes(10)` → `4`.","starter":"class Loops {\n    static int digitSum(int n) { return 0; }\n    static int reverse(int n) { return 0; }\n    static boolean isPrime(int n) { return false; }\n    static int countPrimes(int max) { return 0; }\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    static void check(boolean ok, String msg) { if (!ok) throw new AssertionError(msg); }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        check(Loops.digitSum(4071) == 12, \"digitSum(4071) should be 12\");\n        check(Loops.digitSum(0) == 0, \"digitSum(0) should be 0\");\n        check(Loops.digitSum(9) == 9, \"digitSum(9) should be 9\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS digitSum\");\n        check(Loops.reverse(1230) == 321, \"reverse(1230) should be 321\");\n        check(Loops.reverse(7) == 7, \"reverse(7) should be 7\");\n        check(Loops.reverse(12345) == 54321, \"reverse(12345) should be 54321\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS reverse\");\n        check(!Loops.isPrime(0) && !Loops.isPrime(1), \"0 and 1 are not prime\");\n        check(Loops.isPrime(2) && Loops.isPrime(3) && Loops.isPrime(97), \"2, 3, 97 are prime\");\n        check(!Loops.isPrime(9) && !Loops.isPrime(91), \"9 and 91 are not prime\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS isPrime\");\n        check(Loops.countPrimes(10) == 4, \"countPrimes(10) should be 4\");\n        check(Loops.countPrimes(100) == 25, \"countPrimes(100) should be 25\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS countPrimes\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Loops {\n    static int digitSum(int n) {\n        int sum = 0;\n        while (n > 0) {\n            sum += n % 10;\n            n /= 10;\n        }\n        return sum;\n    }\n    static int reverse(int n) {\n        int r = 0;\n        while (n > 0) {\n            r = r * 10 + n % 10;\n            n /= 10;\n        }\n        return r;\n    }\n    static boolean isPrime(int n) {\n        if (n < 2) return false;\n        for (int d = 2; d * d <= n; d++)\n            if (n % d == 0) return false;\n        return true;\n    }\n    static int countPrimes(int max) {\n        int count = 0;\n        for (int i = 2; i <= max; i++)\n            if (isPrime(i)) count++;\n        return count;\n    }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e6","lang":"java","title":"Reading input with Scanner (from a String)","level":"core","blurb":"Console input in Savitch ch. 2 uses `Scanner`. Here the \"input\" is a String so the test is automatic: `new Scanner(line)` reads from the String exactly like `ne","prompt":"Console input in Savitch ch. 2 uses `Scanner`. Here the \"input\" is a String so the test is automatic: `new Scanner(line)` reads from the String exactly like `new Scanner(System.in)` reads the keyboard. Remember `import java.util.Scanner;` goes at the top of your code — or write the full name `java.util.Scanner`.\n\nClass `Parse`:\n- `static int sumInts(String line)` — sum of all whitespace-separated integers: `sumInts(\"3 48 -1\")` → `50`. Use `hasNextInt()` / `nextInt()`.\n- `static String timeWorked(String line)` — the line holds hours and minutes (`\"3 48\"`); return `\"3 hours and 48 minutes\"`.\n- `static int countWords(String line)` — number of tokens: `countWords(\"  to be  or not \")` → `4`. Use `hasNext()` / `next()`.","starter":"class Parse {\n    static int sumInts(String line) { return 0; }\n    static String timeWorked(String line) { return \"\"; }\n    static int countWords(String line) { return 0; }\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    static void check(boolean ok, String msg) { if (!ok) throw new AssertionError(msg); }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        check(Parse.sumInts(\"3 48 -1\") == 50, \"sumInts(\\\"3 48 -1\\\") should be 50\");\n        check(Parse.sumInts(\"\") == 0, \"sumInts(\\\"\\\") should be 0\");\n        check(Parse.sumInts(\"10\\n20\\t30\") == 60, \"sumInts handles newlines/tabs: 60\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS sumInts\");\n        check(Parse.timeWorked(\"3 48\").equals(\"3 hours and 48 minutes\"), \"timeWorked(\\\"3 48\\\") -> \\\"3 hours and 48 minutes\\\", got \\\"\" + Parse.timeWorked(\"3 48\") + \"\\\"\");\n        check(Parse.timeWorked(\" 0   5 \").equals(\"0 hours and 5 minutes\"), \"timeWorked(\\\" 0   5 \\\")\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS timeWorked\");\n        check(Parse.countWords(\"  to be  or not \") == 4, \"countWords should be 4\");\n        check(Parse.countWords(\"   \") == 0, \"countWords of blanks should be 0\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS countWords\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Parse {\n    static int sumInts(String line) {\n        java.util.Scanner in = new java.util.Scanner(line);\n        int sum = 0;\n        while (in.hasNextInt()) sum += in.nextInt();\n        return sum;\n    }\n    static String timeWorked(String line) {\n        java.util.Scanner in = new java.util.Scanner(line);\n        int hours = in.nextInt();\n        int minutes = in.nextInt();\n        return hours + \" hours and \" + minutes + \" minutes\";\n    }\n    static int countWords(String line) {\n        java.util.Scanner in = new java.util.Scanner(line);\n        int count = 0;\n        while (in.hasNext()) {\n            in.next();\n            count++;\n        }\n        return count;\n    }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e7","lang":"java","title":"Nested loops and labeled break","level":"hard","blurb":"Slides 80–81. Class `Nested`:","prompt":"Slides 80–81. Class `Nested`:\n\n- `static String triangle(int n)` — a right triangle of `*`, one row per line, each line ending in `\"\\n\"`: `triangle(3)` → `\"*\\n**\\n***\\n\"`. `triangle(0)` → `\"\"`.\n- `static String firstPair(int target, int max)` — search `i` from 1 to `max` (outer loop) and `j` from 1 to `max` (inner loop) for the **first** pair with `i * j == target`. Return `\"i,j\"`, or `\"none\"`. Use a **labeled break** to leave both loops as soon as you find it. `firstPair(12, 5)` → `\"3,4\"`; `firstPair(7, 5)` → `\"none\"`.\n- `static int countPairs(int max)` — number of pairs `(i, j)` with `1 ≤ i ≤ j ≤ max`: `countPairs(3)` → `6`.","starter":"class Nested {\n    static String triangle(int n) { return null; }\n    static String firstPair(int target, int max) { return \"\"; }\n    static int countPairs(int max) { return -1; }\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    static void check(boolean ok, String msg) { if (!ok) throw new AssertionError(msg); }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        check(\"*\\n**\\n***\\n\".equals(Nested.triangle(3)), \"triangle(3) wrong: \" + Nested.triangle(3));\n        check(\"\".equals(Nested.triangle(0)), \"triangle(0) should be empty\");\n        check(\"*\\n\".equals(Nested.triangle(1)), \"triangle(1) should be \\\"*\\\\n\\\"\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS triangle\");\n        check(\"3,4\".equals(Nested.firstPair(12, 5)), \"firstPair(12,5) should be 3,4 (2,6 is out of range) but was \" + Nested.firstPair(12, 5));\n        check(\"1,4\".equals(Nested.firstPair(4, 4)), \"firstPair(4,4) should be 1,4\");\n        check(\"none\".equals(Nested.firstPair(7, 5)), \"firstPair(7,5) should be none\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS firstPair\");\n        check(Nested.countPairs(3) == 6, \"countPairs(3) should be 6\");\n        check(Nested.countPairs(1) == 1, \"countPairs(1) should be 1\");\n        check(Nested.countPairs(10) == 55, \"countPairs(10) should be 55\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS countPairs\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Nested {\n    static String triangle(int n) {\n        String out = \"\";\n        for (int row = 1; row <= n; row++) {\n            for (int k = 1; k <= row; k++) out += \"*\";\n            out += \"\\n\";\n        }\n        return out;\n    }\n    static String firstPair(int target, int max) {\n        String found = \"none\";\n        outer:\n        for (int i = 1; i <= max; i++) {\n            for (int j = 1; j <= max; j++) {\n                if (i * j == target) {\n                    found = i + \",\" + j;\n                    break outer;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        return found;\n    }\n    static int countPairs(int max) {\n        int count = 0;\n        for (int i = 1; i <= max; i++)\n            for (int j = i; j <= max; j++)\n                count++;\n        return count;\n    }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e8","lang":"java","title":"Ordering words with compareTo","level":"hard","blurb":"Slide 60 and the `==` pitfall (slide 73). Class `Order`:","prompt":"Slide 60 and the `==` pitfall (slide 73). Class `Order`:\n\n- `static String first(String a, String b, String c)` — the word that comes first **alphabetically, ignoring case** (use `compareToIgnoreCase` or compare lower-cased copies). Return it unchanged. `first(\"pear\", \"Apple\", \"banana\")` → `\"Apple\"`. On a tie keep the earlier argument.\n- `static boolean sameText(String a, String b)` — true when the two strings have the same characters (case matters). **Must not use `==`**: the test passes strings built at run time.\n- `static boolean isSorted(String a, String b, String c)` — true if `a ≤ b ≤ c` in plain `compareTo` order (case-sensitive): `isSorted(\"Zebra\", \"apple\", \"kiwi\")` → `true` because uppercase comes first.","starter":"class Order {\n    static String first(String a, String b, String c) { return c; }\n    static boolean sameText(String a, String b) { return a == b; }\n    static boolean isSorted(String a, String b, String c) { return false; }\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    static void check(boolean ok, String msg) { if (!ok) throw new AssertionError(msg); }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        check(\"Apple\".equals(Order.first(\"pear\", \"Apple\", \"banana\")), \"first(pear, Apple, banana) should be Apple\");\n        check(\"ant\".equals(Order.first(\"bee\", \"cat\", \"ant\")), \"first(bee, cat, ant) should be ant\");\n        check(\"Kiwi\".equals(Order.first(\"Kiwi\", \"kiwi\", \"lime\")), \"tie: keep the earlier argument (Kiwi)\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS first\");\n        String built = new String(\"java\");\n        check(Order.sameText(\"java\", built), \"sameText must compare contents, not references\");\n        check(!Order.sameText(\"Java\", \"java\"), \"sameText is case-sensitive\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS sameText\");\n        check(Order.isSorted(\"Zebra\", \"apple\", \"kiwi\"), \"Zebra < apple < kiwi in Unicode order\");\n        check(!Order.isSorted(\"apple\", \"Zebra\", \"kiwi\"), \"apple > Zebra in Unicode order\");\n        check(Order.isSorted(\"a\", \"a\", \"b\"), \"equal neighbours count as sorted\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS isSorted\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Order {\n    static String first(String a, String b, String c) {\n        String best = a;\n        if (b.compareToIgnoreCase(best) < 0) best = b;\n        if (c.compareToIgnoreCase(best) < 0) best = c;\n        return best;\n    }\n    static boolean sameText(String a, String b) {\n        return a.equals(b);\n    }\n    static boolean isSorted(String a, String b, String c) {\n        return a.compareTo(b) <= 0 && b.compareTo(c) <= 0;\n    }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."}]},{"id":"02","title":"W2 · Defining Classes I (ch. 4)","slides":"Week 2 · 82 slides","md":"# 02 — Week 2 · Defining Classes I (Savitch ch. 4, 82 slides)\n\n_Ingested 15.11.2026 from lectures/Week2_SavichJava_Chap4_KO.pptx (PDF copy in lectures/). Pearson originals by Rose Williams and Kenrick Mock, \"Updated by Dr. Kazeem Oladejo\" — note the title slide still says **October, 2025** (last year's deck reused). All 82 slides read; image-only slides (4–5, 36–40, 59, 72, 79) and the code slides (12, 14, 43, 69) were rendered and read visually. Every code example below was compiled and run on JDK 26._\n\n**Instructor's own additions (not in the Pearson deck):**\n\n| Slide | What he added |\n|---|---|\n| 4–6 | Blueprint photos (\"Class as a Specification\") vs real buildings (\"Object as an Implementation\") |\n| 10 | Note: \"The new operator creates the object reference/location in memory\" |\n| **12, 14, 23–25** | **Running `Building` / `Main` demo** — `getMaxFloor()` (returns a value), `printStatus()` (void) |\n| 28 | \"Java has no global variables\" |\n| 33 | \"We will discuss call-by-reference later\" |\n| **47–53** | **\"Some Questions\" — 7 in-class questions with his answers** (class, object, class vs object, instance variable, local variable, `new`, `this`). Very likely exam material — learn his wording (section 11) |\n| 61 | Note on overloading with different return types |\n| **69** | **DEMO `BigBuilding.java` + `Main.java`**: instance variables, constructors, `this`, getters, setters, `new` |\n| 73–79 | Encapsulation: definition, car analogy, how-it-works (3 steps), benefits, the **4 access modifiers** and the access table (from web sources) |\n| **82** | **\"You will practice Classes in the IUS lab next week\"** → expect a lab task on writing a class |\n\n---\n\n## 1. Class and object (slides 2–7)\n\n- Classes are the most important OOP feature. **Every program is a class**; all programmer-defined types are classes.\n- A class is a **type**. A variable of a class type is an **object** or **instance** of the class. \"Hotel Hills is of type Building\" = \"an object of the class Building\" = \"an instance of the class Building\".\n- The class decides **what data** an object holds and **what actions** (methods) it can do.\n- **Class = specification (blueprint)**; **object = implementation (the real building)** — slides 4–5.\n- A **primitive** value is a single piece of data. A **class type** value can have several pieces of data **and** methods (slide 7).\n- All objects of a class have the **same methods** and the **same data model** (names, types, number of fields); each object holds **its own values**.\n\n## 2. Contents of a class definition (slides 8–13)\n\n- A class definition lists **instance variables** (also called **fields**) and **methods**. Together they are the **members**.\n- Members can appear in any order inside the class.\n- Create an object with `new` (slides 9–10):\n\n```java\nClassName objectName;              // declares a variable (no object yet)\nobjectName = new ClassName();      // new creates the object and returns its reference\nClassName objectName2 = new ClassName();   // both steps in one line\n```\n- Access a public field / call a method with the dot: `objectName.instanceVar1`, `objectName.myMethod()`.\n- File rule (slide 13): a `public` class `MyClass` goes in `MyClass.java`. For now keep all classes of a program in the same folder.\n\nInstructor demo (slide 12, verified):\n```java\n// Building.java\npublic class Building {\n    private static final int MAX_FLOORS = 10;\n    public int getMaxFloor() {\n        return MAX_FLOORS;\n    }\n    public void printStatus() {          // added on slides 23–25\n        System.out.println(\"Building under construction.\");\n    }\n}\n// Main.java\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Building hotel = new Building();\n        int noOfFloors = hotel.getMaxFloor();\n        System.out.println(\"Building Max floors: \" + noOfFloors);  // Building Max floors: 10\n        hotel.printStatus();                                      // Building under construction.\n    }\n}\n```\nNo `import` is needed for `Building` because both classes are in the same (default) package.\n\n## 3. Methods: heading, body, return (slides 15–26)\n\n- A method = **heading** + **body** `{ … }`.\n- Two kinds: methods that **return a value** (heading names the type: `public typeReturned methodName(paramList)`) and **void** methods (`public void methodName(paramList)`).\n- `main` is a **void** method: `public static void main(String[] args)`.\n- A value-returning method **must** contain `return Expression;` — the expression's type must match the heading. `return` ends the method.\n- A void method needs no `return`; it may use `return;` (no expression) to stop early.\n- Calling a void method is a statement: `hotel.printStatus();`\n- **Any method can be used as a void method** (slide 26): `obj.valueMethod();` runs it and throws the result away.\n\n## 4. Local variables, blocks, no globals (slides 27–29)\n\n- A variable declared inside a method is **local** to it. **Parameters are local variables too.**\n- Two methods may each have a local called `x` — they are completely separate variables.\n- **Java has no global variables** (slide 28).\n- A **block** = statements in `{ }`. A variable declared in a block exists only in that block.\n- **Local variables are NOT automatically initialised**; using one before assigning is a compile error (slide 68). Instance variables ARE initialised automatically (section 9).\n\n## 5. Parameters and call-by-value (slides 30–41)\n\n- **Formal parameters** = the variables in the heading: `public double myMethod(int p1, int p2, double p3)`.\n- **Arguments** (actual parameters) = the values passed in the call: `myMethod(a, b, c)`.\n- Number and order of arguments must match; types must be compatible. Java widens automatically along `byte → short → int → long → float → double` (`char → int`). So an `int` argument may go into a `double` parameter.\n- **Call-by-value**: the *value* of each argument is copied into the parameter. Changing the parameter inside the method **does not change the caller's variable**.\n\n```java\nstatic void bump(int v) { v = v + 10; }\nstatic int bump2(int v) { v = v + 10; return v; }\n// in main:\nint x = 5;\nbump(x);           System.out.println(x);   // 5  — only the copy changed\nx = bump2(x);      System.out.println(x);   // 15 — the returned value was assigned\n```\n- Display 4.6 (slides 36–40, `Bill` / `BillingDialog`): `computeFee(int hoursWorked, int minutesWorked)` reuses the parameter `minutesWorked` as a local variable (`minutesWorked = hoursWorked*60 + minutesWorked;`). The field `minutes` that was passed in is **not** changed. Fee = (total minutes / 15, integer division) × RATE. For input `3 48`: 228/15 = 15 quarter hours × $150.0 = **$2250.0**.\n- \"Parameter\" = the variable, \"argument\" = the value passed in. People mix them up; read from context (slide 41).\n\n## 6. The `this` keyword (slides 42–44, 53, 66)\n\n- Inside a method every instance variable has an invisible `this.` in front: `myInstanceVariable` ≡ `this.myInstanceVariable`.\n- `this` = a reference to **the calling object** (the object whose method or constructor is running). It is a hidden parameter, filled in automatically.\n- **`this` is required when a parameter or local variable has the same name as a field** — otherwise the name means the local.\n\n```java\nclass Person {\n    private String name;\n    public Person(String name) { name = name; }        // BUG: assigns the parameter to itself\n    // fix: public Person(String name) { this.name = name; }\n    public String getName() { return name; }\n}\nnew Person(\"Ana\").getName();   // returns null with the bug\n```\n- Constructors also have a `this` parameter: it refers to the new object being built (slide 66).\n\n## 7. equals and toString (slides 45–46)\n\n- Methods returning `boolean` can control `if` and `while` directly: `if (acct.isEmpty())`.\n- Almost every class should define:\n  - `public boolean equals(ClassName other)` — \"are these two objects equal in value?\"\n  - `public String toString()` — a String showing the object's data. Called automatically by `println(obj)` and `\"\" + obj`.\n- `==` on objects compares **references** (same object?), not contents. Slide 46 says \"you cannot use == to compare objects\" — it compiles, but it answers a different question.\n\n```java\nPair a = new Pair(1, 2), b = new Pair(1, 2);\nSystem.out.println(a == b);       // false — two separate objects\nSystem.out.println(a.equals(b));  // true  — equal fields (our equals)\nSystem.out.println(a);            // (1, 2) — toString used automatically\n```\n(Savitch's ch. 4 version `equals(ClassName)` overloads `Object.equals`; the proper override `equals(Object)` comes in ch. 7.)\n\n## 8. public / private, accessors, mutators, pre/postconditions (slides 54–57)\n\n- `public`: usable anywhere. `private`: usable **only inside the class**.\n- **Good practice: make ALL instance variables private.** Most methods public. Private methods = helpers for other methods.\n- **Accessor** (getter, `getX`): returns a field's value; does not change it.\n- **Mutator** (setter, `setX`): changes a field in a controlled way — it **validates** the incoming data.\n- A mutator can **return boolean** instead of ending the program on bad data, letting the caller decide (slide 56):\n\n```java\nclass Temp {\n    private double c;\n    public boolean setC(double v) {\n        if (v < -273.15) return false;   // reject impossible value, field unchanged\n        c = v;\n        return true;\n    }\n    public double getC() { return c; }\n}\n// t.setC(-300) → false, getC() still 0.0; t.setC(25) → true, getC() → 25.0\n```\n- **Precondition**: what must be true when the method is called. **Postcondition**: what will be true afterwards, if the precondition held. Write both in the method comment (slide 57).\n\n## 9. Overloading (slides 58–62)\n\n- **Overloading** = two or more methods in the same class with the **same name** but **different signatures**.\n- **Signature = method name + parameter list (number, types, order).** The **return type is NOT part of the signature** (slide 61).\n  - `int f(int a)` and `double f(int b)` → **compile error** (\"already defined\").\n  - Different return types are fine *only if* the parameter lists also differ.\n- If no exact match, Java tries **automatic type conversion**. This can surprise you, and if two methods match equally well the call is **ambiguous → compile error** (slide 60).\n\n```java\nstatic String f(int a)    { return \"int\"; }\nstatic String f(double a) { return \"double\"; }\nf(3) → \"int\";  f(3.0) → \"double\";  f('c') → \"int\"   // char widens to int first\n\nstatic void h(int a, double b) {}\nstatic void h(double a, int b) {}\nh(1, 2);   // error: reference to h is ambiguous\n```\n- **Java does not allow operator overloading** (no custom `+` like in C++) (slide 62). Use methods instead.\n- Slide 59 (StringTokenizer, `java.util`): `new StringTokenizer(theString)` splits on whitespace; `new StringTokenizer(theString, delimiters)` uses your delimiter characters; `hasMoreTokens()` and `nextToken()` walk the tokens. (Only part 1 of 2 is in the deck, and it sits oddly in the overloading section — the constructors are an example of overloading.)\n\n## 10. Constructors (slides 63–69)\n\n- A **constructor** initialises the instance variables of a new object.\n  - **Same name as the class.**\n  - **No return type, not even `void`.** (If you write `void`, it becomes an ordinary method that happens to share the class name.)\n  - Usually **overloaded**.\n- Called **only** with `new`: `ClassName obj = new ClassName(args);` You **cannot** call it like a normal method (`obj.ClassName(…)` or `ClassName(0);` inside a method → compile error). To change an existing object, use **mutators**.\n- Calling `new` again creates a **new** object; the variable now refers to it, and the old object is discarded if nothing else refers to it (slide 64).\n- A constructor may call other methods (e.g. setters) because the object already exists when its body runs (slide 65). **One constructor can call another with `this(…)`**.\n\n```java\nclass Box {\n    int w, h;\n    Box()            { this(3); }          // → Box(3)\n    Box(int w)       { this(w, w * 2); }   // → Box(3, 6)\n    Box(int w, int h){ this.w = w; this.h = h; }\n}\nnew Box();   // w = 3, h = 6\n```\n  In the textbook's Java (and up to JDK 24) `this(…)` must be the **first** statement. JDK 25+ relaxes this (\"flexible constructor bodies\"), but answer exam questions by the textbook rule.\n- **No-argument constructor** (slide 67):\n  - If you write **no** constructor, Java supplies a **default** no-arg constructor (does nothing, but lets you write `new X()`).\n  - If you write **even one** constructor, Java does **not** supply it. So `new Car()` fails if only `Car(String)` exists → \"constructor Car in class Car cannot be applied to given types\".\n  - Good practice: always write your own no-arg constructor too.\n- **Default initial values of instance variables** (slide 68): `boolean` → `false`; other primitives → zero of their type (`0`, `0.0`, `'\\u0000'`); class types (e.g. `String`) → `null`. Better: initialise explicitly in the constructor. **Local variables get no default.**\n\n## 11. Instructor's in-class questions (slides 47–53) — learn his answers\n\n| # | His question | His answer |\n|---|---|---|\n| 47 | What is a class? | A defined type that includes both data and method (code that performs actions) specifications |\n| 48 | What is an object? | A variable of a class type; also called an instance or implementation of the class |\n| 49 | Difference between class and object? | The class is only a specification; objects include specific data for each instantiated class |\n| 50 | What is an instance variable? | A defined data item of a class; available to all methods in the class |\n| 51 | What is a local variable? | A defined data item of a method; only available within the method |\n| 52 | What is the `new` operator? | It is used to create the object and associate it with its variable name |\n| 53 | What is the `this` keyword? | Within a method, an explicit name for the current (calling) object |\n\n## 12. Testing methods (slides 70–71)\n\n- A **driver program** is a program whose only purpose is to test a method (e.g. `Main.java`).\n- **Bottom-up testing**: test the methods a method calls first, then the method itself.\n- **Stub**: a simplified version of a not-yet-finished method that returns a fixed value so other methods can be tested.\n- **Fundamental rule**: test each method in a program where **every other method has already been fully tested and debugged**.\n\n## 13. Encapsulation and information hiding (slides 72–81)\n\n- **Encapsulation**: data and the methods working on it are combined in one unit (the object), and the implementation details are hidden. Core OOP principle; foundation for data security (slide 73).\n- Benefits (slides 73, 76): data protection/integrity, security, reusability, maintainability, easier debugging, scalability.\n- Car analogy (slide 74): you use the steering wheel and pedals without knowing how the engine works.\n- **How it works — 3 steps (slide 75):** (1) declare data members **private**; (2) provide **public getters and setters**; (3) **validate data** inside the setters.\n- Display 4.10 (slide 72): hidden in the capsule = private instance variables, private constants, private methods, method bodies. Interface = comments, headings of public methods, public constants. **\"A class definition should have no public instance variables.\"**\n- **Information hiding**: separating *how to use* a class from *how it is implemented*. **Abstraction** = discarding details to avoid overload (slide 80).\n- **API** (application programming interface): description of how to use a class. **ADT** (abstract data type): a type written with good information-hiding (slide 81).\n\n**Access modifiers (slides 77–79):**\n\n| Modifier | Same class | Same package (subclass or not) | Subclass in other package | Other package, non-subclass |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `public` | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes |\n| `protected` | Yes | Yes | Yes | No |\n| default (no keyword) | Yes | Yes | No | No |\n| `private` | Yes | No | No | No |\n\n- `protected` applies to fields, methods, constructors — **not to top-level classes**.\n- Default (\"package-private\") is what you get when you write no modifier.\n- `private` applies to fields, methods, nested classes.\n\n## Errata (slides vs correct Java)\n\n| Slide | Slide says | Correct | How verified |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **40** (Display 4.6 sample dialogue) | Input `3 48` → \"Time worked: **2** hours and 48 minutes … Amount due: $2250.0\" | The program prints **3** hours and 48 minutes. $2250.0 is right (228 min / 15 = 15 × 150); 2 h 48 min would cost $1650.0 | ran the slide's Bill code with input \"3 48\" |\n| 16 | \"the name of the calling object (aka instance Variables)\" | The calling object is an *object*, not an instance variable. Instance variables are the object's fields | definition (slide 8) |\n| 29 | \"Once a variable has been declared within a block, its name cannot be used for anything else within the same method\" | Too strong. You cannot redeclare a name while the first is **still in scope** (`int x; { int x; }` → \"already defined\"). After a block ends, the name can be reused (two sequential `for (int i…)` loops, or `{int y;} {String y;}`, compile fine) | javac |\n| 46 | \"You cannot use == to compare objects\" | `==` compiles on objects but compares **references** (same object?), not contents. Use `equals` for contents | ran it |\n| 35 | Parameter \"is initialized to this value in the class' object\" | The parameter is a local variable of the method call, not stored in the object | — |\n| 23 | `\"Buidling under construction.\"` | Typo; slide 25 corrects it to \"Building\" | — |\n| 1 | \"October, 2025\" | Deck date not updated for Fall 2026 | — |\n\n## Likely exam questions\n\n**Tracing — \"What is printed?\"** (all verified by running)\n\n1. `int x = 5; bump(x); System.out.println(x);` with `static void bump(int v){ v = v + 10; }` → **`5`** (call-by-value).\n2. `Counter c1 = new Counter(); Counter c2 = new Counter(5); c1.inc(); c2.inc(); c2.inc(); System.out.println(c1.get() + \" \" + c2.get());` (Counter: private int n; no-arg ctor sets 0; `inc()` does n++) → **`1 7`**\n3. Continue: `Counter c3 = c2; c3.inc(); System.out.println(c2.get() + \" \" + (c3 == c2));` → **`8 true`** (c3 and c2 are the same object).\n4. `class Pt { int x; String name; boolean ok; }` … `System.out.println(p.x + \" \" + p.name + \" \" + p.ok);` → **`0 null false`**\n5. The `Person` with `name = name;` → `getName()` returns **`null`**.\n6. `new Box()` from section 10 → `w = 3, h = 6`.\n7. `f(3)`, `f(3.0)`, `f('c')` with `f(int)` / `f(double)` → **`int double int`**.\n\n**Find the bug / will it compile?**\n\n8. `int f(int a)` and `double f(int b)` in one class → **no**: return type is not part of the signature.\n9. Class has only `public Car(String m)`; main does `new Car()` → **no**: no default constructor once you write one.\n10. `public void Car(String m) { … }` meant as a constructor → compiles, but it is a **method**, not a constructor; `new Car(\"x\")` then fails.\n11. `void reset() { K(0); }` to \"re-run\" the constructor → **no**: constructors are called only with `new`; write a mutator.\n12. `a.bal = 5;` from another class when `bal` is `private` → **no**: \"bal has private access\".\n13. `int k; System.out.println(k);` inside a method → **no**: local variable not initialised.\n14. `h(1, 2)` with `h(int,double)` and `h(double,int)` → **no**: ambiguous.\n\n**Short theory**\n\n15. Signature = ? → method name + parameter types/number/order (not return type).\n16. Accessor vs mutator? → getter reads a field; setter changes it after validating.\n17. Three steps of encapsulation (slide 75)? → private fields, public getters/setters, validation in setters.\n18. When must you write `this.`? → when a parameter/local has the same name as an instance variable.\n19. What does Java give you if you write no constructor? → a default no-argument constructor.\n20. Default values of instance variables? → false / 0 / null. Local variables? → none.\n21. Driver program, stub, bottom-up testing — define each.\n22. His 7 questions (section 11).\n\n**Write a class (lab-style, slide 82)**: `BankAccount` with private `owner` and `balance`, no-arg and 2-arg constructors (use `this`), getters, a `deposit` that rejects non-positive amounts and returns boolean, `equals`, `toString`. → see `exercises-java/w02.md`.\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"What is a class (instructor's answer, slide 47)?","a":"A defined type that includes both data and method (code that performs actions) specifications."},{"q":"What is an object (slide 48)?","a":"A variable of a class type, also called an instance or implementation of the class."},{"q":"What is the difference between a class and an object (slide 49)?","a":"The class is only a specification (blueprint); objects hold specific data for each instance."},{"q":"What is an instance variable (slide 50)?","a":"A data item defined in a class (a field); available to all methods of the class, and each object has its own copy."},{"q":"What is a local variable (slide 51)?","a":"A data item declared in a method (including parameters); only available within that method."},{"q":"What does the new operator do (slide 52)?","a":"Creates the object in memory and returns its reference, which is associated with the variable name."},{"q":"What is the this keyword (slide 53)?","a":"Inside a method or constructor, an explicit reference to the current (calling) object."},{"q":"When is this required?","a":"When a parameter or local variable has the same name as an instance variable; otherwise the name refers to the local."},{"q":"What are the members of a class?","a":"Its instance variables (fields) and its methods."},{"q":"What two parts make up a method definition?","a":"The heading and the body."},{"q":"What must a non-void method contain?","a":"At least one return Expression; whose type matches the declared return type."},{"q":"Can a void method use return?","a":"Yes, return; with no expression ends it early."},{"q":"Does Java have global variables?","a":"No."},{"q":"What is call-by-value?","a":"The argument's value is copied into the parameter, so changing the parameter does not change the caller's variable."},{"q":"Parameter vs argument?","a":"The parameter is the variable in the method heading; the argument is the value passed in the call."},{"q":"What is a method signature?","a":"The method name plus its parameter list (number, types, order) — not the return type."},{"q":"Can two methods differ only in return type?","a":"No — that is a compile error because the signatures are the same."},{"q":"What is overloading?","a":"Two or more methods in the same class with the same name but different signatures."},{"q":"What happens when an overloaded call matches two methods equally well after type conversion?","a":"Compile error: the call is ambiguous."},{"q":"Does Java support operator overloading?","a":"No."},{"q":"What are the rules for a constructor?","a":"Same name as the class, no return type (not even void), usually overloaded, invoked only with new."},{"q":"When does Java provide a default no-argument constructor?","a":"Only if the class defines no constructors at all."},{"q":"How does one constructor call another?","a":"With this(arguments); as the first statement (textbook rule)."},{"q":"Default values of instance variables?","a":"boolean false, other primitives zero, class types null."},{"q":"Are local variables initialised automatically?","a":"No — using one before assignment is a compile error."},{"q":"Accessor vs mutator?","a":"An accessor (getX) returns a field's value; a mutator (setX) changes it in a controlled, validated way."},{"q":"Why might a mutator return boolean?","a":"To report whether the new value was accepted, letting the caller handle bad data instead of ending the program."},{"q":"Precondition vs postcondition?","a":"Precondition = what must be true when the method is called; postcondition = what is true after it runs, if the precondition held."},{"q":"What do equals and toString do?","a":"equals tests whether two objects are equal in value; toString returns a String representing the object's data."},{"q":"What does == compare for objects?","a":"Whether the two references point to the same object, not their contents."},{"q":"What is a driver program?","a":"A program whose only purpose is to test a method or class."},{"q":"What is a stub?","a":"A simplified version of an unfinished method that returns a value so other methods can be tested."},{"q":"What are the three steps of encapsulation (slide 75)?","a":"Make fields private, provide public getters/setters, validate data in the setters."},{"q":"Which access modifier allows access from the same package and from subclasses in other packages?","a":"protected."},{"q":"What is an API and an ADT?","a":"API = description of how to use a class; ADT = a data type written using good information-hiding techniques."}],"figs":[{"n":1,"src":"slides/W2s001.jpg"},{"n":2,"src":"slides/W2s002.jpg"},{"n":3,"src":"slides/W2s003.jpg"},{"n":4,"src":"slides/W2s004.jpg"},{"n":5,"src":"slides/W2s005.jpg"},{"n":6,"src":"slides/W2s006.jpg"},{"n":7,"src":"slides/W2s007.jpg"},{"n":8,"src":"slides/W2s008.jpg"},{"n":9,"src":"slides/W2s009.jpg"},{"n":10,"src":"slides/W2s010.jpg"},{"n":11,"src":"slides/W2s011.jpg"},{"n":12,"src":"slides/W2s012.jpg"},{"n":13,"src":"slides/W2s013.jpg"},{"n":14,"src":"slides/W2s014.jpg"},{"n":15,"src":"slides/W2s015.jpg"},{"n":16,"src":"slides/W2s016.jpg"},{"n":17,"src":"slides/W2s017.jpg"},{"n":18,"src":"slides/W2s018.jpg"},{"n":19,"src":"slides/W2s019.jpg"},{"n":20,"src":"slides/W2s020.jpg"},{"n":21,"src":"slides/W2s021.jpg"},{"n":22,"src":"slides/W2s022.jpg"},{"n":23,"src":"slides/W2s023.jpg"},{"n":24,"src":"slides/W2s024.jpg"},{"n":25,"src":"slides/W2s025.jpg"},{"n":26,"src":"slides/W2s026.jpg"},{"n":27,"src":"slides/W2s027.jpg"},{"n":28,"src":"slides/W2s028.jpg"},{"n":29,"src":"slides/W2s029.jpg"},{"n":30,"src":"slides/W2s030.jpg"},{"n":31,"src":"slides/W2s031.jpg"},{"n":32,"src":"slides/W2s032.jpg"},{"n":33,"src":"slides/W2s033.jpg"},{"n":34,"src":"slides/W2s034.jpg"},{"n":35,"src":"slides/W2s035.jpg"},{"n":36,"src":"slides/W2s036.jpg"},{"n":37,"src":"slides/W2s037.jpg"},{"n":38,"src":"slides/W2s038.jpg"},{"n":39,"src":"slides/W2s039.jpg"},{"n":40,"src":"slides/W2s040.jpg"},{"n":41,"src":"slides/W2s041.jpg"},{"n":42,"src":"slides/W2s042.jpg"},{"n":43,"src":"slides/W2s043.jpg"},{"n":44,"src":"slides/W2s044.jpg"},{"n":45,"src":"slides/W2s045.jpg"},{"n":46,"src":"slides/W2s046.jpg"},{"n":47,"src":"slides/W2s047.jpg"},{"n":48,"src":"slides/W2s048.jpg"},{"n":49,"src":"slides/W2s049.jpg"},{"n":50,"src":"slides/W2s050.jpg"},{"n":51,"src":"slides/W2s051.jpg"},{"n":52,"src":"slides/W2s052.jpg"},{"n":53,"src":"slides/W2s053.jpg"},{"n":54,"src":"slides/W2s054.jpg"},{"n":55,"src":"slides/W2s055.jpg"},{"n":56,"src":"slides/W2s056.jpg"},{"n":57,"src":"slides/W2s057.jpg"},{"n":58,"src":"slides/W2s058.jpg"},{"n":59,"src":"slides/W2s059.jpg"},{"n":60,"src":"slides/W2s060.jpg"},{"n":61,"src":"slides/W2s061.jpg"},{"n":62,"src":"slides/W2s062.jpg"},{"n":63,"src":"slides/W2s063.jpg"},{"n":64,"src":"slides/W2s064.jpg"},{"n":65,"src":"slides/W2s065.jpg"},{"n":66,"src":"slides/W2s066.jpg"},{"n":67,"src":"slides/W2s067.jpg"},{"n":68,"src":"slides/W2s068.jpg"},{"n":69,"src":"slides/W2s069.jpg"},{"n":70,"src":"slides/W2s070.jpg"},{"n":71,"src":"slides/W2s071.jpg"},{"n":72,"src":"slides/W2s072.jpg"},{"n":73,"src":"slides/W2s073.jpg"},{"n":74,"src":"slides/W2s074.jpg"},{"n":75,"src":"slides/W2s075.jpg"},{"n":76,"src":"slides/W2s076.jpg"},{"n":77,"src":"slides/W2s077.jpg"},{"n":78,"src":"slides/W2s078.jpg"},{"n":79,"src":"slides/W2s079.jpg"},{"n":80,"src":"slides/W2s080.jpg"},{"n":81,"src":"slides/W2s081.jpg"},{"n":82,"src":"slides/W2s082.jpg"}],"exercises":[{"id":"e1","lang":"java","title":"BigBuilding — the instructor's demo","level":"warm","blurb":"Slide 69 demo objectives: instance variables, constructors, `this`, getters, setters. Write class `BigBuilding`:","prompt":"Slide 69 demo objectives: instance variables, constructors, `this`, getters, setters. Write class `BigBuilding`:\n\n- private constant `MAX_FLOORS = 10` (`private static final int`), private fields `String name` and `int floors`\n- `BigBuilding()` — no-arg constructor: name `\"Unnamed\"`, 1 floor\n- `BigBuilding(String name, int floors)` — use `this.name = name;` etc. If `floors` is outside 1…MAX_FLOORS, store 1.\n- `getName()`, `getFloors()`, `getMaxFloor()` (returns MAX_FLOORS)\n- `boolean setFloors(int floors)` — accept only 1…MAX_FLOORS and return true; otherwise leave the field unchanged and return false\n- `void setName(String name)`","starter":"class BigBuilding {\n    private static final int MAX_FLOORS = 10;\n    private String name;\n    private int floors;\n    public BigBuilding() { }\n    public BigBuilding(String name, int floors) { }\n    public String getName() { return name; }\n    public int getFloors() { return floors; }\n    public int getMaxFloor() { return 0; }\n    public boolean setFloors(int floors) { return false; }\n    public void setName(String name) { }\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    static void check(boolean ok, String msg) { if (!ok) throw new AssertionError(msg); }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        BigBuilding a = new BigBuilding();\n        check(\"Unnamed\".equals(a.getName()) && a.getFloors() == 1, \"no-arg constructor: Unnamed, 1 floor (got \" + a.getName() + \", \" + a.getFloors() + \")\");\n        check(a.getMaxFloor() == 10, \"getMaxFloor() should be 10\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS no-arg constructor\");\n        BigBuilding h = new BigBuilding(\"Hotel Hills\", 7);\n        check(\"Hotel Hills\".equals(h.getName()) && h.getFloors() == 7, \"2-arg constructor should store Hotel Hills, 7 - did you use this.?\");\n        BigBuilding bad = new BigBuilding(\"Tower\", 99);\n        check(bad.getFloors() == 1, \"constructor with 99 floors should store 1\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS 2-arg constructor\");\n        check(h.setFloors(10) && h.getFloors() == 10, \"setFloors(10) should succeed\");\n        check(!h.setFloors(11) && h.getFloors() == 10, \"setFloors(11) should be rejected and leave 10\");\n        check(!h.setFloors(0) && h.getFloors() == 10, \"setFloors(0) should be rejected\");\n        h.setName(\"Grand\");\n        check(\"Grand\".equals(h.getName()), \"setName should change the name\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS setters\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class BigBuilding {\n    private static final int MAX_FLOORS = 10;\n    private String name;\n    private int floors;\n    public BigBuilding() {\n        this(\"Unnamed\", 1);\n    }\n    public BigBuilding(String name, int floors) {\n        this.name = name;\n        if (floors >= 1 && floors <= MAX_FLOORS) this.floors = floors;\n        else this.floors = 1;\n    }\n    public String getName() { return name; }\n    public int getFloors() { return floors; }\n    public int getMaxFloor() { return MAX_FLOORS; }\n    public boolean setFloors(int floors) {\n        if (floors < 1 || floors > MAX_FLOORS) return false;\n        this.floors = floors;\n        return true;\n    }\n    public void setName(String name) { this.name = name; }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e2","lang":"java","title":"Fix the `this` bug","level":"warm","blurb":"Slides 42–44. The starter compiles but every getter returns a default value. Find and fix the bug (keep the parameter names exactly as they are — the fix is the","prompt":"Slides 42–44. The starter compiles but every getter returns a default value. Find and fix the bug (keep the parameter names exactly as they are — the fix is the `this` keyword). Also add a method `String toString()` returning `\"<name> (<id>)\"`, e.g. `\"Ana (17)\"`.","starter":"class Student {\n    private String name;\n    private int id;\n    public Student(String name, int id) {\n        name = name;\n        id = id;\n    }\n    public String getName() { return name; }\n    public int getId() { return id; }\n    public void setName(String name) { name = name; }\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    static void check(boolean ok, String msg) { if (!ok) throw new AssertionError(msg); }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Student s = new Student(\"Ana\", 17);\n        check(\"Ana\".equals(s.getName()), \"getName() should be Ana, got \" + s.getName());\n        check(s.getId() == 17, \"getId() should be 17, got \" + s.getId());\n        System.out.println(\"PASS constructor\");\n        s.setName(\"Bea\");\n        check(\"Bea\".equals(s.getName()), \"setName(Bea) did not change the field\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS setName\");\n        check(\"Bea (17)\".equals(s.toString()), \"toString should be \\\"Bea (17)\\\", got \\\"\" + s + \"\\\"\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS toString\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Student {\n    private String name;\n    private int id;\n    public Student(String name, int id) {\n        this.name = name;\n        this.id = id;\n    }\n    public String getName() { return name; }\n    public int getId() { return id; }\n    public void setName(String name) { this.name = name; }\n    public String toString() { return name + \" (\" + id + \")\"; }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e3","lang":"java","title":"Bill — parameters are local variables","level":"core","blurb":"Slides 36–40 (Display 4.6), without the keyboard. Class `Bill`:","prompt":"Slides 36–40 (Display 4.6), without the keyboard. Class `Bill`:\n\n- `public static final double RATE = 150.00;` (dollars per **full** quarter hour)\n- private `int hours, minutes;` and `double fee;`\n- `Bill(int hours, int minutes)` stores the time worked\n- `double computeFee(int hoursWorked, int minutesWorked)` — reuse `minutesWorked` as a local variable: `minutesWorked = hoursWorked * 60 + minutesWorked;` then quarter hours = `minutesWorked / 15` (integer division — a partial quarter is free), return quarter hours × RATE\n- `void updateFee()` — `fee = computeFee(hours, minutes);`\n- `double getFee()`, `int getMinutes()`\n- `String timeWorked()` — `\"3 hours and 48 minutes\"` (the slide's sample dialogue misprints this as \"2 hours\")\n\nThe test checks that calling `computeFee(hours, minutes)` does **not** change the field `minutes` (call-by-value).","starter":"class Bill {\n    public static final double RATE = 150.00;\n    private int hours;\n    private int minutes;\n    private double fee;\n    public Bill(int hours, int minutes) { }\n    public double computeFee(int hoursWorked, int minutesWorked) { return 0; }\n    public void updateFee() { }\n    public double getFee() { return fee; }\n    public int getMinutes() { return minutes; }\n    public String timeWorked() { return \"\"; }\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    static void check(boolean ok, String msg) { if (!ok) throw new AssertionError(msg); }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Bill b = new Bill(3, 48);\n        check(b.computeFee(3, 48) == 2250.0, \"computeFee(3,48): 228 min -> 15 quarters -> 2250.0, got \" + b.computeFee(3, 48));\n        check(b.computeFee(0, 14) == 0.0, \"computeFee(0,14): less than a quarter hour is free\");\n        check(b.computeFee(1, 0) == 600.0, \"computeFee(1,0) should be 600.0\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS computeFee\");\n        b.updateFee();\n        check(b.getFee() == 2250.0, \"after updateFee, fee should be 2250.0\");\n        check(b.getMinutes() == 48, \"field minutes must still be 48 (call-by-value), got \" + b.getMinutes());\n        System.out.println(\"PASS updateFee\");\n        check(\"3 hours and 48 minutes\".equals(b.timeWorked()), \"timeWorked() wrong: \" + b.timeWorked());\n        System.out.println(\"PASS timeWorked\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Bill {\n    public static final double RATE = 150.00;\n    private int hours;\n    private int minutes;\n    private double fee;\n    public Bill(int hours, int minutes) {\n        this.hours = hours;\n        this.minutes = minutes;\n    }\n    public double computeFee(int hoursWorked, int minutesWorked) {\n        minutesWorked = hoursWorked * 60 + minutesWorked;\n        int quarterHours = minutesWorked / 15;\n        return quarterHours * RATE;\n    }\n    public void updateFee() { fee = computeFee(hours, minutes); }\n    public double getFee() { return fee; }\n    public int getMinutes() { return minutes; }\n    public String timeWorked() { return hours + \" hours and \" + minutes + \" minutes\"; }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e4","lang":"java","title":"Overloading","level":"core","blurb":"Slides 58–62. Class `Geometry` with **overloaded** static methods named `area` and `describe`:","prompt":"Slides 58–62. Class `Geometry` with **overloaded** static methods named `area` and `describe`:\n\n- `static double area(double radius)` — circle, `Math.PI * r * r`\n- `static double area(double width, double height)` — rectangle\n- `static int area(int side)` — square, returns an int\n- `static String describe(int x)` → `\"int \" + x`; `static String describe(double x)` → `\"double \" + x`; `static String describe(String x)` → `\"String \" + x`\n\nThen think: which `describe` runs for `describe('A')`? (char widens to int → `\"int 65\"`.) And for `describe(5L)`? (long widens to double → `\"double 5.0\"`.) The test checks both.","starter":"class Geometry {\n    static double area(double radius) { return 0; }\n    static double area(double width, double height) { return 0; }\n    static int area(int side) { return 0; }\n    static String describe(double x) { return \"\"; }\n    static String describe(String x) { return \"\"; }\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    static void check(boolean ok, String msg) { if (!ok) throw new AssertionError(msg); }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        check(Math.abs(Geometry.area(1.0) - Math.PI) < 1e-9, \"area(1.0) should be PI (circle)\");\n        check(Geometry.area(2.0, 3.5) == 7.0, \"area(2.0, 3.5) should be 7.0 (rectangle)\");\n        check(Geometry.area(4) == 16, \"area(4) should be 16 (int square)\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS area\");\n        check(\"int 7\".equals(Geometry.describe(7)), \"describe(7) should pick the int version, got \" + Geometry.describe(7));\n        check(\"double 2.5\".equals(Geometry.describe(2.5)), \"describe(2.5) should be \\\"double 2.5\\\"\");\n        check(\"String hi\".equals(Geometry.describe(\"hi\")), \"describe(\\\"hi\\\") should be \\\"String hi\\\"\");\n        check(\"int 65\".equals(Geometry.describe('A')), \"describe('A'): char widens to int -> \\\"int 65\\\"\");\n        check(\"double 5.0\".equals(Geometry.describe(5L)), \"describe(5L): long widens to double -> \\\"double 5.0\\\"\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS describe\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Geometry {\n    static double area(double radius) { return Math.PI * radius * radius; }\n    static double area(double width, double height) { return width * height; }\n    static int area(int side) { return side * side; }\n    static String describe(int x) { return \"int \" + x; }\n    static String describe(double x) { return \"double \" + x; }\n    static String describe(String x) { return \"String \" + x; }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e5","lang":"java","title":"Time of day — equals, toString, validating mutators","level":"core","blurb":"Slides 45–46, 54–57. Class `Time` (24-hour clock):","prompt":"Slides 45–46, 54–57. Class `Time` (24-hour clock):\n\n- private `int hour` (0–23), `int minute` (0–59)\n- `Time()` → 00:00; `Time(int hour, int minute)` → if either value is invalid, use 00:00\n- `boolean set(int hour, int minute)` — validate both; on bad input change nothing and return false\n- `boolean equals(Time other)` — same hour and minute (return false for `null`)\n- `String toString()` — always two digits each: `\"09:05\"`, `\"23:59\"`\n- `boolean isBefore(Time other)`\n- `void addMinutes(int m)` — m ≥ 0, wraps past midnight: 23:50 + 15 → 00:05","starter":"class Time {\n    private int hour;\n    private int minute;\n    public Time() { }\n    public Time(int hour, int minute) { }\n    public boolean set(int hour, int minute) { return false; }\n    public boolean equals(Time other) { return this == other; }\n    public String toString() { return \"\"; }\n    public boolean isBefore(Time other) { return false; }\n    public void addMinutes(int m) { }\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    static void check(boolean ok, String msg) { if (!ok) throw new AssertionError(msg); }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        check(\"00:00\".equals(new Time().toString()), \"new Time() should print 00:00, got \" + new Time());\n        check(\"09:05\".equals(new Time(9, 5).toString()), \"Time(9,5) should print 09:05, got \" + new Time(9, 5));\n        check(\"00:00\".equals(new Time(24, 0).toString()), \"Time(24,0) is invalid -> 00:00\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS constructors + toString\");\n        Time t = new Time(10, 30);\n        check(t.set(23, 59) && \"23:59\".equals(t.toString()), \"set(23,59) should succeed\");\n        check(!t.set(12, 60) && \"23:59\".equals(t.toString()), \"set(12,60) should fail and change nothing\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS set\");\n        Time a = new Time(8, 15), b = new Time(8, 15);\n        check(a != b, \"sanity: two different objects\");\n        check(a.equals(b), \"equals should compare hour and minute, not references\");\n        check(!a.equals(new Time(8, 16)) && !a.equals(null), \"equals: different time or null -> false\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS equals\");\n        check(new Time(7, 59).isBefore(new Time(8, 0)) && !new Time(8, 0).isBefore(new Time(8, 0)), \"isBefore wrong\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS isBefore\");\n        Time late = new Time(23, 50);\n        late.addMinutes(15);\n        check(\"00:05\".equals(late.toString()), \"23:50 + 15 min should be 00:05, got \" + late);\n        Time x = new Time(1, 0);\n        x.addMinutes(1500);\n        check(\"02:00\".equals(x.toString()), \"01:00 + 1500 min should be 02:00, got \" + x);\n        System.out.println(\"PASS addMinutes\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Time {\n    private int hour;\n    private int minute;\n    public Time() { this(0, 0); }\n    public Time(int hour, int minute) {\n        if (!set(hour, minute)) set(0, 0);\n    }\n    public boolean set(int hour, int minute) {\n        if (hour < 0 || hour > 23 || minute < 0 || minute > 59) return false;\n        this.hour = hour;\n        this.minute = minute;\n        return true;\n    }\n    public boolean equals(Time other) {\n        return other != null && hour == other.hour && minute == other.minute;\n    }\n    public String toString() {\n        return (hour < 10 ? \"0\" : \"\") + hour + \":\" + (minute < 10 ? \"0\" : \"\") + minute;\n    }\n    public boolean isBefore(Time other) {\n        return hour * 60 + minute < other.hour * 60 + other.minute;\n    }\n    public void addMinutes(int m) {\n        int total = (hour * 60 + minute + m) % (24 * 60);\n        hour = total / 60;\n        minute = total % 60;\n    }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e6","lang":"java","title":"BankAccount — the lab-style class","level":"hard","blurb":"Slides 54–56, 63–68, 75. An encapsulated class `BankAccount`:","prompt":"Slides 54–56, 63–68, 75. An encapsulated class `BankAccount`:\n\n- private `String owner`, `double balance`\n- `BankAccount()` → owner `\"unknown\"`, balance 0.0; `BankAccount(String owner, double balance)` → a negative starting balance becomes 0.0. Make the no-arg constructor call the other one with `this(...)`.\n- getters `getOwner()`, `getBalance()`\n- `boolean deposit(double amount)` — reject amount ≤ 0\n- `boolean withdraw(double amount)` — reject amount ≤ 0 or amount > balance\n- `boolean transferTo(BankAccount other, double amount)` — withdraw here, deposit there; all-or-nothing; reject `other == null` or `other == this`\n- `boolean equals(BankAccount other)` — same owner text and same balance\n- `String toString()` → `\"Ana: 150.0\"`","starter":"class BankAccount {\n    private String owner;\n    private double balance;\n    public BankAccount() { }\n    public BankAccount(String owner, double balance) { }\n    public String getOwner() { return owner; }\n    public double getBalance() { return balance; }\n    public boolean deposit(double amount) { return false; }\n    public boolean withdraw(double amount) { return false; }\n    public boolean transferTo(BankAccount other, double amount) { return false; }\n    public boolean equals(BankAccount other) { return false; }\n    public String toString() { return \"\"; }\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    static void check(boolean ok, String msg) { if (!ok) throw new AssertionError(msg); }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        BankAccount u = new BankAccount();\n        check(\"unknown\".equals(u.getOwner()) && u.getBalance() == 0.0, \"no-arg: unknown, 0.0\");\n        check(new BankAccount(\"Neg\", -5).getBalance() == 0.0, \"negative opening balance -> 0.0\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS constructors\");\n        BankAccount a = new BankAccount(\"Ana\", 100);\n        check(a.deposit(50) && a.getBalance() == 150.0, \"deposit(50) -> 150.0\");\n        check(!a.deposit(0) && !a.deposit(-10) && a.getBalance() == 150.0, \"deposit <= 0 rejected\");\n        check(!a.withdraw(200) && a.getBalance() == 150.0, \"withdraw more than balance rejected\");\n        check(a.withdraw(20) && a.getBalance() == 130.0, \"withdraw(20) -> 130.0\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS deposit/withdraw\");\n        BankAccount b = new BankAccount(\"Bo\", 10);\n        check(a.transferTo(b, 30) && a.getBalance() == 100.0 && b.getBalance() == 40.0, \"transfer 30: Ana 100, Bo 40\");\n        check(!a.transferTo(b, 1000) && a.getBalance() == 100.0 && b.getBalance() == 40.0, \"failed transfer must change nothing\");\n        check(!a.transferTo(a, 10) && !a.transferTo(null, 10), \"transfer to self or null rejected\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS transferTo\");\n        check(new BankAccount(\"Cy\", 5).equals(new BankAccount(new String(\"Cy\"), 5)), \"equals compares owner text and balance\");\n        check(!new BankAccount(\"Cy\", 5).equals(new BankAccount(\"Cy\", 6)), \"different balance -> not equal\");\n        check(\"Ana: 100.0\".equals(a.toString()), \"toString should be \\\"Ana: 100.0\\\", got \\\"\" + a + \"\\\"\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS equals/toString\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class BankAccount {\n    private String owner;\n    private double balance;\n    public BankAccount() { this(\"unknown\", 0.0); }\n    public BankAccount(String owner, double balance) {\n        this.owner = owner;\n        this.balance = (balance < 0) ? 0.0 : balance;\n    }\n    public String getOwner() { return owner; }\n    public double getBalance() { return balance; }\n    public boolean deposit(double amount) {\n        if (amount <= 0) return false;\n        balance += amount;\n        return true;\n    }\n    public boolean withdraw(double amount) {\n        if (amount <= 0 || amount > balance) return false;\n        balance -= amount;\n        return true;\n    }\n    public boolean transferTo(BankAccount other, double amount) {\n        if (other == null || other == this) return false;\n        if (!withdraw(amount)) return false;\n        other.deposit(amount);\n        return true;\n    }\n    public boolean equals(BankAccount other) {\n        return other != null && owner.equals(other.owner) && balance == other.balance;\n    }\n    public String toString() { return owner + \": \" + balance; }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e7","lang":"java","title":"Temperature — overloaded constructors chained with this(...)","level":"hard","blurb":"Slides 63–67 (a classic Savitch ch. 4 programming project). Class `Temperature` stores a `double degrees` and a `char scale` (`'C'` or `'F'`).","prompt":"Slides 63–67 (a classic Savitch ch. 4 programming project). Class `Temperature` stores a `double degrees` and a `char scale` (`'C'` or `'F'`).\n\n- Four constructors: `()` → 0.0 C; `(double degrees)` → that value in C; `(char scale)` → 0.0 in that scale; `(double degrees, char scale)`. The first three must call the fourth with `this(...)`. An invalid scale (anything but C/c/F/f) becomes `'C'`; store the scale in upper case.\n- `double getC()` — Celsius value: C = 5 × (F − 32) / 9. Round to one decimal: `Math.round(x * 10) / 10.0`\n- `double getF()` — Fahrenheit value: F = 9 × C / 5 + 32, rounded to one decimal\n- `boolean equals(Temperature other)` — same temperature after converting both to Celsius (compare the rounded values)\n- `boolean isWarmerThan(Temperature other)`\n- `String toString()` → `\"25.0C\"` / `\"98.6F\"` (degrees as stored + scale)","starter":"class Temperature {\n    private double degrees;\n    private char scale;\n    public Temperature() { }\n    public Temperature(double degrees) { }\n    public Temperature(char scale) { }\n    public Temperature(double degrees, char scale) { }\n    public double getC() { return 0; }\n    public double getF() { return 0; }\n    public boolean equals(Temperature other) { return false; }\n    public boolean isWarmerThan(Temperature other) { return false; }\n    public String toString() { return \"\"; }\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    static void check(boolean ok, String msg) { if (!ok) throw new AssertionError(msg); }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        check(\"0.0C\".equals(new Temperature().toString()), \"() -> 0.0C, got \" + new Temperature());\n        check(\"21.5C\".equals(new Temperature(21.5).toString()), \"(21.5) -> 21.5C\");\n        check(\"0.0F\".equals(new Temperature('f').toString()), \"('f') -> 0.0F (upper-case scale)\");\n        check(\"50.0C\".equals(new Temperature(50, 'K').toString()), \"invalid scale K -> C\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS constructors\");\n        check(new Temperature(100, 'C').getF() == 212.0, \"100C = 212.0F\");\n        check(new Temperature(98.6, 'F').getC() == 37.0, \"98.6F = 37.0C\");\n        check(new Temperature(-40, 'F').getC() == -40.0, \"-40F = -40.0C\");\n        check(new Temperature(37, 'C').getC() == 37.0 && new Temperature(212, 'F').getF() == 212.0, \"same-scale getters\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS conversions\");\n        check(new Temperature(100, 'C').equals(new Temperature(212, 'F')), \"100C equals 212F\");\n        check(!new Temperature(0, 'C').equals(new Temperature(0, 'F')), \"0C is not 0F\");\n        check(new Temperature(30, 'C').isWarmerThan(new Temperature(80, 'F')), \"30C (86F) is warmer than 80F\");\n        check(!new Temperature(10, 'C').isWarmerThan(new Temperature(50, 'F')), \"10C is not warmer than 50F (equal)\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS equals/isWarmerThan\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Temperature {\n    private double degrees;\n    private char scale;\n    public Temperature() { this(0.0, 'C'); }\n    public Temperature(double degrees) { this(degrees, 'C'); }\n    public Temperature(char scale) { this(0.0, scale); }\n    public Temperature(double degrees, char scale) {\n        this.degrees = degrees;\n        if (scale == 'F' || scale == 'f') this.scale = 'F';\n        else this.scale = 'C';\n    }\n    private static double round1(double x) { return Math.round(x * 10) / 10.0; }\n    public double getC() {\n        if (scale == 'C') return round1(degrees);\n        return round1(5 * (degrees - 32) / 9);\n    }\n    public double getF() {\n        if (scale == 'F') return round1(degrees);\n        return round1(9 * degrees / 5 + 32);\n    }\n    public boolean equals(Temperature other) {\n        return other != null && getC() == other.getC();\n    }\n    public boolean isWarmerThan(Temperature other) {\n        return getC() > other.getC();\n    }\n    public String toString() { return degrees + \"\" + scale; }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."}]},{"id":"03","title":"W3 · Defining Classes II (ch. 5)","slides":"Week 3 · 87 slides","md":"# 03 — Week 3 · Defining Classes II: static, wrappers, references, packages (Savitch ch. 5, 87 slides)\n\n_Ingested 15.11.2026 from lectures/Week3_SavichJava_chap5_KO.pptx (PDF copy in lectures/). Pearson slides by Rose Williams & Kenrick Mock (Absolute Java 6e, ch. 5), with Dr. Oladejo's additions: a recap of println/print/printf (slides 3–11), four in-class questions (Q1–Q4, slides 20–22 and 57–58), the System.out questions (slide 5), three DEMO slides (PrintDemo, StaticDemo, WrapperDemo), a binary parseInt example (slide 39) and a privacy-leak \"Why?\" (slide 75). All code below was compiled and run on JDK 26._\n\n> **Instructor-specific items (flagged).** Dr. Oladejo's own slides are question/answer slides. Expect these on quizzes and exams almost word for word:\n> - Slide 5: *class vs object*; *is System.out a class or an object?* (an object: a static field of `System`, an instance of `PrintStream`); *why is println a method?* (it is an overloaded method of `System.out`).\n> - Slides 20–22: **Q1** what is a static method? **Q2** can a static method use `this`? (No.) **Q3** can a static method directly call a non-static method without naming an object? (No.)\n> - Slides 57–58: **Q4** (MCQ) what is stored in a class-type variable? **D. A memory address that identifies where the object is stored.**\n> - Slide 60 NOTE: Java is *always* call-by-value; for class parameters the value copied is the address.\n> - Slide 75: why is `private` not enough for a class-type instance variable? Because handing out the object's address lets outside code change it (privacy leak).\n> - DEMO files mentioned (not in the folder): PrintDemo.java, StaticDemo.java, WrapperDemo.java.\n\n## 1. Recap: System.out, print, println, printf (slides 3–11)\n\n- `System` is a class in `java.lang` (imported automatically). `out` is a **static field** of `System`. Its type is `PrintStream`. `println` is a method of that object (slides 4–6).\n- **Invoking a method** = \"sending a message\": object, dot, method name, parentheses with zero or more arguments (slide 7).\n- `print` does not end the line. `println` does (slide 8).\n- `printf(format, values...)`: first argument is the format string; it does **not** add a newline (slide 9).\n\n| Specifier | Meaning | Example → output |\n|---|---|---|\n| `%6.2f` | float/double, field width 6, 2 decimals, right-justified | `printf(\"%6.2f\", 19.8)` → `\" 19.80\"` |\n| `%8.3f` | width 8, 3 decimals | `Math.PI` → `\"   3.142\"` |\n| `%d` / `%-6d` | integer / left-justified in width 6 | `42` → `\"42    \"` |\n| `%5s` | string, width 5 | `\"ab\"` → `\"   ab\"` |\n| `%n` | platform newline | |\n\nSlide 10's example prints `$ 19.80 each` (one space of padding because \"19.80\" has 5 characters and the width is 6). Verified.\n\n## 2. Static methods (slides 12–22)\n\n- A **static method** belongs to the class, not to an object. Call it with the class name: `Math.random()`, `Temperature.toCelsius(212)`.\n- Header: `public static returnType name(params)`.\n- **Rule (slide 14):** a static method has no `this`. So inside it you **cannot** use an instance variable or call a non-static method of the same class without naming an object. It **can** call other static methods and use static variables.\n- `main` is static. You can put a `main` in any class, e.g. as test code for that class (slides 15–19, `Temperature`).\n\n```java\npublic class Temperature {\n    private double degrees;                         // instance variable (Celsius)\n    public Temperature(double d) { degrees = d; }\n    public static double toCelsius(double degreesF) {   // static: no object needed\n        return 5 * (degreesF - 32) / 9;\n    }\n    public String toString() { return degrees + \" C\"; }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        double c = toCelsius(212);                  // same as Temperature.toCelsius(212)\n        Temperature t = new Temperature(c);\n        System.out.println(\"Equivalent Celsius temperature is \" + t.toString());\n        // System.out.println(toString());          // COMPILE ERROR: non-static method from static context\n    }\n}\n```\nOutput: `Equivalent Celsius temperature is 100.0 C`.\n\nNote `5 * (degreesF - 32) / 9`: the multiplication happens first, so no integer-division trap here (degreesF is a double anyway).\n\n## 3. Static variables and constants (slides 23–25)\n\n- One copy **per class**, shared by all objects. Instance variables: one copy **per object**.\n- Static methods can read and write static variables.\n- Default values if not initialised: `boolean` → `false`, other primitives → 0, class types → `null`. Initialise explicitly anyway.\n- Make static variables `private`, unless they are **constants**: `public static final int BIRTH_YEAR = 1954;` Use them as `MyClass.BIRTH_YEAR`.\n\n```java\nclass Counter {\n    static int count = 0;      // shared\n    int id;                    // per object\n    Counter() { count++; id = count; }\n}\n// Counter a = new Counter(), b = new Counter(), c = new Counter();\n// System.out.println(a.id + \" \" + c.id + \" \" + Counter.count);   →  1 3 3\n```\n\n## 4. The Math class (slides 26–33)\n\nAll members are static; `Math` is in `java.lang`, so no import. Constants `Math.PI`, `Math.E`.\n\n| Method | Returns | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| `pow(double, double)` | double | `Math.pow(2.0, 3.0)` → `8.0` |\n| `abs(x)` (int/long/float/double) | same type | `Math.abs(-6)` → `6`, `Math.abs(-5.5)` → `5.5` |\n| `max(a,b)`, `min(a,b)` | same type (widened) | `Math.max(3, 2)` → `3`; `Math.max(3, 2.0)` → `3.0` |\n| `round(double)` | **long** | `Math.round(3.2)` → `3`, `Math.round(3.6)` → `4` |\n| `round(float)` | **int** | `Math.round(3.6f)` → `4` |\n| `ceil(double)` | double | `Math.ceil(3.2)` → `4.0`, `Math.ceil(-3.2)` → `-3.0` |\n| `floor(double)` | double | `Math.floor(3.9)` → `3.0`, `Math.floor(-3.2)` → `-4.0` |\n| `sqrt(double)` | double | `Math.sqrt(4)` → `2.0` |\n| `random()` | double in **[0.0, 1.0)** | no arguments |\n\nGotchas: `ceil`/`floor` return **double** (`(int) Math.ceil(4.01)` → `5`). `Math.round(2.5)` → `3` but `Math.round(-2.5)` → `-2` (rounds half up). Pseudo-random = produced by a deterministic function; class `java.util.Random` is more flexible (slide 33).\n\n## 5. Wrapper classes, boxing, unboxing (slides 34–44)\n\n| Primitive | Wrapper | Unboxing method | Parse method |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| byte | Byte | byteValue() | Byte.parseByte |\n| short | Short | shortValue() | Short.parseShort |\n| int | **Integer** | intValue() | Integer.parseInt |\n| long | Long | longValue() | Long.parseLong |\n| float | Float | floatValue() | Float.parseFloat |\n| double | Double | doubleValue() | Double.parseDouble |\n| char | **Character** | charValue() | — |\n| boolean | Boolean | booleanValue() | Boolean.parseBoolean |\n\n- **Boxing**: primitive → wrapper object. **Unboxing**: wrapper → primitive (slides 35–36). Wrappers have **no no-argument constructor**.\n- **Automatic** since Java 5: `Integer n = 42;` (autoboxing), `int i = n;` (auto-unboxing) (slide 37).\n- Constants: `Integer.MAX_VALUE`, `Integer.MIN_VALUE`, `Double.MAX_VALUE`, `Boolean.TRUE`, `Boolean.FALSE` (slide 38). Trap: `Double.MIN_VALUE` is the **smallest positive** double (4.9E-324), not the most negative.\n- `Integer.parseInt(\"101\", 2)` → `5` (radix 2, slide 39). `Integer.parseInt(\"ff\", 16)` → `255`. `Integer.parseInt(\"12a\")` throws `NumberFormatException`.\n- `Double.toString(123.99)` → `\"123.99\"` (slide 41).\n- Character methods (slides 42–44, all static): `toUpperCase`, `toLowerCase`, `isUpperCase`, `isLowerCase`, `isWhitespace` (space, `'\\t'`, `'\\n'`), `isLetter`, `isDigit`, `isLetterOrDigit`. `isLetterOrDigit('_')` is `false`.\n\nTraps (verified):\n```java\nSystem.out.println(Character.toUpperCase('a') + 1);       // 66   (char + int = int)\nSystem.out.println(\"\" + Character.toUpperCase('a') + 1);  // A1\nInteger i1 = 1000, i2 = 1000;\nSystem.out.println(i1 == i2);        // false  (two objects, compares addresses)\nSystem.out.println(i1.equals(i2));   // true\nInteger x = 127, y = 127;\nSystem.out.println(x == y);          // true   (values -128..127 are cached: never rely on this)\nInteger z = null; int k = z;         // NullPointerException on auto-unboxing\n```\n\n## 6. Variables and memory (slides 45–48)\n\n- Secondary memory = files (permanent). Main memory = running program's variables.\n- Main memory = numbered **bytes** (8 bits each). The number is the **address**.\n- A data item that needs several bytes uses adjacent bytes = its **memory location**; its address is the address of the **first** byte.\n- Display 5.10 (slide 48): variable1 = 3 bytes at address 0, variable2 = 2 bytes at address 3, variable3 = 1 byte at 5, variable4 = 3 bytes at 6.\n\n## 7. References (slides 49–58)\n\n- Primitive variable: the **value itself** is in the variable's location. Every primitive type always uses the same number of bytes.\n- Class-type variable: holds only the **memory address (reference)** of the object. The object lives elsewhere and can be any size; the reference is fixed size.\n- `ToyClass sampleVariable;` creates the variable with no value. `sampleVariable = new ToyClass(\"Josephine Student\", 42);` creates the object and stores its address (Display 5.12, address \"2056\").\n- **Assignment copies the address**, not the object (Display 5.13):\n\n```\nToyClass variable1 = new ToyClass(\"Joe\", 42);   variable1 ──► [4068: \"Joe\", 42]\nToyClass variable2;                             variable2 = ?\nvariable2 = variable1;                          variable1 ──► [4068] ◄── variable2\nvariable2.set(\"Josephine\", 1);                  object at 4068 is now \"Josephine\", 1\nSystem.out.println(variable1);                  → Josephine 1\n```\n\n- Slide 57/58 (Q4): a class variable stores **a memory address that identifies where the object is stored**.\n\n## 8. Class parameters: call-by-value of an address (slides 59–70)\n\n- **All Java parameters are call-by-value.** The parameter is a local variable initialised with a copy of the argument.\n- For a class-type parameter, the copied value is the **address**. Parameter and argument name the **same object**, so the method **can change the object's instance variables**.\n- But the method **cannot make the caller's variable point to a different object** — assigning a new object to the parameter only changes the local copy (slide 60 NOTE).\n- A method **cannot change a primitive argument** (slide 66).\n\nDisplay 5.14 / 5.15 (slides 61–65): `ToyClass.changer(anObject)` sets `aParameter.name = \"Hot Shot\"; aParameter.number = 42;` → prints `Mr. Cellophane 0`, then `Hot Shot 42`.\n\nDisplay 5.16 / 5.17 (slides 67–70): `object1.makeEqual(object2)` copies object1's data INTO object2 (Scorpius 1); `object1.tryToMakeEqual(aNumber)` does nothing to `aNumber` (stays 42).\n\n```java\nstatic void moveAway(Point p) { p = new Point(9, 9); }  // only the local copy changes\nstatic void shift(Point p)    { p.x++; }                 // changes the shared object\nPoint a = new Point(1, 1);\nmoveAway(a); shift(a);\nSystem.out.println(a.x + \" \" + a.y);                     // 2 1\n```\n\n## 9. `=` and `==` with objects; `null`; anonymous objects (slides 71–74)\n\n- `=` with class types makes two names for **one** object.\n- `==` compares **addresses**. Two different objects with equal data → `==` is `false`. Use `equals` to compare content.\n- `null` = a placeholder reference that names no object. Test with `==` / `!=`, never `x.equals(null)`-style on a null `x`.\n- Calling a method on `null` → **NullPointerException** (slide 73). (An uninitialised *local* class variable is a compile error, \"might not have been initialized\"; an uninitialised *instance* variable defaults to `null`.)\n- `new` calls a constructor and **returns a reference**. An object whose reference is never stored in a variable is an **anonymous object**: `System.out.println(new ToyClass(\"Tmp\", 0));`\n\n## 10. Privacy leaks (slide 75)\n\n- `private` is enough for primitives and immutable types (`String`, wrappers).\n- For a **mutable** class-type instance variable, returning it from a getter (or storing a caller's object directly in a constructor) hands out its address. The caller can then change your \"private\" data.\n- Fix: return/store a **copy** (copy constructor — Week 5), or return only primitive/immutable data.\n\n```java\nclass Wallet {\n    private int[] coins = {1, 2, 3};\n    public int[] getCoins() { return coins; }           // LEAK: returns the address\n    public int total() { int s = 0; for (int c : coins) s += c; return s; }\n}\nWallet w = new Wallet();\nw.getCoins()[0] = 100;\nSystem.out.println(w.total());                          // 105, private data was changed\n// Fix: public int[] getCoins() { return coins.clone(); }   or  Arrays.copyOf(coins, coins.length)\n```\n\n## 11. Packages and import (slides 76–86)\n\n- A **package** = a group of classes in one directory, used via `import`.\n- `import java.util.Scanner;` (one class) or `import java.util.*;` (all classes in that package; no extra run-time overhead).\n- Order in a file: `package` statement first (only blank lines/comments before it), then `import`s, then classes.\n- `java.lang` (Math, String, wrappers, System) is imported automatically.\n- Package name = directory path relative to a CLASSPATH entry: `utilities.numericstuff` ↔ `…\\libraries\\newlibraries\\utilities\\numericstuff` when `\\libraries\\newlibraries` is on the class path (Display 5.22, slide 81). Only the `.class` files are required in the directory.\n- **CLASSPATH** = list of directories Java searches, in order; first match wins. If you set it, include `.` (current directory) or Java may not find your own classes (slide 84). Override per command with `-classpath` (use it both when compiling and when running, slide 85).\n- **Subpackages are not imported by a wildcard**: `import utilities.numericstuff.*;` does NOT import `utilities.numericstuff.statistical` (slide 82).\n- **Default package** = all classes in the current directory with no `package` statement.\n- **Name clash**: two classes with the same name in different packages → use the **fully qualified name** `package_name.ClassName`; then no import is needed.\n\n## 12. Errata (slide claims that are wrong or misleading)\n\n| Slide | Slide says | Correct | How verified |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 62 (ToyClass listing, the instructor's own screenshot, untitled) | `equals` returns `(name.equals(otherObject.name)) && (number = otherObject.number)` | must be `number == otherObject.number`. With `=` it is an assignment (an `int`), and `boolean && int` does not compile. Display 5.17 on slide 70 has it right. | `javac`: \"bad operand types for binary operator '&&' … second type: int\" |\n| 82 | \"The import statements: `import utilities.numericstuff.*;` import both the utilities.numericstuff and utilities.numericstuff.statistical packages\" | A second line is missing. You need **two** statements: `import utilities.numericstuff.*;` **and** `import utilities.numericstuff.statistical.*;` One wildcard never imports a subpackage — the slide's own first bullet says so. | Savitch 6e §5.4 / Java language spec: a type-import-on-demand imports only that package |\n| 35 | `Integer integerObject = new Integer(42);` | Still compiles, but the wrapper constructors are **deprecated (for removal) since Java 9/16**. Use autoboxing `Integer n = 42;` or `Integer.valueOf(42)`. | JDK 26 `javac` warns: \"[deprecation] Integer(int) in Integer has been deprecated\" |\n| 34 | lists wrappers for byte, short, long, float, double, char only | Same as the book, but it leaves out **int → Integer** and **boolean → Boolean** — the two irregular names you are most likely to be asked about. | Java API |\n| 4 | System.out \"is instantiated from the System class\" | `out` is a static field **of** `System`, but the object is an instance of **`PrintStream`** (slide 5 says this correctly). | Java API: `public static final PrintStream out` |\n| 85 | `–classpath` (typographic en dash) | The option is typed with a normal hyphen: `-classpath` (or `-cp`). Copy-pasting the slide's dash fails. | `javac --help` |\n| 73 | \"if the object has not been initialized at all (and is not assigned to null), the results will be the same\" | True only for **instance variables** (they default to `null`). An uninitialised **local** variable is a **compile-time** error, not a NullPointerException. | `javac`: \"variable x might not have been initialized\" |\n\n## Likely exam questions\n\n1. **What is printed?**\n   ```java\n   Counter a = new Counter(), b = new Counter(), c = new Counter();\n   System.out.println(a.id + \" \" + c.id + \" \" + Counter.count);\n   ```\n   (Counter from §3.) **Answer:** `1 3 3`.\n2. **What is printed?** `ToyClass t1 = new ToyClass(\"A\",1), t2 = new ToyClass(\"A\",1), t3 = t1; System.out.println((t1==t2) + \" \" + t1.equals(t2) + \" \" + (t1==t3));` **Answer:** `false true true`. Then `t3.set(\"B\",2); System.out.println(t1);` → `B 2`.\n3. **What is printed?** `static void change(int n, ToyClass t) { n = 99; t.set(\"Changed\", 99); }` called with `n = 5`, `t = new ToyClass(\"Toy\",1)`, then `println(n + \" \" + t)`. **Answer:** `5 Changed 99`.\n4. **Find the bug.**\n   ```java\n   public class Box {\n       private int size;\n       public static void grow() { size++; }\n   }\n   ```\n   **Answer:** a static method cannot use the instance variable `size` (no `this`). Make `grow` non-static, or make `size` static if one shared value is intended.\n5. **Short answer (Q2/Q3, slides 21–22):** can a static method use `this`? Call a non-static method directly? **Answer:** No to both; there is no calling object. Create/receive an object and use `obj.method()`.\n6. **MCQ (Q4, slide 57):** what does a class-type variable store? **Answer:** the memory address of the object.\n7. **What is printed?** `System.out.println(Math.round(3.6) + Math.ceil(3.2));` **Answer:** `8.0` (long 4 + double 4.0 = double 8.0).\n8. **What is printed?** `System.out.println(Integer.parseInt(\"101\", 2) + Integer.parseInt(\"10\"));` **Answer:** `15`.\n9. **Explain the privacy leak** in `Wallet.getCoins()` (§10) and fix it. **Answer:** it returns the array's address; return `coins.clone()`.\n10. **True/False:** `import java.util.*;` also imports `java.util.concurrent.*`. **Answer:** False — subpackages are never included.\n11. **Write a method** `static int countDigits(String s)` using `Character.isDigit`. **Answer:** loop over `s.charAt(i)`, count when `Character.isDigit(c)`.\n12. **What happens?** `Integer z = null; int k = z;` **Answer:** NullPointerException at run time (auto-unboxing calls `z.intValue()`).\n13. **printf:** what does `System.out.printf(\"%6.2f|\", 3.14159)` print? **Answer:** `  3.14|` (two leading spaces).\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"What is a static method?","a":"A method that belongs to the class itself, not to any object; you call it with the class name, e.g. Math.random()."},{"q":"Can a static method use the keyword this?","a":"No. There is no calling object associated with a static method."},{"q":"Can a static method call a non-static method of its own class directly?","a":"No. It must name an object: obj.method()."},{"q":"Can a static method use a static variable?","a":"Yes. It cannot use an instance variable directly."},{"q":"How many copies of a static variable exist?","a":"Exactly one per class, shared by all objects."},{"q":"Default values of uninitialised static (and instance) variables?","a":"boolean false, other primitives 0, class types null."},{"q":"When may a static variable be public?","a":"When it is a constant: public static final, e.g. public static final int BIRTH_YEAR = 1954."},{"q":"Is System.out a class or an object?","a":"An object: a static field of class System, an instance of PrintStream."},{"q":"What does printf(\"%6.2f\", 19.8) print?","a":"\" 19.80\" — width 6, two decimals, right-justified."},{"q":"What does Math.random() return?","a":"A pseudo-random double ≥ 0.0 and < 1.0; it takes no arguments."},{"q":"Return types of Math.round(double), Math.ceil, Math.floor?","a":"round(double) returns long; ceil and floor return double."},{"q":"Math.ceil(-3.2) and Math.floor(-3.2)?","a":"-3.0 and -4.0."},{"q":"Why must you not import java.lang.Math?","a":"java.lang is imported automatically (Math, String, System, the wrapper classes)."},{"q":"Wrapper classes of int and char?","a":"Integer and Character."},{"q":"What is boxing? Unboxing?","a":"Boxing: primitive value to wrapper object. Unboxing: wrapper object to primitive value (e.g. intValue())."},{"q":"Do wrapper classes have a no-argument constructor?","a":"No."},{"q":"What does Integer.parseInt(\"101\", 2) return?","a":"5 — the second argument is the radix."},{"q":"What is Double.MIN_VALUE?","a":"The smallest positive double (about 4.9E-324), not the most negative one."},{"q":"What does Character.toUpperCase('a') + 1 print?","a":"66, because char + int is an int."},{"q":"Integer a = 1000, b = 1000; what is a == b?","a":"false — == compares addresses of two objects; use equals."},{"q":"What does a primitive variable store vs a class-type variable?","a":"Primitive: the value itself. Class type: the memory address (reference) of the object."},{"q":"What does obj2 = obj1 do for class types?","a":"Copies the address: both variables now name the same object."},{"q":"Is Java call-by-value or call-by-reference?","a":"Always call-by-value; for class types the value copied is the address."},{"q":"Can a method change the instance variables of an object passed to it?","a":"Yes, because parameter and argument name the same object."},{"q":"Can a method make the caller's variable refer to a new object by assigning to its parameter?","a":"No — only the local copy of the address changes."},{"q":"Can a method change a primitive argument?","a":"No."},{"q":"What does == test for objects?","a":"Whether the two variables hold the same memory address (the same object), not equal content."},{"q":"How do you test whether a variable is null?","a":"With == or != (e.g. if (x == null)), not with equals."},{"q":"What happens when you call a method on a null reference?","a":"A NullPointerException at run time."},{"q":"What is an anonymous object?","a":"An object created with new whose reference is never assigned to a variable."},{"q":"Why is private not enough for a class-type instance variable?","a":"A getter that returns it hands out its address, so outside code can change the private object (privacy leak); return a copy instead."},{"q":"Order of package and import statements in a file?","a":"package first (only comments/blank lines before it), then imports."},{"q":"Does import utilities.numericstuff.* import utilities.numericstuff.statistical?","a":"No. Subpackages need their own import."},{"q":"What is the CLASSPATH?","a":"The list of directories Java searches, in order, for packages; it should include . (current directory)."},{"q":"How do you resolve a name clash between two classes with the same name?","a":"Use the fully qualified name package_name.ClassName; no import is then needed."}],"figs":[{"n":1,"src":"slides/W3s001.jpg"},{"n":2,"src":"slides/W3s002.jpg"},{"n":3,"src":"slides/W3s003.jpg"},{"n":4,"src":"slides/W3s004.jpg"},{"n":5,"src":"slides/W3s005.jpg"},{"n":6,"src":"slides/W3s006.jpg"},{"n":7,"src":"slides/W3s007.jpg"},{"n":8,"src":"slides/W3s008.jpg"},{"n":9,"src":"slides/W3s009.jpg"},{"n":10,"src":"slides/W3s010.jpg"},{"n":11,"src":"slides/W3s011.jpg"},{"n":12,"src":"slides/W3s012.jpg"},{"n":13,"src":"slides/W3s013.jpg"},{"n":14,"src":"slides/W3s014.jpg"},{"n":15,"src":"slides/W3s015.jpg"},{"n":16,"src":"slides/W3s016.jpg"},{"n":17,"src":"slides/W3s017.jpg"},{"n":18,"src":"slides/W3s018.jpg"},{"n":19,"src":"slides/W3s019.jpg"},{"n":20,"src":"slides/W3s020.jpg"},{"n":21,"src":"slides/W3s021.jpg"},{"n":22,"src":"slides/W3s022.jpg"},{"n":23,"src":"slides/W3s023.jpg"},{"n":24,"src":"slides/W3s024.jpg"},{"n":25,"src":"slides/W3s025.jpg"},{"n":26,"src":"slides/W3s026.jpg"},{"n":27,"src":"slides/W3s027.jpg"},{"n":28,"src":"slides/W3s028.jpg"},{"n":29,"src":"slides/W3s029.jpg"},{"n":30,"src":"slides/W3s030.jpg"},{"n":31,"src":"slides/W3s031.jpg"},{"n":32,"src":"slides/W3s032.jpg"},{"n":33,"src":"slides/W3s033.jpg"},{"n":34,"src":"slides/W3s034.jpg"},{"n":35,"src":"slides/W3s035.jpg"},{"n":36,"src":"slides/W3s036.jpg"},{"n":37,"src":"slides/W3s037.jpg"},{"n":38,"src":"slides/W3s038.jpg"},{"n":39,"src":"slides/W3s039.jpg"},{"n":40,"src":"slides/W3s040.jpg"},{"n":41,"src":"slides/W3s041.jpg"},{"n":42,"src":"slides/W3s042.jpg"},{"n":43,"src":"slides/W3s043.jpg"},{"n":44,"src":"slides/W3s044.jpg"},{"n":45,"src":"slides/W3s045.jpg"},{"n":46,"src":"slides/W3s046.jpg"},{"n":47,"src":"slides/W3s047.jpg"},{"n":48,"src":"slides/W3s048.jpg"},{"n":49,"src":"slides/W3s049.jpg"},{"n":50,"src":"slides/W3s050.jpg"},{"n":51,"src":"slides/W3s051.jpg"},{"n":52,"src":"slides/W3s052.jpg"},{"n":53,"src":"slides/W3s053.jpg"},{"n":54,"src":"slides/W3s054.jpg"},{"n":55,"src":"slides/W3s055.jpg"},{"n":56,"src":"slides/W3s056.jpg"},{"n":57,"src":"slides/W3s057.jpg"},{"n":58,"src":"slides/W3s058.jpg"},{"n":59,"src":"slides/W3s059.jpg"},{"n":60,"src":"slides/W3s060.jpg"},{"n":61,"src":"slides/W3s061.jpg"},{"n":62,"src":"slides/W3s062.jpg"},{"n":63,"src":"slides/W3s063.jpg"},{"n":64,"src":"slides/W3s064.jpg"},{"n":65,"src":"slides/W3s065.jpg"},{"n":66,"src":"slides/W3s066.jpg"},{"n":67,"src":"slides/W3s067.jpg"},{"n":68,"src":"slides/W3s068.jpg"},{"n":69,"src":"slides/W3s069.jpg"},{"n":70,"src":"slides/W3s070.jpg"},{"n":71,"src":"slides/W3s071.jpg"},{"n":72,"src":"slides/W3s072.jpg"},{"n":73,"src":"slides/W3s073.jpg"},{"n":74,"src":"slides/W3s074.jpg"},{"n":75,"src":"slides/W3s075.jpg"},{"n":76,"src":"slides/W3s076.jpg"},{"n":77,"src":"slides/W3s077.jpg"},{"n":78,"src":"slides/W3s078.jpg"},{"n":79,"src":"slides/W3s079.jpg"},{"n":80,"src":"slides/W3s080.jpg"},{"n":81,"src":"slides/W3s081.jpg"},{"n":82,"src":"slides/W3s082.jpg"},{"n":83,"src":"slides/W3s083.jpg"},{"n":84,"src":"slides/W3s084.jpg"},{"n":85,"src":"slides/W3s085.jpg"},{"n":86,"src":"slides/W3s086.jpg"},{"n":87,"src":"slides/W3s087.jpg"}],"exercises":[{"id":"e1","lang":"java","title":"Temperature with static helpers","level":"warm","blurb":"Slides 15–19 (Display 5.3). Write class `Temperature`:","prompt":"Slides 15–19 (Display 5.3). Write class `Temperature`:\n- private instance variable `degrees` (Celsius), constructor `Temperature(double degrees)`, getter `double getDegrees()`.\n- `public static double toCelsius(double degreesF)` → `5 * (degreesF - 32) / 9`.\n- `public static double toFahrenheit(double degreesC)` → `degreesC * 9 / 5 + 32`.\n- a **static** counter: `public static int getCount()` returns how many `Temperature` objects have been created so far.\n\nExample: `Temperature.toCelsius(212)` → `100.0`; after creating 3 objects, `Temperature.getCount()` → `3`.","starter":"class Temperature {\n    private double degrees;\n\n    public Temperature(double degrees) {\n        this.degrees = degrees;\n    }\n\n    public double getDegrees() { return degrees; }\n\n    public static double toCelsius(double degreesF) {\n        return 0;   // TODO\n    }\n\n    public static double toFahrenheit(double degreesC) {\n        return 0;   // TODO\n    }\n\n    public static int getCount() {\n        return 0;   // TODO: needs a static variable\n    }\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    static void check(boolean ok, String msg) { if (!ok) throw new AssertionError(msg); }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        check(Math.abs(Temperature.toCelsius(212) - 100.0) < 1e-9, \"toCelsius(212) should be 100.0\");\n        check(Math.abs(Temperature.toCelsius(32)) < 1e-9, \"toCelsius(32) should be 0.0\");\n        check(Math.abs(Temperature.toFahrenheit(100) - 212.0) < 1e-9, \"toFahrenheit(100) should be 212.0\");\n        check(Math.abs(Temperature.toFahrenheit(-40) + 40.0) < 1e-9, \"toFahrenheit(-40) should be -40.0\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS conversions\");\n        int before = Temperature.getCount();\n        Temperature a = new Temperature(10), b = new Temperature(20), c = new Temperature(30);\n        check(Temperature.getCount() == before + 3, \"getCount() should grow by 1 per new object; got \" + Temperature.getCount());\n        check(b.getDegrees() == 20, \"getDegrees() broken\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS static counter\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Temperature {\n    private double degrees;\n    private static int count = 0;          // one copy shared by all objects\n\n    public Temperature(double degrees) {\n        this.degrees = degrees;\n        count++;\n    }\n\n    public double getDegrees() { return degrees; }\n\n    public static double toCelsius(double degreesF) {\n        return 5 * (degreesF - 32) / 9;\n    }\n\n    public static double toFahrenheit(double degreesC) {\n        return degreesC * 9 / 5 + 32;\n    }\n\n    public static int getCount() {\n        return count;                      // static method may use a static variable\n    }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e2","lang":"java","title":"Character statistics","level":"warm","blurb":"Slides 41–44 (Character methods). Write class `CharStats` with three **static** methods:","prompt":"Slides 41–44 (Character methods). Write class `CharStats` with three **static** methods:\n- `static int countDigits(String s)` — characters for which `Character.isDigit` is true.\n- `static int countUpper(String s)` — uppercase letters.\n- `static String swapCase(String s)` — every uppercase letter becomes lowercase and vice versa; other characters unchanged.\n\nExample: `countDigits(\"CS105 rocks\")` → `3`; `countUpper(\"CS105 Rocks\")` → `3`; `swapCase(\"Java 17!\")` → `\"jAVA 17!\"`.","starter":"class CharStats {\n    static int countDigits(String s) {\n        return 0;   // TODO\n    }\n\n    static int countUpper(String s) {\n        return 0;   // TODO\n    }\n\n    static String swapCase(String s) {\n        return s;   // TODO\n    }\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    static void check(boolean ok, String msg) { if (!ok) throw new AssertionError(msg); }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        check(CharStats.countDigits(\"CS105 rocks\") == 3, \"countDigits(\\\"CS105 rocks\\\") should be 3\");\n        check(CharStats.countDigits(\"\") == 0, \"countDigits(\\\"\\\") should be 0\");\n        check(CharStats.countUpper(\"CS105 Rocks\") == 3, \"countUpper(\\\"CS105 Rocks\\\") should be 3\");\n        check(CharStats.countUpper(\"abc\") == 0, \"countUpper(\\\"abc\\\") should be 0\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS counting\");\n        check(CharStats.swapCase(\"Java 17!\").equals(\"jAVA 17!\"), \"swapCase(\\\"Java 17!\\\") should be \\\"jAVA 17!\\\", got \" + CharStats.swapCase(\"Java 17!\"));\n        check(CharStats.swapCase(\"\").equals(\"\"), \"swapCase(\\\"\\\") should be empty\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS swapCase\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class CharStats {\n    static int countDigits(String s) {\n        int n = 0;\n        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++)\n            if (Character.isDigit(s.charAt(i))) n++;\n        return n;\n    }\n\n    static int countUpper(String s) {\n        int n = 0;\n        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++)\n            if (Character.isUpperCase(s.charAt(i))) n++;\n        return n;\n    }\n\n    static String swapCase(String s) {\n        String out = \"\";\n        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {\n            char c = s.charAt(i);\n            if (Character.isUpperCase(c)) out += Character.toLowerCase(c);\n            else if (Character.isLowerCase(c)) out += Character.toUpperCase(c);\n            else out += c;\n        }\n        return out;\n    }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e3","lang":"java","title":"Safe parsing with wrapper classes","level":"core","blurb":"Slides 38–41. Write class `Parse` with:","prompt":"Slides 38–41. Write class `Parse` with:\n- `static int parseOrDefault(String s, int def)` — returns `Integer.parseInt(s)`, or `def` if `s` is `null` or not a valid integer (catch `NumberFormatException`).\n- `static int sumBinary(String[] bins)` — each string is a binary number (`\"101\"`); return the sum of their values (`Integer.parseInt(x, 2)`). Skip invalid strings.\n- `static String describe(Integer n)` — returns `\"none\"` if `n` is `null`, otherwise `\"even\"` or `\"odd\"` (careful: auto-unboxing `null` throws).\n\nExample: `parseOrDefault(\"42\", -1)` → `42`, `parseOrDefault(\"4x2\", -1)` → `-1`; `sumBinary({\"101\",\"11\",\"2\"})` → `8`.","starter":"class Parse {\n    static int parseOrDefault(String s, int def) {\n        return def;   // TODO\n    }\n\n    static int sumBinary(String[] bins) {\n        return 0;     // TODO\n    }\n\n    static String describe(Integer n) {\n        return n % 2 == 0 ? \"even\" : \"odd\";   // TODO: crashes on null\n    }\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    static void check(boolean ok, String msg) { if (!ok) throw new AssertionError(msg); }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        check(Parse.parseOrDefault(\"42\", -1) == 42, \"parseOrDefault(\\\"42\\\", -1) should be 42\");\n        check(Parse.parseOrDefault(\"-7\", 0) == -7, \"parseOrDefault(\\\"-7\\\", 0) should be -7\");\n        check(Parse.parseOrDefault(\"4x2\", -1) == -1, \"parseOrDefault(\\\"4x2\\\", -1) should be -1\");\n        check(Parse.parseOrDefault(null, 5) == 5, \"parseOrDefault(null, 5) should be 5\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS parseOrDefault\");\n        check(Parse.sumBinary(new String[]{\"101\", \"11\", \"2\"}) == 8, \"sumBinary({101, 11, 2}) should be 5+3 = 8\");\n        check(Parse.sumBinary(new String[]{}) == 0, \"sumBinary({}) should be 0\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS sumBinary\");\n        String r;\n        try { r = Parse.describe(null); } catch (NullPointerException e) { throw new AssertionError(\"describe(null) threw NullPointerException; return \\\"none\\\"\"); }\n        check(r.equals(\"none\"), \"describe(null) should be \\\"none\\\"\");\n        check(Parse.describe(4).equals(\"even\") && Parse.describe(7).equals(\"odd\"), \"describe(4)/describe(7) wrong\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS describe\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Parse {\n    static int parseOrDefault(String s, int def) {\n        if (s == null) return def;\n        try {\n            return Integer.parseInt(s);\n        } catch (NumberFormatException e) {\n            return def;\n        }\n    }\n\n    static int sumBinary(String[] bins) {\n        int sum = 0;\n        for (String b : bins) {\n            try {\n                sum += Integer.parseInt(b, 2);\n            } catch (NumberFormatException e) {\n                // \"2\" is not a binary digit: skip\n            }\n        }\n        return sum;\n    }\n\n    static String describe(Integer n) {\n        if (n == null) return \"none\";      // test with == before unboxing\n        return n % 2 == 0 ? \"even\" : \"odd\";\n    }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e4","lang":"java","title":"Class parameters: copy and swap contents","level":"core","blurb":"Slides 59–70 (Display 5.16/5.17). Class `Toy` has private `name` and `number`, a constructor, `toString()` returning `name + \" \" + number`, and `equals(Toy othe","prompt":"Slides 59–70 (Display 5.16/5.17). Class `Toy` has private `name` and `number`, a constructor, `toString()` returning `name + \" \" + number`, and `equals(Toy other)`. Write:\n- `void makeEqual(Toy other)` — copies **this** object's data into `other` (like Display 5.17).\n- `static void swapContents(Toy a, Toy b)` — after the call, `a` holds b's old data and `b` holds a's old data. (Swapping the parameters `a` and `b` themselves would not work — why?)\n- `boolean equals(Toy other)` — same name (use `equals`) and same number; `false` if `other` is `null`.","starter":"class Toy {\n    private String name;\n    private int number;\n\n    public Toy(String name, int number) { this.name = name; this.number = number; }\n\n    public String toString() { return name + \" \" + number; }\n\n    public void makeEqual(Toy other) {\n        other = this;   // TODO: this only changes the local copy of the address\n    }\n\n    public static void swapContents(Toy a, Toy b) {\n        Toy t = a; a = b; b = t;   // TODO: swaps the local copies only\n    }\n\n    public boolean equals(Toy other) {\n        return this == other;   // TODO: compare contents, handle null\n    }\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    static void check(boolean ok, String msg) { if (!ok) throw new AssertionError(msg); }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Toy o1 = new Toy(\"Scorpius\", 1), o2 = new Toy(\"John Crichton\", 2);\n        o1.makeEqual(o2);\n        check(o2.toString().equals(\"Scorpius 1\"), \"after o1.makeEqual(o2), o2 should be \\\"Scorpius 1\\\", got \" + o2);\n        check(o1 != o2, \"makeEqual must copy data, not make the variables share an object\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS makeEqual\");\n        Toy a = new Toy(\"A\", 1), b = new Toy(\"B\", 2);\n        Toy keepA = a;\n        Toy.swapContents(a, b);\n        check(a.toString().equals(\"B 2\") && b.toString().equals(\"A 1\"), \"after swapContents: a should be \\\"B 2\\\", b \\\"A 1\\\"; got \" + a + \", \" + b);\n        check(a == keepA, \"swapContents cannot change which object a refers to\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS swapContents\");\n        check(new Toy(\"X\", 3).equals(new Toy(\"X\", 3)), \"two different objects with equal data should be equal\");\n        check(!new Toy(\"X\", 3).equals(new Toy(\"X\", 4)), \"different numbers should not be equal\");\n        check(!new Toy(\"X\", 3).equals(null), \"equals(null) should be false\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS equals\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Toy {\n    private String name;\n    private int number;\n\n    public Toy(String name, int number) { this.name = name; this.number = number; }\n\n    public String toString() { return name + \" \" + number; }\n\n    public void makeEqual(Toy other) {\n        other.name = this.name;          // change the object, not the parameter\n        other.number = this.number;\n    }\n\n    public static void swapContents(Toy a, Toy b) {\n        String n = a.name; int k = a.number;\n        a.name = b.name; a.number = b.number;\n        b.name = n; b.number = k;\n    }\n\n    public boolean equals(Toy other) {\n        if (other == null) return false;\n        return name.equals(other.name) && number == other.number;\n    }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e5","lang":"java","title":"Fix the privacy leak","level":"core","blurb":"Slide 75. `Birthday` is a **mutable** class (it has setters). `Person` stores one in a private field. The starter leaks it twice: the constructor stores the cal","prompt":"Slide 75. `Birthday` is a **mutable** class (it has setters). `Person` stores one in a private field. The starter leaks it twice: the constructor stores the caller's object, and the getter returns the private object. Fix `Person` so that **no outside code can change a Person's birthday** after construction:\n- the constructor must store a **copy** (use the copy constructor `new Birthday(other)`),\n- `getBirthday()` must return a **copy**.\n\nDo not change `Birthday`.","starter":"class Birthday {\n    private int day, month, year;\n    public Birthday(int day, int month, int year) { this.day = day; this.month = month; this.year = year; }\n    public Birthday(Birthday other) { this(other.day, other.month, other.year); }\n    public void setYear(int year) { this.year = year; }\n    public int getYear() { return year; }\n    public String toString() { return day + \".\" + month + \".\" + year; }\n}\n\nclass Person {\n    private String name;\n    private Birthday born;\n\n    public Person(String name, Birthday born) {\n        this.name = name;\n        this.born = born;            // TODO: leak\n    }\n\n    public Birthday getBirthday() {\n        return born;                 // TODO: leak\n    }\n\n    public String toString() { return name + \" \" + born; }\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    static void check(boolean ok, String msg) { if (!ok) throw new AssertionError(msg); }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Birthday b = new Birthday(1, 1, 2005);\n        Person p = new Person(\"Ana\", b);\n        b.setYear(1900);\n        check(p.toString().equals(\"Ana 1.1.2005\"), \"changing the caller's Birthday changed the Person: \" + p);\n        System.out.println(\"PASS constructor copies\");\n        Birthday got = p.getBirthday();\n        check(got.getYear() == 2005, \"getBirthday() should return the right data\");\n        got.setYear(1800);\n        check(p.toString().equals(\"Ana 1.1.2005\"), \"changing the returned Birthday changed the Person: \" + p);\n        check(p.getBirthday() != p.getBirthday(), \"getBirthday() should return a fresh copy each time\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS getter copies\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Birthday {\n    private int day, month, year;\n    public Birthday(int day, int month, int year) { this.day = day; this.month = month; this.year = year; }\n    public Birthday(Birthday other) { this(other.day, other.month, other.year); }\n    public void setYear(int year) { this.year = year; }\n    public int getYear() { return year; }\n    public String toString() { return day + \".\" + month + \".\" + year; }\n}\n\nclass Person {\n    private String name;                 // String is immutable: no copy needed\n    private Birthday born;\n\n    public Person(String name, Birthday born) {\n        this.name = name;\n        this.born = new Birthday(born);  // store a copy\n    }\n\n    public Birthday getBirthday() {\n        return new Birthday(born);       // hand out a copy\n    }\n\n    public String toString() { return name + \" \" + born; }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e6","lang":"java","title":"== versus equals","level":"core","blurb":"Slides 71–72. Write class `Point` (int `x`, `y`; constructor) with:","prompt":"Slides 71–72. Write class `Point` (int `x`, `y`; constructor) with:\n- `boolean equals(Point other)` — same coordinates; `false` for `null`.\n- `static int countEqual(Point[] pts, Point target)` — how many elements have the same **content** as `target` (skip `null` elements).\n- `static int countSame(Point[] pts, Point target)` — how many elements are the **same object** as `target`.","starter":"class Point {\n    int x, y;\n    Point(int x, int y) { this.x = x; this.y = y; }\n\n    public boolean equals(Point other) {\n        return this == other;   // TODO\n    }\n\n    static int countEqual(Point[] pts, Point target) {\n        return 0;   // TODO\n    }\n\n    static int countSame(Point[] pts, Point target) {\n        return 0;   // TODO\n    }\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    static void check(boolean ok, String msg) { if (!ok) throw new AssertionError(msg); }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Point t = new Point(1, 2);\n        Point[] pts = { new Point(1, 2), t, null, new Point(2, 1), t, new Point(1, 2) };\n        check(new Point(3, 4).equals(new Point(3, 4)), \"equal coordinates should be equal\");\n        check(!t.equals(null), \"equals(null) should be false\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS equals\");\n        check(Point.countEqual(pts, t) == 4, \"countEqual should be 4 (content), got \" + Point.countEqual(pts, t));\n        check(Point.countSame(pts, t) == 2, \"countSame should be 2 (same object), got \" + Point.countSame(pts, t));\n        System.out.println(\"PASS countEqual / countSame\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Point {\n    int x, y;\n    Point(int x, int y) { this.x = x; this.y = y; }\n\n    public boolean equals(Point other) {\n        if (other == null) return false;\n        return x == other.x && y == other.y;\n    }\n\n    static int countEqual(Point[] pts, Point target) {\n        int n = 0;\n        for (Point p : pts)\n            if (p != null && p.equals(target)) n++;\n        return n;\n    }\n\n    static int countSame(Point[] pts, Point target) {\n        int n = 0;\n        for (Point p : pts)\n            if (p == target) n++;        // same address\n        return n;\n    }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e7","lang":"java","title":"Immutable Fraction with static members","level":"hard","blurb":"Combines static methods, static constants, wrapper parsing and equals. Write class `Fraction`:","prompt":"Combines static methods, static constants, wrapper parsing and equals. Write class `Fraction`:\n- private final `num`, `den`; constructor `Fraction(int num, int den)` stores the fraction **in lowest terms** with a **positive denominator** (`new Fraction(2, -4)` is `-1/2`); throw `IllegalArgumentException` if `den == 0`.\n- `public static final Fraction ZERO` (0/1) and `ONE` (1/1).\n- `static int gcd(int a, int b)` — greatest common divisor of |a| and |b| (gcd(0, b) = |b|).\n- `static Fraction parse(String s)` — accepts `\"3/4\"` or a whole number `\"5\"` (use `Integer.parseInt`, trim spaces around parts).\n- `Fraction plus(Fraction o)`, `Fraction times(Fraction o)` — return new objects (never change `this`).\n- `boolean equals(Fraction o)`, `String toString()` → `\"num/den\"`, or just `\"num\"` when den is 1.","starter":"class Fraction {\n    private final int num, den;\n    public static final Fraction ZERO = null;   // TODO\n    public static final Fraction ONE = null;    // TODO\n\n    public Fraction(int num, int den) {\n        this.num = num;   // TODO: reduce, fix sign, reject 0\n        this.den = den;\n    }\n\n    static int gcd(int a, int b) { return 1; }              // TODO\n    static Fraction parse(String s) { return null; }        // TODO\n    public Fraction plus(Fraction o) { return this; }       // TODO\n    public Fraction times(Fraction o) { return this; }      // TODO\n    public boolean equals(Fraction o) { return this == o; } // TODO\n    public String toString() { return num + \"/\" + den; }    // TODO: whole numbers\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    static void check(boolean ok, String msg) { if (!ok) throw new AssertionError(msg); }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        check(Fraction.gcd(12, 18) == 6 && Fraction.gcd(-4, 6) == 2 && Fraction.gcd(0, 5) == 5, \"gcd wrong\");\n        check(new Fraction(2, 4).toString().equals(\"1/2\"), \"2/4 should print 1/2, got \" + new Fraction(2, 4));\n        check(new Fraction(2, -4).toString().equals(\"-1/2\"), \"2/-4 should print -1/2, got \" + new Fraction(2, -4));\n        check(new Fraction(6, 3).toString().equals(\"2\"), \"6/3 should print 2, got \" + new Fraction(6, 3));\n        boolean threw = false;\n        try { new Fraction(1, 0); } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) { threw = true; }\n        check(threw, \"denominator 0 should throw IllegalArgumentException\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS constructor\");\n        check(Fraction.ZERO != null && Fraction.ZERO.toString().equals(\"0\"), \"ZERO should print 0\");\n        check(Fraction.ONE != null && Fraction.ONE.toString().equals(\"1\"), \"ONE should print 1\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS constants\");\n        Fraction a = Fraction.parse(\" 1 / 3 \"), b = Fraction.parse(\"1/6\");\n        check(a != null && a.equals(new Fraction(1, 3)), \"parse(\\\" 1 / 3 \\\") should be 1/3\");\n        check(Fraction.parse(\"5\").equals(new Fraction(5, 1)), \"parse(\\\"5\\\") should be 5\");\n        check(a.plus(b).toString().equals(\"1/2\"), \"1/3 + 1/6 should be 1/2, got \" + a.plus(b));\n        check(a.times(b).toString().equals(\"1/18\"), \"1/3 * 1/6 should be 1/18, got \" + a.times(b));\n        check(a.toString().equals(\"1/3\"), \"plus/times must not change this\");\n        check(!a.equals(b) && !a.equals(null), \"equals wrong\");\n        check(Fraction.ONE.plus(Fraction.ZERO).equals(Fraction.ONE), \"1 + 0 should equal ONE\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS arithmetic\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Fraction {\n    private final int num, den;\n    public static final Fraction ZERO = new Fraction(0, 1);\n    public static final Fraction ONE = new Fraction(1, 1);\n\n    public Fraction(int num, int den) {\n        if (den == 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"denominator is 0\");\n        if (den < 0) { num = -num; den = -den; }\n        int g = gcd(num, den);\n        this.num = num / g;\n        this.den = den / g;\n    }\n\n    static int gcd(int a, int b) {\n        a = Math.abs(a); b = Math.abs(b);\n        while (b != 0) { int t = a % b; a = b; b = t; }\n        return a == 0 ? 1 : a;\n    }\n\n    static Fraction parse(String s) {\n        String[] parts = s.trim().split(\"/\");\n        int n = Integer.parseInt(parts[0].trim());\n        int d = parts.length > 1 ? Integer.parseInt(parts[1].trim()) : 1;\n        return new Fraction(n, d);\n    }\n\n    public Fraction plus(Fraction o) { return new Fraction(num * o.den + o.num * den, den * o.den); }\n\n    public Fraction times(Fraction o) { return new Fraction(num * o.num, den * o.den); }\n\n    public boolean equals(Fraction o) {\n        return o != null && num == o.num && den == o.den;   // both are in lowest terms\n    }\n\n    public String toString() { return den == 1 ? \"\" + num : num + \"/\" + den; }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."}]},{"id":"04","title":"W4 · UML & Patterns (ch. 12)","slides":"Week 4 · 47 slides","md":"# 04 — Week 4 · UML and Patterns (Savitch ch. 12, 47 slides)\n\n_Ingested 15.11.2026 from lectures/Week4_SavichJava_chap12_UML_ko.pptx (PDF copy in lectures/). Pearson slides by Rose Williams & Kenrick Mock (Absolute Java 6e, ch. 12), \"Updated by Dr. Kazeem Oladejo, October 2026\". His additions: the five UML diagram types with web examples (slides 5–10), \"Java Design Patterns\" (slide 18), the **Singleton** pattern (slide 19, not in Savitch ch. 12), and pictures of merge sort and quick sort (slides 28, 38, 44). All code below was compiled and run on JDK 26._\n\n> **Instructor-specific items (flagged).**\n> - **Five UML diagrams** (slide 5) with one online-shopping example each: activity (6), use case (7), sequence (8), state (9), class (10). Know one sentence per diagram.\n> - **Singleton** (slide 19): private constructor + public static method returning the one instance; uses: DB connection, logger; drawbacks: hard to test, tight coupling, bottleneck. This is his addition — likely exam material (\"write a Singleton\").\n> - Slide 18: patterns are \"established best practices and blueprints\", **not** code or libraries you paste in.\n> - Slide 45 repeats slide 42 with `join` highlighted in red: the point is that quick sort's `join` **does nothing**.\n> - No in-class question slides in this deck.\n\n## 1. UML and patterns: definitions (slides 2–4, 17–18)\n\n- **UML (Unified Modeling Language)**: a *graphical* language for designing and documenting OOP software. Language-independent.\n- **Pattern**: a template/outline of a software task that can be realised as different code in different but similar applications.\n- Before UML: **pseudocode** (linear, algebraic, no syntax details) and **flowcharts**.\n- History: UML released in **1996** by **Grady Booch, Ivar Jacobson and James Rumbaugh** (the \"three amigos\"); now maintained by the **Object Management Group (OMG)**.\n- A useful pattern applies across many situations; a substantive pattern makes some assumptions about its domain (slide 17).\n\n## 2. The five UML diagram types (slides 5–10)\n\n| Diagram | Shows | Slide example (online shop) | Key notation |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Activity** | the activities (workflow) of a process | Receive Order → fork → Fill Order (rush/else) ‖ Send Invoice → Receive Payment → join → end | rounded boxes, black start dot, fork/join bars, diamonds for decisions with [guards] |\n| **Use case** | interactions between **actors** and the system | Customer: View items, Make purchase, Complete checkout, Log in; Make purchase `<<include>>` View items and Complete checkout; actors Authentication service, Identity Provider, Credit Payment Service, PayPal | stick-figure actors, ovals inside a system boundary box |\n| **Sequence** | time-ordered messages between actors and components | Customer → EShopWebsite (access, browse, add to cart, checkout) → Payment Gateway → Shipping Company | vertical **lifelines**, horizontal message arrows, time flows down |\n| **State** | how the system's state changes in response to events | Register/Login → Search products → View item → Add to cart → See shopping cart → Checkout | states (rounded boxes), transitions with [conditions] |\n| **Class** | classes (name, data, methods) and their associations | Customer, User, Shopping cart, Order, Shipping info | 3-compartment boxes, association lines with multiplicities |\n\n### Slide 10 class diagram reproduced (online shopping cart)\n\n```\n            ┌────────────────────────────┐        ┌──────────────────────────┐\n            │ Customer                   │        │ User                     │\n            ├────────────────────────────┤        ├──────────────────────────┤\n            │ - customerName : string    │        │ - userId : string        │\n            │ - address : string         │        │ - password : string      │\n            │ - email : string           │───────▷│ - loginStatus : string   │◁──── (Administrator, cut off)\n            │ - creditCardInfo : string  │ (is-a) │ - registerDate : date    │\n            │ - shippingInfo : string    │        ├──────────────────────────┤\n            │ - accountBalance : float   │        │ + verifyLogin() : bool   │\n            ├────────────────────────────┤        └──────────────────────────┘\n            │ + register()               │\n            │ + login()                  │\n            │ + updateProfile()          │\n            └────────────────────────────┘\n   1 ◆ (to Shopping cart, 0..*)      1 ◆ (to Order, 0..*)\n\n  ┌──────────────────────┐   ┌──────────────────────────┐ 1◆────1 ┌──────────────────────────┐\n  │ Shopping cart        │   │ Order                    │         │ Shipping info            │\n  ├──────────────────────┤   ├──────────────────────────┤         ├──────────────────────────┤\n  │ - cartId : int       │   │ - orderId : int          │         │ - shippingId : int       │\n  │ - productID : int    │   │ - dateCreated : string   │         │ - shippingType : string  │\n  │ - quantity : int     │   │ - dateShipped : string   │         │ - shippingCost : int     │\n  │ - dateAdded : int    │   │ - customerName : string  │         │ - shippingRegionId : int │\n  ├──────────────────────┤   │   …                      │         ├──────────────────────────┤\n  │ + addCartItem()      │   └──────────────────────────┘         │ + updateShippingInfo()   │\n  │ + updateQuantity()   │                                        └──────────────────────────┘\n  │ + viewCartDetails()  │\n  │ + checkOut()         │\n  └──────────────────────┘\n```\n\nReading it:\n- Hollow triangle arrow (Customer ──▷ User) = **inheritance**: `class Customer extends User`. A second subclass (administrator: `-adminName`, `-email`) is cut off at the slide's right edge.\n- Filled diamond ◆ = **composition** (\"whole owns part\"): one Customer has 0..* shopping carts and 0..* orders; each Order has exactly 1 Shipping info.\n- Multiplicities: `1`, `0..*` (zero or more).\n- All data `-` (private), all methods `+` (public). The types `string`, `bool`, `date`, `float` are generic UML types; in Java they become `String`, `boolean`, a date class, `float`/`double`.\n\n## 3. Class diagram notation (slides 11–16)\n\nThree compartments: **name** (top), **data** (middle), **methods** (bottom).\n\n| Symbol | Access | Java |\n|---|---|---|\n| `+` | public | `public` |\n| `-` | private | `private` |\n| `#` | protected | `protected` |\n| `~` | package | (no modifier) |\n\n- Data: `visibility name : type` → `- side : double`\n- Method: `visibility name(parameter list) : returnType` → `+ move(double newX, double newY) : void`\n- A diagram need not be complete; omitted members are shown by an **ellipsis `...`** (slide 15).\n- Class diagrams mainly show **interactions** between classes (arrows for information flow, inheritance, packages). UML is **extensible** (slide 16).\n\nSlide 14 (Display 12.1) reproduced:\n\n```\n┌──────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ Square                                   │\n├──────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ - side : double                          │\n│ - xCoordinate : double                   │\n│ - yCoordinate : double                   │\n├──────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ + resize(double newSide) : void          │\n│ + move(double newX, double newY) : void  │\n│ # erase() : void                         │\n│ ...                                      │\n└──────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\nThe Java skeleton it describes (compiles):\n\n```java\npublic class Square {\n    private double side;\n    private double xCoordinate;\n    private double yCoordinate;\n    public void resize(double newSide) { side = newSide; }\n    public void move(double newX, double newY) { xCoordinate = newX; yCoordinate = newY; }\n    protected void erase() { /* ... */ }\n    // ... other members not shown in the diagram\n}\n```\n\nCommon arrows (beyond the slides, standard UML, useful for \"draw the UML\" questions):\n\n| Arrow | Meaning | Java |\n|---|---|---|\n| solid line, hollow triangle ──▷ | inheritance (is-a) | `extends` |\n| dashed line, hollow triangle ┄┄▷ | realisation | `implements` |\n| solid line ── (optional arrowhead) | association (has/uses) | field of the other type |\n| ◇── hollow diamond | aggregation (has-a, part can live alone) | field |\n| ◆── filled diamond | composition (owns, part dies with whole) | field created inside |\n\n## 4. Singleton pattern (slide 19 — instructor addition)\n\n- Restricts a class to **exactly one instance** and gives one global access point to it.\n- Uses: database connection, logger, configuration.\n- Implementation: **private constructor** + **private static** field holding the instance + **public static `getInstance()`**.\n- Drawbacks: harder to test, tight coupling, possible performance bottleneck.\n\n```java\nclass Logger {\n    private static Logger instance;          // the one instance (lazy)\n    private int lines = 0;\n    private Logger() { }                      // nobody outside can call new Logger()\n    public static Logger getInstance() {\n        if (instance == null)\n            instance = new Logger();\n        return instance;\n    }\n    public void log(String msg) { lines++; System.out.println(lines + \": \" + msg); }\n}\n// Logger a = Logger.getInstance(), b = Logger.getInstance();\n// a == b  →  true ;  new Logger()  →  compile error (constructor is private)\n```\n\nUML for it: `- instance : Logger` (underlined = static), `- Logger()`, `+ getInstance() : Logger` (underlined).\n\n## 5. Container–Iterator pattern (slide 20)\n\n- **Container**: an object that holds many data items — array, vector (ArrayList), linked list, even a String (holds characters).\n- **Iterator**: anything that cycles through all the items. For an array, the **index** is the iterator.\n- The pattern describes how an iterator is used on a container (Java's `Iterator` interface with `hasNext()` / `next()` comes in later chapters).\n\n## 6. Adaptor pattern (slide 21)\n\n- Turns one class into a different class **without changing the underlying class**, by adding a new interface on top.\n- Example from the slide: build a **stack** from an array — the array stays an array; the stack class exposes only `push`, `pop`, `peek`, `isEmpty`.\n- (Savitch spells it \"Adaptor\"; the usual design-pattern name is \"Adapter\". Same thing.)\n\n```java\nclass ArrayStack {                       // adapts an int[] to a stack interface\n    private int[] data = new int[10];\n    private int top = 0;\n    public void push(int x) { data[top++] = x; }\n    public int pop() { return data[--top]; }\n    public boolean isEmpty() { return top == 0; }\n}\n```\n\n## 7. Model–View–Controller (slides 22–26)\n\n- Separates the I/O of an application from its core.\n  - **Model**: the heart of the application (data + actions).\n  - **View**: output; displays a picture of the Model's state.\n  - **Controller**: input; relays the user's commands to the Model.\n- Each part is an object with its own responsibilities → a **divide-and-conquer** strategy.\n- Slide 24 example: Model = an array; View = shows one element; Controller = says which index to show; the Model **notifies** the View when the content or index changes.\n- Fits any application but is especially suited to **GUI** design.\n\nDisplay 12.4 (slide 26):\n\n```\n                ┌───────────┐\n                │ Model     │\n                │ data1     │\n                │ data2 ... │\n                │ action1() │\n                │ action2() │\n                └───────────┘\n        Notify ↙            ↖ Manipulate\n  ┌───────────┐              ┌────────────┐\n  │ View      │              │ Controller │\n  │ update()  │              │ ...        │\n  └───────────┘              └────────────┘\n```\n\nModel → View: notify (View calls/has `update()`); Controller → Model: manipulate.\n\n## 8. The divide-and-conquer sorting pattern (slides 27–30, 43)\n\n```java\npublic static void sort(Type[] a, int begin, int end) {\n    if ((end - begin) >= 1) {                // 2 or more elements\n        int splitPoint = split(a, begin, end);\n        sort(a, begin, splitPoint);\n        sort(a, splitPoint + 1, end);\n        join(a, begin, splitPoint, end);\n    } // else 0 or 1 element: already sorted\n}\n```\n\n- `split` rearranges a[begin..end] and returns the split point; `sort` is called recursively on both halves; `join` combines them.\n- The pattern does **not** say how split and join work. Different split/join → different algorithms (merge sort, quick sort).\n- **Restrictions** (slide 43): works only for types with `<`, and only sorts in increasing order. Generalise by replacing `<` with a boolean `compare(x, y)` method.\n- Trade-off (slide 31): you can make split simpler at the cost of a more complex join, or vice versa.\n\n## 9. Merge sort (slides 31–36)\n\n- **split** = trivial: `return (begin + end) / 2;` (no rearranging).\n- **join** = the real work: merge two sorted halves into a temp array by repeatedly taking the smaller front element, then copy leftovers, then copy temp back.\n- Slide 28 picture: [53 13 | 43 10] → [53 | 13], [43 | 10] → [13 53], [10 43] → [10 13 43 53] (3 joins).\n- On the 10-element demo array `{7.7, 5.5, 11, 3, 16, 4.4, 20, 14, 13, 42}`: first splitPoint = 4; **9 joins**, 19 calls to `sort`; result `3.0 4.4 5.5 7.7 11.0 13.0 14.0 16.0 20.0 42.0` (verified). Merging n elements down to one list always takes n − 1 joins.\n- Always splits into two roughly equal halves → O(n log n) every time.\n\n## 10. Quick sort (slides 37–42, 44–45)\n\n- **split** = the real work: choose a **splitting value** (the code uses `a[begin]`), put all elements `<=` it at the front, all `>` at the back, the splitting value between them; return its index.\n- **join** = does nothing (slide 45 highlights this).\n- After split the two parts are **not** sorted, but every element before the splitting value is ≤ every element after it (slide 39).\n- Trace (verified) of the first split on the demo array, splitValue = 7.7: smaller values go to the front left-to-right (5.5, 3, 4.4); larger values fill temp from the **back** (11 at the end, then 16, 20, 14, 13, 42). Result: `5.5 3.0 4.4 7.7 42.0 13.0 14.0 20.0 16.0 11.0`, splitPoint = 3.\n- Efficiency (slide 46): good when split gives two substantial chunks. Merge sort always halves; quick sort may not → its **worst case** (e.g. already sorted input with first-element pivot) is O(n²), slower than merge sort. Average O(n log n).\n- Pragmatics (slide 47): patterns are guides, not requirements. Calling an empty `join` costs a method call, so in practice it is removed.\n\n## 11. Errata\n\n| Slide | Slide says | Correct | How verified |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 40 (QuickSort.sort, also Savitch's code) | `sort(a, begin, splitPoint); sort(a, splitPoint + 1, end);` | The splitting value is already in its final place at `splitPoint`, so quick sort should recurse on `sort(a, begin, splitPoint - 1)`. With the slide's version, any interval whose values are all `<=` its first element and equal to it never shrinks: **infinite recursion → StackOverflowError** whenever the array contains a repeated value. The demo array works only because all its values are distinct. | Ran the slide code: `{5,5}` and `{3,1,3}` → StackOverflowError; with `splitPoint - 1` both sort correctly, and the demo array still sorts. |\n| 4 | \"Brady Booch\" | **Grady** Booch | Standard UML history (Booch, Jacobson, Rumbaugh) |\n| 5, 8 | sequence diagrams show interactions \"(aka 'swim lines')\" | The vertical lines in a sequence diagram are **lifelines**. \"Swimlanes\" are partitions in **activity** diagrams. | UML 2 specification terminology |\n| 38, 44 | picture: pivot = last element (16), then 19 | The slide code (slide 40) uses the **first** element `a[begin]` as the splitting value. The picture is a different variant of quick sort; do not trace the code with the picture's pivot. | Compare slide 38 with slide 40, line `double splitValue = a[begin];` |\n| 10 | types `string`, `bool`, `date` | Fine as UML, but not Java types. In Java write `String`, `boolean`. | Java language |\n| 44–45 | — | Duplicates of slides 38 and 42 (no new content). | Visual check |\n\n## Likely exam questions\n\n1. **Draw the UML class diagram** for:\n   ```java\n   public class BankAccount {\n       private String owner;\n       private double balance;\n       protected int pin;\n       public void deposit(double amount) { ... }\n       public double getBalance() { ... }\n       private boolean check(int p) { ... }\n   }\n   ```\n   **Answer:** `BankAccount` / `- owner : String`, `- balance : double`, `# pin : int` / `+ deposit(double amount) : void`, `+ getBalance() : double`, `- check(int p) : boolean`.\n2. **Write the Java class** from the Square diagram (slide 14). **Answer:** §3 skeleton; `erase` must be `protected`.\n3. **What do `+ - # ~` mean?** public, private, protected, package.\n4. **Name the five UML diagrams and what each shows.** (§2 table.)\n5. **Write a Singleton** class `Config` with `getInstance()`. **Answer:** private static field, private constructor, public static `getInstance()` that creates the object on first call (§4). Why private constructor? So no other class can call `new`.\n6. **What does this print?** `Logger a = Logger.getInstance(); Logger b = Logger.getInstance(); System.out.println(a == b);` **Answer:** `true`.\n7. **MVC:** which part handles input? output? data? **Answer:** Controller, View, Model.\n8. **Sorting pattern:** in merge sort which method is simple, which complex? In quick sort? **Answer:** merge: split simple, join complex; quick: split complex, join does nothing.\n9. **Trace:** first split of quick sort (slide code) on `{4, 1, 3, 2}`. **Answer:** splitValue 4; all others ≤ 4 go to the front: `1 3 2 4`, splitPoint = 3 (verified).\n10. **Find the bug** in slide 40's quick sort. **Answer:** recursion on `sort(a, begin, splitPoint)` includes the pivot; arrays with duplicates recurse forever. Use `splitPoint - 1`.\n11. **How many joins** does merge sort make on 8 elements? **Answer:** 7 (n − 1).\n12. **Why is quick sort's worst case slower than merge sort's?** Its split may give very unequal parts (e.g. 0 and n−1 elements), giving O(n²); merge sort always halves.\n13. **Adaptor:** how do you build a stack from an array without changing the array class? **Answer:** wrap it in a class that exposes only push/pop/peek/isEmpty.\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"What is UML?","a":"Unified Modeling Language: a graphical language for designing and documenting object-oriented software."},{"q":"What is a pattern in programming?","a":"A template or outline of a software task that can be realised as different code in different but similar applications."},{"q":"Who released UML, and when?","a":"Grady Booch, Ivar Jacobson and James Rumbaugh, in 1996."},{"q":"Which organisation maintains the UML standard today?","a":"The Object Management Group (OMG)."},{"q":"What does an activity diagram show?","a":"The activities (workflow) involved in a software process."},{"q":"What does a use case diagram show?","a":"The interactions between actors (users or external systems) and the software system."},{"q":"What does a sequence diagram show?","a":"The time-ordered messages between actors and system components, along vertical lifelines."},{"q":"What does a state diagram show?","a":"How the system's state changes in response to internal and external events."},{"q":"What does a class diagram show?","a":"The classes (name, data, methods) and the associations between them."},{"q":"What are the three compartments of a UML class box?","a":"Top: class name; middle: data (name : type); bottom: methods."},{"q":"UML access symbols + - # ~ mean?","a":"+ public, - private, # protected, ~ package."},{"q":"How is a method written in a UML class diagram?","a":"visibility name(parameter list) : returnType, e.g. + move(double newX, double newY) : void."},{"q":"How does a class diagram show omitted members?","a":"With an ellipsis (three dots)."},{"q":"In the Square diagram, what is # erase() : void in Java?","a":"protected void erase()."},{"q":"What does a hollow-triangle arrow from Customer to User mean?","a":"Inheritance: Customer extends User (Customer is a User)."},{"q":"What does a filled diamond mean in a class diagram?","a":"Composition: the whole owns the part (e.g. Order owns its Shipping info)."},{"q":"What does the multiplicity 0..* mean?","a":"Zero or more objects."},{"q":"What is the Singleton pattern?","a":"A pattern that restricts a class to one instance and gives a single global access point to it."},{"q":"How is a Singleton implemented?","a":"Private constructor, private static field for the instance, public static getInstance() that returns it."},{"q":"Two typical uses and two drawbacks of a Singleton?","a":"Uses: database connection, logger. Drawbacks: hard to test, tight coupling (also possible bottleneck)."},{"q":"What is a container? An iterator?","a":"Container: object holding many items (array, list, String). Iterator: anything that cycles through all the items (an array index)."},{"q":"What does the Adaptor pattern do?","a":"Turns one class into a different one by adding a new interface on top, without changing the underlying class (e.g. a stack built on an array)."},{"q":"What do Model, View and Controller do?","a":"Model: the core data and logic; View: output (shows the Model's state); Controller: input (relays user commands to the Model)."},{"q":"Which kind of application is MVC especially suited to?","a":"GUI (graphical user interface) applications."},{"q":"What are the three steps of the divide-and-conquer sorting pattern?","a":"split the array, recursively sort both parts, join the sorted parts."},{"q":"Two restrictions of the sorting pattern as given?","a":"Only types with < defined, and only increasing order; fix by using a boolean compare method."},{"q":"In merge sort, which of split/join is simple?","a":"split is simple (midpoint); join (merging with a temp array) is complex."},{"q":"In quick sort, which of split/join is simple?","a":"join does nothing; split (partition around the splitting value) does all the work."},{"q":"What value does the slide's quick sort use as the splitting value?","a":"The first element of the interval, a[begin]."},{"q":"After a quick sort split, are the two parts sorted?","a":"No, but every element before the splitting value is ≤ every element after it."},{"q":"Why is merge sort's worst case better than quick sort's?","a":"Merge sort always splits into equal halves (O(n log n)); quick sort's split can be very unequal (worst case O(n²))."},{"q":"How many joins does merge sort do on n elements?","a":"n − 1."},{"q":"What bug is in the slide's QuickSort.sort?","a":"It recurses on sort(a, begin, splitPoint), which includes the pivot; arrays with repeated values recurse forever (StackOverflowError). Use splitPoint - 1."},{"q":"\"Patterns are guides, not requirements\" — example from the slides?","a":"Quick sort's empty join call would be removed in practice to save the method-call overhead."}],"figs":[{"n":1,"src":"slides/W4s001.jpg"},{"n":2,"src":"slides/W4s002.jpg"},{"n":3,"src":"slides/W4s003.jpg"},{"n":4,"src":"slides/W4s004.jpg"},{"n":5,"src":"slides/W4s005.jpg"},{"n":6,"src":"slides/W4s006.jpg"},{"n":7,"src":"slides/W4s007.jpg"},{"n":8,"src":"slides/W4s008.jpg"},{"n":9,"src":"slides/W4s009.jpg"},{"n":10,"src":"slides/W4s010.jpg"},{"n":11,"src":"slides/W4s011.jpg"},{"n":12,"src":"slides/W4s012.jpg"},{"n":13,"src":"slides/W4s013.jpg"},{"n":14,"src":"slides/W4s014.jpg"},{"n":15,"src":"slides/W4s015.jpg"},{"n":16,"src":"slides/W4s016.jpg"},{"n":17,"src":"slides/W4s017.jpg"},{"n":18,"src":"slides/W4s018.jpg"},{"n":19,"src":"slides/W4s019.jpg"},{"n":20,"src":"slides/W4s020.jpg"},{"n":21,"src":"slides/W4s021.jpg"},{"n":22,"src":"slides/W4s022.jpg"},{"n":23,"src":"slides/W4s023.jpg"},{"n":24,"src":"slides/W4s024.jpg"},{"n":25,"src":"slides/W4s025.jpg"},{"n":26,"src":"slides/W4s026.jpg"},{"n":27,"src":"slides/W4s027.jpg"},{"n":28,"src":"slides/W4s028.jpg"},{"n":29,"src":"slides/W4s029.jpg"},{"n":30,"src":"slides/W4s030.jpg"},{"n":31,"src":"slides/W4s031.jpg"},{"n":32,"src":"slides/W4s032.jpg"},{"n":33,"src":"slides/W4s033.jpg"},{"n":34,"src":"slides/W4s034.jpg"},{"n":35,"src":"slides/W4s035.jpg"},{"n":36,"src":"slides/W4s036.jpg"},{"n":37,"src":"slides/W4s037.jpg"},{"n":38,"src":"slides/W4s038.jpg"},{"n":39,"src":"slides/W4s039.jpg"},{"n":40,"src":"slides/W4s040.jpg"},{"n":41,"src":"slides/W4s041.jpg"},{"n":42,"src":"slides/W4s042.jpg"},{"n":43,"src":"slides/W4s043.jpg"},{"n":44,"src":"slides/W4s044.jpg"},{"n":45,"src":"slides/W4s045.jpg"},{"n":46,"src":"slides/W4s046.jpg"},{"n":47,"src":"slides/W4s047.jpg"}],"exercises":[{"id":"e1","lang":"java","title":"Square from its UML class diagram","level":"warm","blurb":"Slide 14 (Display 12.1). Implement exactly this diagram. The test uses reflection to check the visibility of every member, so `-`, `+` and `#` matter.","prompt":"Slide 14 (Display 12.1). Implement exactly this diagram. The test uses reflection to check the visibility of every member, so `-`, `+` and `#` matter.\n\n```\n┌──────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ Square                                   │\n├──────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ - side : double                          │\n│ - xCoordinate : double                   │\n│ - yCoordinate : double                   │\n├──────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ + Square(double side, double x, double y)│\n│ + resize(double newSide) : void          │\n│ + move(double newX, double newY) : void  │\n│ # erase() : void                         │   sets side to 0\n│ + getSide() : double                     │\n│ + getArea() : double                     │\n│ + toString() : String                    │   \"Square 2.0 at (1.0, 3.0)\"\n└──────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```","starter":"class Square {\n    public double side;          // TODO: check the diagram\n    public double xCoordinate;\n    public double yCoordinate;\n\n    public Square(double side, double x, double y) { }\n\n    public void resize(double newSide) { }\n    public void move(double newX, double newY) { }\n    public void erase() { }\n    public double getSide() { return 0; }\n    public double getArea() { return 0; }\n    public String toString() { return \"\"; }\n}","test":"import java.lang.reflect.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static void check(boolean ok, String msg) { if (!ok) throw new AssertionError(msg); }\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n        for (String f : new String[]{\"side\", \"xCoordinate\", \"yCoordinate\"}) {\n            Field fl = Square.class.getDeclaredField(f);\n            check(Modifier.isPrivate(fl.getModifiers()), f + \" should be private (-)\");\n            check(fl.getType() == double.class, f + \" should be a double\");\n        }\n        check(Modifier.isProtected(Square.class.getDeclaredMethod(\"erase\").getModifiers()), \"erase() should be protected (#)\");\n        check(Modifier.isPublic(Square.class.getDeclaredMethod(\"move\", double.class, double.class).getModifiers()), \"move should be public (+)\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS visibility\");\n        Square s = new Square(2, 1, 3);\n        check(s.toString().equals(\"Square 2.0 at (1.0, 3.0)\"), \"toString wrong: \\\"\" + s + \"\\\"\");\n        s.resize(5); s.move(-1, 0);\n        check(s.getSide() == 5 && Math.abs(s.getArea() - 25) < 1e-9, \"resize/getArea wrong\");\n        check(s.toString().equals(\"Square 5.0 at (-1.0, 0.0)\"), \"move wrong: \\\"\" + s + \"\\\"\");\n        s.erase();\n        check(s.getSide() == 0, \"erase() should set side to 0\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS behaviour\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Square {\n    private double side;\n    private double xCoordinate;\n    private double yCoordinate;\n\n    public Square(double side, double x, double y) {\n        this.side = side;\n        xCoordinate = x;\n        yCoordinate = y;\n    }\n\n    public void resize(double newSide) { side = newSide; }\n\n    public void move(double newX, double newY) {\n        xCoordinate = newX;\n        yCoordinate = newY;\n    }\n\n    protected void erase() { side = 0; }\n\n    public double getSide() { return side; }\n\n    public double getArea() { return side * side; }\n\n    public String toString() {\n        return \"Square \" + side + \" at (\" + xCoordinate + \", \" + yCoordinate + \")\";\n    }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e2","lang":"java","title":"Singleton registry","level":"warm","blurb":"Slide 19 (Singleton). Write class `Registry` that can have **only one instance**:","prompt":"Slide 19 (Singleton). Write class `Registry` that can have **only one instance**:\n- a **private** constructor,\n- `public static Registry getInstance()` — creates the instance on the first call, returns the same object afterwards,\n- `int register(String name)` — stores the name and returns its id (1, 2, 3, … across the whole program),\n- `int size()` — how many names are registered.","starter":"class Registry {\n    private int count = 0;\n\n    public Registry() { }                      // TODO: should not be callable from outside\n\n    public static Registry getInstance() {\n        return new Registry();                 // TODO: always returns a new object\n    }\n\n    public int register(String name) {\n        count++;\n        return count;\n    }\n\n    public int size() { return count; }\n}","test":"import java.lang.reflect.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static void check(boolean ok, String msg) { if (!ok) throw new AssertionError(msg); }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        for (Constructor<?> c : Registry.class.getDeclaredConstructors())\n            check(Modifier.isPrivate(c.getModifiers()), \"every constructor of a Singleton must be private\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS private constructor\");\n        Registry a = Registry.getInstance();\n        Registry b = Registry.getInstance();\n        check(a != null && a == b, \"getInstance() must return the same object every time\");\n        check(a.register(\"logger\") == 1, \"first id should be 1\");\n        check(Registry.getInstance().register(\"db\") == 2, \"ids must continue across getInstance() calls\");\n        check(b.size() == 2, \"size() should be 2, got \" + b.size());\n        System.out.println(\"PASS single instance\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Registry {\n    private static Registry instance;      // the one and only object\n    private int count = 0;\n\n    private Registry() { }                 // no \"new Registry()\" outside this class\n\n    public static Registry getInstance() {\n        if (instance == null)\n            instance = new Registry();     // created lazily on first use\n        return instance;\n    }\n\n    public int register(String name) {\n        count++;\n        return count;\n    }\n\n    public int size() { return count; }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e3","lang":"java","title":"ShoppingCart from the slide-10 class diagram","level":"core","blurb":"Slide 10 (online shop). Implement the \"Shopping cart\" class with arrays (at most 10 different products):","prompt":"Slide 10 (online shop). Implement the \"Shopping cart\" class with arrays (at most 10 different products):\n\n```\n┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ ShoppingCart                                         │\n├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ - cartId : int                                       │\n│ - productIds : int[]          (capacity 10)          │\n│ - quantities : int[]                                 │\n│ - used : int                  (distinct products)    │\n├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ + ShoppingCart(int cartId)                           │\n│ + addCartItem(int productId, int qty) : void         │  adds to the existing quantity if present\n│ + updateQuantity(int productId, int qty) : void      │  qty 0 removes the product\n│ + getQuantity(int productId) : int                   │  0 if absent\n│ + itemCount() : int                                  │  number of distinct products\n│ + checkOut() : int                                   │  returns total units, empties the cart\n└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```","starter":"class ShoppingCart {\n    private int cartId;\n    private int[] productIds = new int[10];\n    private int[] quantities = new int[10];\n    private int used = 0;\n\n    public ShoppingCart(int cartId) { this.cartId = cartId; }\n\n    public void addCartItem(int productId, int qty) { }          // TODO\n    public void updateQuantity(int productId, int qty) { }       // TODO\n    public int getQuantity(int productId) { return 0; }          // TODO\n    public int itemCount() { return used; }\n    public int checkOut() { return 0; }                          // TODO\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    static void check(boolean ok, String msg) { if (!ok) throw new AssertionError(msg); }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        ShoppingCart c = new ShoppingCart(7);\n        c.addCartItem(101, 2);\n        c.addCartItem(202, 1);\n        c.addCartItem(101, 3);\n        check(c.getQuantity(101) == 5, \"101 should have 5 units, got \" + c.getQuantity(101));\n        check(c.itemCount() == 2, \"2 distinct products expected, got \" + c.itemCount());\n        check(c.getQuantity(999) == 0, \"absent product should have quantity 0\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS addCartItem\");\n        c.updateQuantity(202, 4);\n        check(c.getQuantity(202) == 4, \"updateQuantity should set 202 to 4\");\n        c.updateQuantity(101, 0);\n        check(c.getQuantity(101) == 0 && c.itemCount() == 1, \"quantity 0 should remove the product\");\n        c.addCartItem(303, 1);\n        check(c.getQuantity(202) == 4 && c.getQuantity(303) == 1, \"removing must not disturb other products\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS updateQuantity\");\n        check(c.checkOut() == 5, \"checkOut should return 4 + 1 = 5 units\");\n        check(c.itemCount() == 0 && c.getQuantity(202) == 0, \"checkOut should empty the cart\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS checkOut\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class ShoppingCart {\n    private int cartId;\n    private int[] productIds = new int[10];\n    private int[] quantities = new int[10];\n    private int used = 0;\n\n    public ShoppingCart(int cartId) { this.cartId = cartId; }\n\n    private int indexOf(int productId) {\n        for (int i = 0; i < used; i++)\n            if (productIds[i] == productId) return i;\n        return -1;\n    }\n\n    public void addCartItem(int productId, int qty) {\n        int i = indexOf(productId);\n        if (i >= 0) {\n            quantities[i] += qty;\n        } else {\n            productIds[used] = productId;\n            quantities[used] = qty;\n            used++;\n        }\n    }\n\n    public void updateQuantity(int productId, int qty) {\n        int i = indexOf(productId);\n        if (i < 0) return;\n        if (qty > 0) {\n            quantities[i] = qty;\n        } else {                           // remove: move the last entry into the gap\n            used--;\n            productIds[i] = productIds[used];\n            quantities[i] = quantities[used];\n        }\n    }\n\n    public int getQuantity(int productId) {\n        int i = indexOf(productId);\n        return i < 0 ? 0 : quantities[i];\n    }\n\n    public int itemCount() { return used; }\n\n    public int checkOut() {\n        int total = 0;\n        for (int i = 0; i < used; i++) total += quantities[i];\n        used = 0;\n        return total;\n    }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e4","lang":"java","title":"Adaptor: a stack built on an array","level":"core","blurb":"Slide 21 (Adaptor pattern: \"start with an array, then add the stack interface\"). Write `IntStack` that wraps an `int[]` (start with capacity 2) and exposes only","prompt":"Slide 21 (Adaptor pattern: \"start with an array, then add the stack interface\"). Write `IntStack` that wraps an `int[]` (start with capacity 2) and exposes only:\n- `void push(int x)` — when the array is full, replace it with one twice as big (copy the old values),\n- `int pop()` and `int peek()` — throw `IllegalStateException` when empty,\n- `boolean isEmpty()`, `int size()`.","starter":"class IntStack {\n    private int[] data = new int[2];\n    private int size = 0;\n\n    public void push(int x) { data[size] = x; size++; }   // TODO: crashes when full\n    public int pop() { return 0; }                         // TODO\n    public int peek() { return 0; }                        // TODO\n    public boolean isEmpty() { return true; }              // TODO\n    public int size() { return size; }\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    static void check(boolean ok, String msg) { if (!ok) throw new AssertionError(msg); }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        IntStack s = new IntStack();\n        check(s.isEmpty() && s.size() == 0, \"new stack should be empty\");\n        try {\n            for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) s.push(i * 10);\n        } catch (ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException e) {\n            throw new AssertionError(\"push must grow the array when it is full\");\n        }\n        check(s.size() == 5 && !s.isEmpty(), \"size should be 5\");\n        check(s.peek() == 50, \"peek should be 50\");\n        check(s.pop() == 50 && s.pop() == 40, \"pop order should be LIFO: 50 then 40\");\n        check(s.size() == 3, \"size should be 3 after two pops\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS push/pop/peek\");\n        s.pop(); s.pop(); s.pop();\n        boolean threw = false;\n        try { s.pop(); } catch (IllegalStateException e) { threw = true; }\n        check(threw, \"pop on an empty stack should throw IllegalStateException\");\n        threw = false;\n        try { s.peek(); } catch (IllegalStateException e) { threw = true; }\n        check(threw, \"peek on an empty stack should throw IllegalStateException\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS empty stack\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class IntStack {\n    private int[] data = new int[2];\n    private int size = 0;\n\n    public void push(int x) {\n        if (size == data.length) {\n            int[] bigger = new int[data.length * 2];\n            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) bigger[i] = data[i];\n            data = bigger;\n        }\n        data[size] = x;\n        size++;\n    }\n\n    public int pop() {\n        if (size == 0) throw new IllegalStateException(\"empty stack\");\n        size--;\n        return data[size];\n    }\n\n    public int peek() {\n        if (size == 0) throw new IllegalStateException(\"empty stack\");\n        return data[size - 1];\n    }\n\n    public boolean isEmpty() { return size == 0; }\n\n    public int size() { return size; }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e5","lang":"java","title":"Merge sort: write join","level":"core","blurb":"Slides 31–35. The sorting pattern is given. Write `join(int[] a, int begin, int splitPoint, int end)` which merges the sorted intervals `a[begin..splitPoint]` a","prompt":"Slides 31–35. The sorting pattern is given. Write `join(int[] a, int begin, int splitPoint, int end)` which merges the sorted intervals `a[begin..splitPoint]` and `a[splitPoint+1..end]` using a temporary array, exactly as on slides 34–35. Also count how many times `join` runs in the static variable `joins`.","starter":"class MergeSort {\n    static int joins = 0;\n\n    public static void sort(int[] a, int begin, int end) {\n        if ((end - begin) >= 1) {\n            int splitPoint = split(a, begin, end);\n            sort(a, begin, splitPoint);\n            sort(a, splitPoint + 1, end);\n            join(a, begin, splitPoint, end);\n        }\n    }\n\n    private static int split(int[] a, int begin, int end) {\n        return (begin + end) / 2;\n    }\n\n    private static void join(int[] a, int begin, int splitPoint, int end) {\n        // TODO\n    }\n}","test":"import java.util.Arrays;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static void check(boolean ok, String msg) { if (!ok) throw new AssertionError(msg); }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[] a = {53, 13, 43, 10};\n        MergeSort.joins = 0;\n        MergeSort.sort(a, 0, a.length - 1);\n        check(Arrays.equals(a, new int[]{10, 13, 43, 53}), \"slide 28 example: got \" + Arrays.toString(a));\n        check(MergeSort.joins == 3, \"4 elements need 3 joins, counted \" + MergeSort.joins);\n        System.out.println(\"PASS slide example\");\n        int[] b = {7, 5, 11, 3, 16, 4, 20, 14, 13, 42, 5, 3};\n        MergeSort.joins = 0;\n        MergeSort.sort(b, 0, b.length - 1);\n        check(Arrays.equals(b, new int[]{3, 3, 4, 5, 5, 7, 11, 13, 14, 16, 20, 42}), \"got \" + Arrays.toString(b));\n        check(MergeSort.joins == 11, \"n elements need n - 1 joins\");\n        int[] c = {9, 1, 8, 2};\n        MergeSort.sort(c, 1, 2);\n        check(Arrays.equals(c, new int[]{9, 1, 8, 2}), \"sorting a[1..2] must only touch that interval\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS duplicates and sub-intervals\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class MergeSort {\n    static int joins = 0;\n\n    public static void sort(int[] a, int begin, int end) {\n        if ((end - begin) >= 1) {\n            int splitPoint = split(a, begin, end);\n            sort(a, begin, splitPoint);\n            sort(a, splitPoint + 1, end);\n            join(a, begin, splitPoint, end);\n        }\n    }\n\n    private static int split(int[] a, int begin, int end) {\n        return (begin + end) / 2;\n    }\n\n    private static void join(int[] a, int begin, int splitPoint, int end) {\n        joins++;\n        int intervalSize = end - begin + 1;\n        int[] temp = new int[intervalSize];\n        int nextLeft = begin, nextRight = splitPoint + 1, i = 0;\n        while (nextLeft <= splitPoint && nextRight <= end) {\n            if (a[nextLeft] < a[nextRight]) temp[i++] = a[nextLeft++];\n            else temp[i++] = a[nextRight++];\n        }\n        while (nextLeft <= splitPoint) temp[i++] = a[nextLeft++];   // rest of left chunk\n        while (nextRight <= end) temp[i++] = a[nextRight++];        // rest of right chunk\n        for (i = 0; i < intervalSize; i++)\n            a[begin + i] = temp[i];\n    }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e6","lang":"java","title":"Fix the slide's quick sort","level":"hard","blurb":"Slides 40–42 (see the Errata in `04-uml-and-patterns.md`). The starter is the slide code. It sorts the demo array but **recurses forever** on arrays with repeat","prompt":"Slides 40–42 (see the Errata in `04-uml-and-patterns.md`). The starter is the slide code. It sorts the demo array but **recurses forever** on arrays with repeated values. Fix `sort` so that it works for every input. Keep `split` as on the slides (splitting value = `a[begin]`); the test also checks the first split of the demo array.","starter":"class QuickSort {\n    public static void sort(double[] a, int begin, int end) {\n        if ((end - begin) >= 1) {\n            int splitPoint = split(a, begin, end);\n            sort(a, begin, splitPoint);\n            sort(a, splitPoint + 1, end);\n            join(a, begin, splitPoint, end);\n        }\n    }\n\n    static int split(double[] a, int begin, int end) {\n        double[] temp;\n        int size = (end - begin + 1);\n        temp = new double[size];\n        double splitValue = a[begin];\n        int up = 0;\n        int down = size - 1;\n        for (int i = begin + 1; i <= end; i++) {\n            if (a[i] <= splitValue) { temp[up] = a[i]; up++; }\n            else { temp[down] = a[i]; down--; }\n        }\n        temp[up] = a[begin];\n        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++)\n            a[begin + i] = temp[i];\n        return (begin + up);\n    }\n\n    private static void join(double[] a, int begin, int splitPoint, int end) {\n        // Nothing to do.\n    }\n}","test":"import java.util.Arrays;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static void check(boolean ok, String msg) { if (!ok) throw new AssertionError(msg); }\n    static void sorts(double[] in) {\n        double[] want = in.clone(); Arrays.sort(want);\n        double[] got = in.clone();\n        try {\n            QuickSort.sort(got, 0, got.length - 1);\n        } catch (StackOverflowError e) {\n            throw new AssertionError(\"infinite recursion on \" + Arrays.toString(in) + \" (pivot included in a recursive call?)\");\n        }\n        check(Arrays.equals(got, want), \"wrong result for \" + Arrays.toString(in) + \": \" + Arrays.toString(got));\n    }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        double[] demo = {7.7, 5.5, 11, 3, 16, 4.4, 20, 14, 13, 42};\n        double[] d = demo.clone();\n        int sp = QuickSort.split(d, 0, d.length - 1);\n        check(sp == 3 && Arrays.equals(d, new double[]{5.5, 3, 4.4, 7.7, 42, 13, 14, 20, 16, 11}), \"first split changed: \" + sp + \" \" + Arrays.toString(d));\n        sorts(demo);\n        System.out.println(\"PASS demo array\");\n        sorts(new double[]{5, 5});\n        sorts(new double[]{3, 1, 3});\n        sorts(new double[]{2, 2, 2, 1, 2});\n        sorts(new double[]{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6});\n        sorts(new double[]{9});\n        System.out.println(\"PASS duplicates and edge cases\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class QuickSort {\n    public static void sort(double[] a, int begin, int end) {\n        if ((end - begin) >= 1) {\n            int splitPoint = split(a, begin, end);\n            sort(a, begin, splitPoint - 1);    // the splitting value is already in place:\n            sort(a, splitPoint + 1, end);      // leave it out of both recursive calls\n            join(a, begin, splitPoint, end);\n        }\n    }\n\n    static int split(double[] a, int begin, int end) {\n        double[] temp;\n        int size = (end - begin + 1);\n        temp = new double[size];\n        double splitValue = a[begin];\n        int up = 0;\n        int down = size - 1;\n        for (int i = begin + 1; i <= end; i++) {\n            if (a[i] <= splitValue) { temp[up] = a[i]; up++; }\n            else { temp[down] = a[i]; down--; }\n        }\n        temp[up] = a[begin];\n        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++)\n            a[begin + i] = temp[i];\n        return (begin + up);\n    }\n\n    private static void join(double[] a, int begin, int splitPoint, int end) {\n        // Nothing to do.\n    }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e7","lang":"java","title":"Generalised sorting pattern with a compare method","level":"hard","blurb":"Slide 43: \"the `<` operator can be replaced with a boolean valued method called compare\". Write a merge sort for `String[]` that takes the ordering as an object","prompt":"Slide 43: \"the `<` operator can be replaced with a boolean valued method called compare\". Write a merge sort for `String[]` that takes the ordering as an object:\n\n```java\ninterface Order { boolean before(String x, String y); }   // true if x must come before y\n```\nWrite `static void sort(String[] a, Order order)` in class `StringSorter`. It must be **stable**: when neither string comes before the other, keep their original order (take from the left half on ties). The test sorts alphabetically, by length, and in reverse.","starter":"interface Order { boolean before(String x, String y); }\n\nclass StringSorter {\n    static void sort(String[] a, Order order) {\n        // TODO: split / sort / join, using order.before(x, y) instead of <\n    }\n}","test":"import java.util.Arrays;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static void check(boolean ok, String msg) { if (!ok) throw new AssertionError(msg); }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        String[] w = {\"pear\", \"fig\", \"banana\", \"kiwi\", \"apple\", \"date\"};\n        String[] a = w.clone();\n        StringSorter.sort(a, (x, y) -> x.compareTo(y) < 0);\n        check(Arrays.equals(a, new String[]{\"apple\", \"banana\", \"date\", \"fig\", \"kiwi\", \"pear\"}), \"alphabetical: \" + Arrays.toString(a));\n        System.out.println(\"PASS alphabetical\");\n        String[] b = w.clone();\n        StringSorter.sort(b, (x, y) -> x.length() < y.length());\n        check(Arrays.equals(b, new String[]{\"fig\", \"pear\", \"kiwi\", \"date\", \"apple\", \"banana\"}), \"by length, stable: \" + Arrays.toString(b));\n        System.out.println(\"PASS by length (stable)\");\n        String[] c = w.clone();\n        StringSorter.sort(c, (x, y) -> x.compareTo(y) > 0);\n        check(Arrays.equals(c, new String[]{\"pear\", \"kiwi\", \"fig\", \"date\", \"banana\", \"apple\"}), \"reverse: \" + Arrays.toString(c));\n        String[] e = {};\n        StringSorter.sort(e, (x, y) -> x.compareTo(y) < 0);\n        System.out.println(\"PASS reverse and empty\");\n    }\n}","solution":"interface Order { boolean before(String x, String y); }\n\nclass StringSorter {\n    static void sort(String[] a, Order order) {\n        sort(a, 0, a.length - 1, order);\n    }\n\n    private static void sort(String[] a, int begin, int end, Order order) {\n        if (end - begin >= 1) {\n            int splitPoint = (begin + end) / 2;\n            sort(a, begin, splitPoint, order);\n            sort(a, splitPoint + 1, end, order);\n            join(a, begin, splitPoint, end, order);\n        }\n    }\n\n    private static void join(String[] a, int begin, int splitPoint, int end, Order order) {\n        String[] temp = new String[end - begin + 1];\n        int l = begin, r = splitPoint + 1, i = 0;\n        while (l <= splitPoint && r <= end) {\n            if (order.before(a[r], a[l])) temp[i++] = a[r++];   // right only if strictly before\n            else temp[i++] = a[l++];                            // ties: left first (stable)\n        }\n        while (l <= splitPoint) temp[i++] = a[l++];\n        while (r <= end) temp[i++] = a[r++];\n        for (i = 0; i < temp.length; i++) a[begin + i] = temp[i];\n    }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e8","lang":"java","title":"Model–View–Controller counter","level":"core","blurb":"Slides 22–26. Three classes with one job each:","prompt":"Slides 22–26. Three classes with one job each:\n- `CounterModel` — holds `int value`; `setView(CounterView v)`; `increment()`, `decrement()`, `reset()`; `getValue()`. **After every change it notifies the view** by calling `view.update(value)` (if a view is set).\n- `CounterView` — `update(int v)` stores `\"Count: \" + v` as the last shown text; `getShown()` returns it (initially `\"\"`); `getUpdates()` = how many times `update` was called.\n- `CounterController` — constructor takes the model; `handle(String cmd)` maps `\"+\"` → increment, `\"-\"` → decrement, `\"reset\"` → reset, and ignores anything else.","starter":"class CounterModel {\n    private int value = 0;\n    private CounterView view;\n    public void setView(CounterView v) { view = v; }\n    public void increment() { value++; }        // TODO: notify the view\n    public void decrement() { value--; }        // TODO\n    public void reset() { value = 0; }          // TODO\n    public int getValue() { return value; }\n}\n\nclass CounterView {\n    private String shown = \"\";\n    private int updates = 0;\n    public void update(int v) { }               // TODO\n    public String getShown() { return shown; }\n    public int getUpdates() { return updates; }\n}\n\nclass CounterController {\n    private CounterModel model;\n    public CounterController(CounterModel model) { this.model = model; }\n    public void handle(String cmd) { }          // TODO\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    static void check(boolean ok, String msg) { if (!ok) throw new AssertionError(msg); }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        CounterModel m = new CounterModel();\n        CounterView v = new CounterView();\n        m.setView(v);\n        CounterController c = new CounterController(m);\n        c.handle(\"+\"); c.handle(\"+\"); c.handle(\"+\"); c.handle(\"-\");\n        check(m.getValue() == 2, \"model value should be 2, got \" + m.getValue());\n        check(v.getShown().equals(\"Count: 2\"), \"view should show \\\"Count: 2\\\", got \\\"\" + v.getShown() + \"\\\"\");\n        check(v.getUpdates() == 4, \"view should be notified once per change (4), got \" + v.getUpdates());\n        System.out.println(\"PASS controller -> model -> view\");\n        c.handle(\"jump\");\n        check(v.getUpdates() == 4 && m.getValue() == 2, \"unknown commands must be ignored\");\n        c.handle(\"reset\");\n        check(m.getValue() == 0 && v.getShown().equals(\"Count: 0\") && v.getUpdates() == 5, \"reset should notify with 0\");\n        CounterModel lonely = new CounterModel();\n        lonely.increment();\n        check(lonely.getValue() == 1, \"a model without a view must still work\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS reset and no-view\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class CounterModel {\n    private int value = 0;\n    private CounterView view;\n\n    public void setView(CounterView v) { view = v; }\n\n    private void changed() {\n        if (view != null) view.update(value);   // Model notifies View\n    }\n\n    public void increment() { value++; changed(); }\n    public void decrement() { value--; changed(); }\n    public void reset() { value = 0; changed(); }\n    public int getValue() { return value; }\n}\n\nclass CounterView {\n    private String shown = \"\";\n    private int updates = 0;\n\n    public void update(int v) {\n        shown = \"Count: \" + v;\n        updates++;\n    }\n\n    public String getShown() { return shown; }\n    public int getUpdates() { return updates; }\n}\n\nclass CounterController {\n    private CounterModel model;\n\n    public CounterController(CounterModel model) { this.model = model; }\n\n    public void handle(String cmd) {             // Controller turns input into Model actions\n        if (cmd.equals(\"+\")) model.increment();\n        else if (cmd.equals(\"-\")) model.decrement();\n        else if (cmd.equals(\"reset\")) model.reset();\n    }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."}]},{"id":"05","title":"W5 · Copy Constructors","slides":"Week 5 · 33 slides","md":"# 05 — Week 5 · Copy Constructors, Privacy Leaks, Deep vs Shallow Copy (Savitch ch. 5, 33 slides)\n\n_Ingested 15.11.2026 from lectures/Week5_SavichJava_CopyConstr.pptx (PDF copy rendered and read slide by slide; slides 4–8 and 12–15 are image-only Pearson displays and were transcribed from the images). The deck is titled \"Chapter 5 — Copy Constructor\" and mixes Savitch Section 5.3 (class parameters, Displays 5.14–5.17) with the **instructor's own material**: the `Person`/`Address`/\"Moved Person\" examples (slides 20–32), the \"Multiple Developer\" story (slide 11), an in-class question (slide 25) and a HOME STUDY task (slide 29). Six slide errors were found and checked with JDK 26 — see Section 9._\n\n> **Instructor items (Dr. Oladejo) — high priority**\n> - **In-class question, slide 25:** \"What is a copy-constructor?\" — answer given on the slide (Section 4).\n> - **HOME STUDY, slide 29:** \"Review the code for the `movedperson` package\" (`Address` + `MovedPerson` classes, demoed on slide 31). The package code is not in the deck; Section 6 rebuilds it from slides 29–32.\n> - **DEMOs, slides 10 and 31:** `ToyClass.java` + `ClassParameterDemo.java`; `Address` + `MovedPerson`.\n> - **\"Multiple Developer\" issue, slide 11:** his framing of why class parameters are dangerous.\n> - His own definition of a privacy leak (slide 18) — learn it word for word.\n\n## 1. Java is always call-by-value (slides 2–3, 9)\n\n- **Every** parameter in Java is call-by-value. A parameter is a local variable set to the **value** of the argument.\n- For a primitive (`int`, `double`, `boolean`…) the value is the number itself. The method **cannot** change the caller's variable.\n- For a class type the value is a **reference** (memory address). The parameter becomes **another name for the same object**.\n  - Changes to the object's **instance variables** through the parameter ARE seen by the caller.\n  - Assigning a **new object** to the parameter (`p = new X()`) is NOT seen by the caller — the address itself was copied.\n- So class parameters only *look like* call-by-reference (C++ `&`). They are not.\n\n| | Primitive parameter | Class-type parameter |\n|---|---|---|\n| What is copied | the value | the reference (address) |\n| Can the method change caller's variable? | No | No (cannot make it point elsewhere) |\n| Can the method change caller's object? | n/a | **Yes** (via setters or direct field access) |\n\n## 2. Display 5.14 — `ToyClass.changer` (slides 4–8, 10)\n\n```java\npublic class ToyClass {\n    private String name;\n    private int number;\n    public ToyClass(String initialName, int initialNumber) { name = initialName; number = initialNumber; }\n    public ToyClass() { name = \"No name yet.\"; number = 0; }\n    public void set(String newName, int newNumber) { name = newName; number = newNumber; }\n    public String toString() { return (name + \" \" + number); }\n    public static void changer(ToyClass aParameter) {\n        aParameter.name = \"Hot Shot\";       // allowed: same class, so private fields are visible\n        aParameter.number = 42;\n    }\n    public boolean equals(ToyClass otherObject) {\n        return ((name.equals(otherObject.name)) && (number == otherObject.number));  // slide shows \"=\" — see Errata\n    }\n}\n// ClassParameterDemo.main\nToyClass anObject = new ToyClass(\"Mr. Cellophane\", 0);\nSystem.out.println(anObject);\nSystem.out.println(\"Now we call changer with anObject as argument.\");\nToyClass.changer(anObject);\nSystem.out.println(anObject);\n```\nOutput (verified):\n```\nMr. Cellophane 0\nNow we call changer with anObject as argument.\nHot Shot 42\n```\n**Memory picture (slides 6–8, Display 5.15):** `anObject` holds address 3078. On the call, 3078 is copied into `aParameter`. Both names point to the one object at 3078. `changer` writes \"Hot Shot\"/42 into that object, so `anObject` sees the change.\n\n**\"Multiple Developer\" issue (slide 11, instructor):** Developer 1 builds `anObject` (\"Mr. Cellophane\", 0). Developer 2 writes `ToyClass.changer()` — not a setter — and silently changes Developer 1's object to (\"Hot Shot\", 42). Lesson: once a method gets a reference, it can change your object behind your back.\n\n## 3. Display 5.16/5.17 — class type vs primitive argument (slides 12–15)\n\n```java\npublic void makeEqual(ToyClass2 anObject) { anObject.name = this.name; anObject.number = this.number; }\npublic void tryToMakeEqual(int aNumber) { aNumber = this.number; }   // has no effect on the caller\n```\n```java\nobject1.set(\"Scorpius\", 1);  object2.set(\"John Crichton\", 2);\nobject1.makeEqual(object2);          // object2 becomes \"Scorpius 1\"   — class-type argument CAN change\nint aNumber = 42;\nobject1.tryToMakeEqual(aNumber);     // aNumber stays 42               — primitive argument CANNOT change\n```\nVerified output: `John Crichton 2` → `Scorpius 1`; `42` → `42`.\n\n## 4. `=` and `==` with objects; privacy leaks (slides 16–18, 25)\n\n- `b = a;` with objects copies the **reference**. Now two variables name **one** object (aliasing). No new object is made.\n- `a == b` is true only if both hold the **same address**. Two different objects with equal fields give `false`. Use `equals` to compare contents.\n- `private` is enough for primitive fields (and immutable ones like `String`). For a **mutable class-type** field, `private` alone is **not** enough.\n- **Privacy leak (his definition, slide 18):** a class exposes a direct reference to a mutable internal object, so outside code can change the private state without the class knowing — it breaks encapsulation. It happens when the live reference is **passed in** (constructor/setter stores the caller's object) or **returned** (getter returns the field).\n- Cure: copy constructors (defensive copies) on the way **in** and on the way **out**.\n\n**Slide 25 in-class question — \"What is a copy-constructor?\"**\n> A constructor that creates a separate, independent object from an existing object, with the instance variables set so that it is an exact copy of the argument object, but with a **new memory address**.\n\n## 5. Copy constructor (slides 19–24)\n\n- A constructor with **one parameter of the same class type**. It builds a new, independent object that is an exact copy.\n- Java has no built-in copy constructor (C++ does). It is the \"copy constructor **idiom**\": you write it yourself.\n\n```java\nclass Person {\n    private String name;\n    private int age;\n    public Person(String name, int age) { this.name = name; this.age = age; }\n    public Person(Person other) {                       // copy constructor (slide 22)\n        if (other == null) throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"other person is null\");\n        this.name = other.name;    // sharing a String is safe: String is immutable\n        this.age = other.age;\n    }\n    @Override\n    public String toString() { return \"Person{name='\" + name + \"', age=\" + age + \"}\"; }\n}\n```\n- Note: `this.name = other.name` is fine **because String is immutable**. For a mutable field you must write `this.addr = new Address(other.addr)`.\n- `toString` (slide 23) is **overridden** from `Object` with `@Override` so `println(p)` prints all fields.\n\nSlide 24 usage:\n```java\nPerson original = new Person(\"Emina\", 22);\nPerson alias = original;              // NOT a copy at all — same object (slide calls it \"shallow copy\", see Errata)\nPerson copy  = new Person(original);  // new independent object\nSystem.out.println(alias == original); // true\nSystem.out.println(copy == original);  // false\n```\n\n## 6. Mutable vs immutable; the Moved Person / Address example (slides 26–32)\n\n- **Immutable class:** no methods (other than constructors) change the object's data. Example: `String`. Returning a reference to an immutable object is **safe** — so `return name;` in `getName()` is NOT a leak (slide 26).\n- **Mutable class:** has public mutators (setters) or other methods that change data. A custom class with setters is mutable.\n- Rule (slide 28): **never return a reference to a mutable private field.** Return a copy made with the copy constructor.\n\nRebuilt `movedperson` example (HOME STUDY, slide 29) — compiles and runs on JDK 26:\n```java\nclass Address {\n    private String street, city, postalCode, country;\n    public Address(String street, String city, String postalCode, String country) {\n        this.street = street; this.city = city; this.postalCode = postalCode; this.country = country;\n    }\n    public Address(Address other) {                    // copy constructor (slide 30, corrected)\n        if (other == null) throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"other address is null\");\n        this.street = other.street; this.city = other.city;\n        this.postalCode = other.postalCode; this.country = other.country;\n    }\n    public void setCity(String city) { this.city = city; }   // setter => Address is MUTABLE\n    public String getCity() { return city; }\n}\nclass MovedPerson {\n    private final String name;\n    private final Address originalAddress;   // final reference, but the Address object is still mutable!\n    private Address movedTo;\n    public MovedPerson(String name, Address original, Address movedTo) {\n        this.name = name;\n        this.originalAddress = new Address(original);   // defensive copy IN\n        this.movedTo = new Address(movedTo);\n    }\n    public MovedPerson(MovedPerson other) {            // deep copy\n        this(other.name, other.originalAddress, other.movedTo);\n    }\n    public Address getMovedToAddress() { return new Address(movedTo); }   // defensive copy OUT (slide 32)\n}\n```\nSlide 32 contrast:\n```java\npublic Address getMovedToAddress() { return movedTo; }               // LEAK: caller gets the live object\npublic Address getMovedToAddress() { return new Address(movedTo); }  // correct: defensive copy\n```\n\n## 7. Deep copy vs shallow copy (slide 33)\n\n- **Deep copy:** has **no references in common** with the original — except references to **immutable** objects (e.g. `String`), which may be shared.\n- **Shallow copy:** any copy that is not deep. A new outer object whose mutable fields still point to the original's inner objects. Causes privacy leaks.\n- **Alias (`b = a`):** not a copy at all — zero new objects.\n\n| Code | New outer object? | Inner `Address` shared? | Name |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `Person b = a;` | no | yes (same object) | alias / reference copy |\n| `this.addr = other.addr;` in copy ctor | yes | **yes** | shallow copy |\n| `this.addr = new Address(other.addr);` | yes | no | deep copy |\n\n## 8. Gotchas checklist\n\n- `final` on a reference field means the **reference** cannot be reassigned. The **object** can still change through its setters. `final` ≠ immutable.\n- The copy constructor itself must copy mutable fields with **their** copy constructors, or it is only shallow.\n- A null check that only prints (slide 30) still crashes with `NullPointerException` on the next line. Throw instead.\n- Inside a class you can access `other.privateField` of another object **of the same class** — that is how copy constructors and `changer` work.\n- An `equals(ToyClass other)` with a class-type parameter **overloads** `Object.equals(Object)`; it does not override it (Week 6, slide 62).\n- `clone()` is not covered on these slides; the copy constructor is the course's tool.\n\n## 9. Errata (all verified with JDK 26.0.2)\n\n| Slide | Slide says | Correct | How verified |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 5 (Display 5.11, line 32) | `&& (number = otherObject.number)` | `==`. With `=` it does not compile: \"bad operand types for binary operator '&&' — boolean, int\". | javac |\n| 11 | changer sets the name to \"Mr. Hot Shot\" | The code sets `\"Hot Shot\"`; output is `Hot Shot 42`. | ran Display 5.14 |\n| 20 | \"the private instance variables are all the primitive data types (int and String)\" | `String` is **not** primitive; it is a class. Sharing it is safe only because String is **immutable**. | Java language rule |\n| 23 | `toString` prints `\", address=\" + address` | Person on slides 20–22 has no `address` field; this line compiles only in the MovedPerson version. | javac (cannot find symbol) |\n| 24 | `Person copy = original;` is \"make a shallow copy\" | That is **aliasing**, not a copy: no new object, `copy == original` is true. A shallow copy is a *new* object sharing inner mutable objects (slide 33). Also this example is in `main`, not a \"mutator or accessor\" as the slide's heading says. | ran it |\n| 29 | OriginalAddress \"is IMMUTABLE, because we marked the variable final\" | `final` freezes the **reference**, not the object. An `Address` with setters is still mutable through a `final` reference (verified: `f.setStreet(\"Y\")` on a `final Address f` works). That is exactly why the defensive copy is still needed. | ran it |\n| 30 | `// Copy constructor public Address(Address other) {` | The signature is inside the `//` comment, so the class does not compile (\"statements not expected outside of methods\"). Put the signature on its own line. Also the null check only prints, then `other.street` throws `NullPointerException`; throw `IllegalArgumentException` instead (as slide 22 does). | javac + ran it |\n\nMinor: slide 1 says \"Chapter 5\" (the material is Savitch §5.3, plus instructor additions).\n\n## Likely exam questions\n\n1. **What is printed?**\n   ```java\n   ToyClass t = new ToyClass(\"A\", 1);\n   ToyClass u = t;\n   u.set(\"B\", 2);\n   System.out.println(t + \" \" + (t == u));\n   ```\n   **Answer:** `B 2 true` — `u = t` is an alias.\n\n2. **What is printed?**\n   ```java\n   static void reset(int n) { n = 0; }\n   static void reset(ToyClass t) { t = new ToyClass(\"Z\", 0); }\n   ...\n   int k = 5; ToyClass t = new ToyClass(\"A\", 1);\n   reset(k); reset(t);\n   System.out.println(k + \" \" + t);\n   ```\n   **Answer:** `5 A 1` — reassigning a parameter never affects the caller.\n\n3. **Find the bug (privacy leak):**\n   ```java\n   class Course { private Date start; public Date getStart() { return start; } }\n   ```\n   **Answer:** `Date` is mutable; caller can `getStart().setTime(...)`. Fix: `return new Date(start.getTime());` (or a copy constructor).\n\n4. **Write** a copy constructor for `class Car { private String model; private Engine engine; }` where `Engine` has a copy constructor.\n   **Answer:** `public Car(Car o) { if (o == null) throw new IllegalArgumentException(); model = o.model; engine = new Engine(o.engine); }`\n\n5. **Shallow or deep?** `public Team(Team o) { players = o.players; }` with `players` an array. **Answer:** shallow — both teams share one array. Deep: copy the array and each mutable element.\n\n6. **Why is `return name;` safe but `return address;` not?** **Answer:** `String` is immutable; `Address` has setters (mutable).\n\n7. **Theory:** Is Java call-by-reference for objects? **Answer:** No. Always call-by-value; for objects the value copied is the reference.\n\n8. **Does `final Address home;` make `home` immutable?** **Answer:** No. Only the reference is fixed; `home.setCity(...)` still works.\n\n9. **What does `==` compare for objects? What does a proper `equals` compare?** **Answer:** addresses; field values.\n\n10. **Slide 25 question:** define a copy constructor (see Section 4).\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"What parameter-passing mechanism does Java use for all parameters?","a":"Call-by-value — the parameter is a local variable initialised with the argument's value."},{"q":"What value is passed for a class-type parameter?","a":"The reference (memory address) of the object, so the parameter names the same object as the argument."},{"q":"Can a method change the caller's object through a class-type parameter?","a":"Yes — changes to the object's instance variables are seen by the caller."},{"q":"Can a method make the caller's variable point to a new object by assigning to its parameter?","a":"No — only the local copy of the address changes."},{"q":"Can a method change a primitive argument's value in the caller?","a":"No — it gets a copy of the value."},{"q":"After `ToyClass.changer(anObject)` on (\"Mr. Cellophane\", 0), what does `println(anObject)` print?","a":"`Hot Shot 42`."},{"q":"After `object1.tryToMakeEqual(aNumber)` with aNumber = 42, what is aNumber?","a":"Still 42 — a primitive argument cannot change."},{"q":"What does `b = a;` do when a and b are object variables?","a":"Copies the reference; both variables now name the same object (aliasing). No new object is created."},{"q":"What does `==` test for two object variables?","a":"Whether they hold the same memory address (same object), not whether the fields are equal."},{"q":"What is a privacy leak (Dr. Oladejo's slide 18 definition)?","a":"A class exposes a direct reference to a mutable internal object, letting outside code change its private state without its knowledge — it breaks encapsulation."},{"q":"Name the two ways a live reference to a private mutable field escapes.","a":"It is passed in (stored from an argument) or returned from a method (e.g. a getter)."},{"q":"Is `private` enough to protect a mutable class-type instance variable?","a":"No — you also need defensive copies on the way in and out."},{"q":"What is a copy constructor?","a":"A constructor with one parameter of the same class that creates a separate, independent object that is an exact copy of the argument, at a new memory address."},{"q":"Does Java have a built-in copy constructor like C++?","a":"No — it is a coding idiom; you write it yourself."},{"q":"Why may a copy constructor copy a `String` field with `this.name = other.name`?","a":"String is immutable, so sharing the reference is safe."},{"q":"How must a copy constructor copy a mutable `Address` field?","a":"`this.address = new Address(other.address);` — using Address's copy constructor."},{"q":"What should a copy constructor do if its argument is null?","a":"Throw an exception (slide 22: `IllegalArgumentException`) — printing a message and continuing causes a NullPointerException."},{"q":"What is an immutable class?","a":"A class with no methods (other than constructors) that change its objects' data, e.g. String."},{"q":"What is a mutable class?","a":"A class with public mutators or other public methods that change its objects' data."},{"q":"Is it safe for a getter to return a reference to an immutable object?","a":"Yes — the object cannot be changed."},{"q":"What should a getter for a mutable field return?","a":"A copy, e.g. `return new Address(movedTo);`."},{"q":"Define a deep copy.","a":"A copy with no references in common with the original, except references to immutable objects."},{"q":"Define a shallow copy.","a":"Any copy that is not deep — a new object that still shares mutable inner objects with the original."},{"q":"Is `Person copy = original;` a shallow copy?","a":"No — it is not a copy at all; it is an alias (copy == original is true). The slide's label is wrong."},{"q":"Does declaring a field `final Address home` make the Address immutable?","a":"No — final fixes the reference; the object can still change via its setters."},{"q":"Why does `ToyClass.changer` compile even though name and number are private?","a":"It is inside ToyClass, and private means private to the class, not to the object."},{"q":"What does slide 5's `(number = otherObject.number)` inside `&&` cause?","a":"A compile error (boolean && int); it must be `==`."},{"q":"What is wrong with `// Copy constructor public Address(Address other) {` on slide 30?","a":"The signature is commented out, so the code does not compile."},{"q":"What is the instructor's HOME STUDY task in week 5?","a":"Review the code of the movedperson package (Address + MovedPerson, slide 29)."},{"q":"Why must `toString` be overridden in Person?","a":"Object's default prints ClassName@hashcode; overriding prints the fields, e.g. `Person{name='Emina', age=22}`."}],"figs":[{"n":1,"src":"slides/W5s001.jpg"},{"n":2,"src":"slides/W5s002.jpg"},{"n":3,"src":"slides/W5s003.jpg"},{"n":4,"src":"slides/W5s004.jpg"},{"n":5,"src":"slides/W5s005.jpg"},{"n":6,"src":"slides/W5s006.jpg"},{"n":7,"src":"slides/W5s007.jpg"},{"n":8,"src":"slides/W5s008.jpg"},{"n":9,"src":"slides/W5s009.jpg"},{"n":10,"src":"slides/W5s010.jpg"},{"n":11,"src":"slides/W5s011.jpg"},{"n":12,"src":"slides/W5s012.jpg"},{"n":13,"src":"slides/W5s013.jpg"},{"n":14,"src":"slides/W5s014.jpg"},{"n":15,"src":"slides/W5s015.jpg"},{"n":16,"src":"slides/W5s016.jpg"},{"n":17,"src":"slides/W5s017.jpg"},{"n":18,"src":"slides/W5s018.jpg"},{"n":19,"src":"slides/W5s019.jpg"},{"n":20,"src":"slides/W5s020.jpg"},{"n":21,"src":"slides/W5s021.jpg"},{"n":22,"src":"slides/W5s022.jpg"},{"n":23,"src":"slides/W5s023.jpg"},{"n":24,"src":"slides/W5s024.jpg"},{"n":25,"src":"slides/W5s025.jpg"},{"n":26,"src":"slides/W5s026.jpg"},{"n":27,"src":"slides/W5s027.jpg"},{"n":28,"src":"slides/W5s028.jpg"},{"n":29,"src":"slides/W5s029.jpg"},{"n":30,"src":"slides/W5s030.jpg"},{"n":31,"src":"slides/W5s031.jpg"},{"n":32,"src":"slides/W5s032.jpg"},{"n":33,"src":"slides/W5s033.jpg"}],"exercises":[{"id":"e1","lang":"java","title":"Point copy constructor","level":"warm","blurb":"Write the copy constructor `Point(Point other)` so it builds a **new** object with the same `x` and `y`.","prompt":"Write the copy constructor `Point(Point other)` so it builds a **new** object with the same `x` and `y`.\nNotes: `05-copy-constructors.md` §5.","starter":"class Point {\n    private int x, y;\n    public Point(int x, int y) { this.x = x; this.y = y; }\n    public Point(Point other) {\n        // TODO: copy the fields of other\n    }\n    public int getX() { return x; }\n    public int getY() { return y; }\n    public void move(int dx, int dy) { x += dx; y += dy; }\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Point p = new Point(3, 4);\n        Point q = new Point(p);\n        if (q == p) throw new AssertionError(\"copy must be a different object\");\n        if (q.getX() != 3 || q.getY() != 4) throw new AssertionError(\"copy should be (3,4) but is (\" + q.getX() + \",\" + q.getY() + \")\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS fields copied\");\n        q.move(1, 1);\n        if (p.getX() != 3) throw new AssertionError(\"moving the copy changed the original\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS copy is independent\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Point {\n    private int x, y;\n    public Point(int x, int y) { this.x = x; this.y = y; }\n    public Point(Point other) {\n        this.x = other.x;\n        this.y = other.y;\n    }\n    public int getX() { return x; }\n    public int getY() { return y; }\n    public void move(int dx, int dy) { x += dx; y += dy; }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e2","lang":"java","title":"Safe Person copy constructor","level":"warm","blurb":"Complete `Person(Person other)` exactly like slide 22: if `other` is `null`, throw `IllegalArgumentException(\"other person is null\")`; otherwise copy `name` and","prompt":"Complete `Person(Person other)` exactly like slide 22: if `other` is `null`, throw `IllegalArgumentException(\"other person is null\")`; otherwise copy `name` and `age`. Also override `toString()` to return `Person{name='Emina', age=22}` format.","starter":"class Person {\n    private String name;\n    private int age;\n    public Person(String name, int age) { this.name = name; this.age = age; }\n    public Person(Person other) {\n        this.name = other.name;\n        this.age = other.age;\n    }\n    // TODO: override toString\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Person original = new Person(\"Emina\", 22);\n        Person copy = new Person(original);\n        if (copy == original) throw new AssertionError(\"copy must be a new object\");\n        if (!copy.toString().equals(\"Person{name='Emina', age=22}\"))\n            throw new AssertionError(\"toString gave: \" + copy);\n        System.out.println(\"PASS copy + toString\");\n        boolean thrown = false;\n        try { new Person((Person) null); }\n        catch (IllegalArgumentException e) { thrown = true; }\n        catch (NullPointerException e) { throw new AssertionError(\"got NullPointerException: check for null first and throw IllegalArgumentException\"); }\n        if (!thrown) throw new AssertionError(\"null argument must throw IllegalArgumentException\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS null rejected\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Person {\n    private String name;\n    private int age;\n    public Person(String name, int age) { this.name = name; this.age = age; }\n    public Person(Person other) {\n        if (other == null) throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"other person is null\");\n        this.name = other.name;   // String is immutable: sharing is safe\n        this.age = other.age;\n    }\n    @Override\n    public String toString() { return \"Person{name='\" + name + \"', age=\" + age + \"}\"; }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e3","lang":"java","title":"Deep copy with an Address field","level":"core","blurb":"`Address` is **mutable** (it has `setCity`). Write both copy constructors so that `new Person(p)` is a **deep** copy: changing the copy's address must not chang","prompt":"`Address` is **mutable** (it has `setCity`). Write both copy constructors so that `new Person(p)` is a **deep** copy: changing the copy's address must not change the original's.","starter":"class Address {\n    private String street, city;\n    public Address(String street, String city) { this.street = street; this.city = city; }\n    public Address(Address other) {\n        // TODO\n    }\n    public String getCity() { return city; }\n    public void setCity(String city) { this.city = city; }\n}\nclass Person {\n    private String name;\n    private Address address;\n    public Person(String name, Address address) { this.name = name; this.address = new Address(address); }\n    public Person(Person other) {\n        this.name = other.name;\n        this.address = other.address;   // shallow!\n    }\n    public Address getAddressDirect() { return address; }   // for testing only\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Address a = new Address(\"Hrasnicka cesta 15\", \"Sarajevo\");\n        Address b = new Address(a);\n        if (b == a || !\"Sarajevo\".equals(b.getCity())) throw new AssertionError(\"Address copy constructor must copy street and city into a new object\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS Address copy\");\n        Person p = new Person(\"Emina\", a);\n        Person q = new Person(p);\n        if (q.getAddressDirect() == p.getAddressDirect()) throw new AssertionError(\"the copy shares the Address object: shallow copy\");\n        q.getAddressDirect().setCity(\"Mostar\");\n        if (!\"Sarajevo\".equals(p.getAddressDirect().getCity())) throw new AssertionError(\"changing the copy's city changed the original\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS deep copy\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Address {\n    private String street, city;\n    public Address(String street, String city) { this.street = street; this.city = city; }\n    public Address(Address other) {\n        this.street = other.street;\n        this.city = other.city;\n    }\n    public String getCity() { return city; }\n    public void setCity(String city) { this.city = city; }\n}\nclass Person {\n    private String name;\n    private Address address;\n    public Person(String name, Address address) { this.name = name; this.address = new Address(address); }\n    public Person(Person other) {\n        this.name = other.name;\n        this.address = new Address(other.address);   // deep\n    }\n    public Address getAddressDirect() { return address; }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e4","lang":"java","title":"Plug the privacy leaks","level":"core","blurb":"`Student` leaks its private `Address` three ways: the constructor stores the caller's object, `getAddress()` returns the live field, and `setAddress()` stores t","prompt":"`Student` leaks its private `Address` three ways: the constructor stores the caller's object, `getAddress()` returns the live field, and `setAddress()` stores the caller's object. Fix all three with defensive copies (`new Address(...)`). The `Address` class is correct.","starter":"class Address {\n    private String city;\n    public Address(String city) { this.city = city; }\n    public Address(Address other) { this.city = other.city; }\n    public String getCity() { return city; }\n    public void setCity(String city) { this.city = city; }\n}\nclass Student {\n    private String name;\n    private Address address;\n    public Student(String name, Address address) { this.name = name; this.address = address; }\n    public Address getAddress() { return address; }\n    public void setAddress(Address address) { this.address = address; }\n    public String getCity() { return address.getCity(); }\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Address home = new Address(\"Sarajevo\");\n        Student s = new Student(\"the student\", home);\n        home.setCity(\"Tuzla\");\n        if (!\"Sarajevo\".equals(s.getCity())) throw new AssertionError(\"constructor leak: caller changed the student's city to \" + s.getCity());\n        System.out.println(\"PASS constructor copies\");\n        s.getAddress().setCity(\"Zenica\");\n        if (!\"Sarajevo\".equals(s.getCity())) throw new AssertionError(\"getter leak: returned the live Address\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS getter copies\");\n        Address next = new Address(\"Mostar\");\n        s.setAddress(next);\n        next.setCity(\"Bihac\");\n        if (!\"Mostar\".equals(s.getCity())) throw new AssertionError(\"setter leak: city is now \" + s.getCity());\n        System.out.println(\"PASS setter copies\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Address {\n    private String city;\n    public Address(String city) { this.city = city; }\n    public Address(Address other) { this.city = other.city; }\n    public String getCity() { return city; }\n    public void setCity(String city) { this.city = city; }\n}\nclass Student {\n    private String name;\n    private Address address;\n    public Student(String name, Address address) { this.name = name; this.address = new Address(address); }\n    public Address getAddress() { return new Address(address); }\n    public void setAddress(Address address) { this.address = new Address(address); }\n    public String getCity() { return address.getCity(); }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e5","lang":"java","title":"Swap that really swaps","level":"core","blurb":"Java is call-by-value, so a method cannot swap two **variables** of the caller. But it can swap the **contents** of two objects. Write `static void swapContents","prompt":"Java is call-by-value, so a method cannot swap two **variables** of the caller. But it can swap the **contents** of two objects. Write `static void swapContents(Box a, Box b)` so that after the call `a` holds b's old value and `b` holds a's old value. (The starter swaps the parameters — it has no effect on the caller.)","starter":"class Box {\n    int value;\n    Box(int value) { this.value = value; }\n    static void swapContents(Box a, Box b) {\n        Box tmp = a;\n        a = b;\n        b = tmp;\n    }\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Box x = new Box(1), y = new Box(2);\n        Box keepX = x;\n        Box.swapContents(x, y);\n        if (x != keepX) throw new AssertionError(\"impossible: the caller's variable changed\");\n        if (x.value != 2 || y.value != 1) throw new AssertionError(\"expected x=2, y=1 but got x=\" + x.value + \", y=\" + y.value + \" (swapping parameters does not affect the caller)\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS contents swapped\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Box {\n    int value;\n    Box(int value) { this.value = value; }\n    static void swapContents(Box a, Box b) {\n        int tmp = a.value;\n        a.value = b.value;\n        b.value = tmp;\n    }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e6","lang":"java","title":"Playlist with an array of mutable Songs","level":"hard","blurb":"`Playlist` holds a `Song[]`. Write:","prompt":"`Playlist` holds a `Song[]`. Write:\n- `Playlist(Playlist other)` — a **deep** copy: new array **and** a new `Song` for each element (use `Song`'s copy constructor).\n- `Song[] getSongs()` — returns a deep copy, so callers cannot change the playlist.\n- `Song(Song other)` copy constructor.","starter":"class Song {\n    private String title;\n    private int plays;\n    public Song(String title) { this.title = title; }\n    public Song(Song other) { /* TODO */ }\n    public String getTitle() { return title; }\n    public int getPlays() { return plays; }\n    public void play() { plays++; }\n}\nclass Playlist {\n    private Song[] songs;\n    public Playlist(Song[] songs) {\n        this.songs = new Song[songs.length];\n        for (int i = 0; i < songs.length; i++) this.songs[i] = new Song(songs[i]);\n    }\n    public Playlist(Playlist other) { this.songs = other.songs; }\n    public Song[] getSongs() { return songs; }\n    public int totalPlays() { int t = 0; for (Song s : songs) t += s.getPlays(); return t; }\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Song a = new Song(\"A\"); a.play();\n        Song b = new Song(\"B\");\n        Song c = new Song(a);\n        if (c == a || !\"A\".equals(c.getTitle()) || c.getPlays() != 1) throw new AssertionError(\"Song copy constructor must copy title and plays\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS Song copy\");\n        Playlist p = new Playlist(new Song[]{a, b});\n        Playlist q = new Playlist(p);\n        q.getSongs()[0].play();\n        Song[] view = p.getSongs();\n        view[1] = new Song(\"Hacked\");\n        view[0].play();\n        if (p.totalPlays() != 1) throw new AssertionError(\"original playlist was changed through a getter or a copy: totalPlays=\" + p.totalPlays());\n        if (!\"B\".equals(p.getSongs()[1].getTitle())) throw new AssertionError(\"array element replaced through getter\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS getter is a deep copy\");\n        Playlist r = new Playlist(p);\n        if (r.getSongs()[0].getPlays() != 1) throw new AssertionError(\"copy lost data\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS copy constructor is deep\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Song {\n    private String title;\n    private int plays;\n    public Song(String title) { this.title = title; }\n    public Song(Song other) { this.title = other.title; this.plays = other.plays; }\n    public String getTitle() { return title; }\n    public int getPlays() { return plays; }\n    public void play() { plays++; }\n}\nclass Playlist {\n    private Song[] songs;\n    public Playlist(Song[] songs) {\n        this.songs = new Song[songs.length];\n        for (int i = 0; i < songs.length; i++) this.songs[i] = new Song(songs[i]);\n    }\n    public Playlist(Playlist other) { this(other.songs); }   // reuses the deep-copying constructor\n    public Song[] getSongs() {\n        Song[] copy = new Song[songs.length];\n        for (int i = 0; i < songs.length; i++) copy[i] = new Song(songs[i]);\n        return copy;\n    }\n    public int totalPlays() { int t = 0; for (Song s : songs) t += s.getPlays(); return t; }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e7","lang":"java","title":"MovedPerson (the HOME STUDY package)","level":"hard","blurb":"Rebuild Dr. Oladejo's `movedperson` example (slides 29–32). `MovedPerson` has a `final Address originalAddress` and a changeable `Address movedTo`. Requirements","prompt":"Rebuild Dr. Oladejo's `movedperson` example (slides 29–32). `MovedPerson` has a `final Address originalAddress` and a changeable `Address movedTo`. Requirements:\n- the constructor stores **copies** of both addresses;\n- `MovedPerson(MovedPerson other)` is a deep copy;\n- `getOriginalAddress()` and `getMovedToAddress()` return copies;\n- `moveTo(Address a)` stores a copy of `a` in `movedTo`.\nRemember: `final` does **not** make the Address object immutable.","starter":"class Address {\n    private String city;\n    public Address(String city) { this.city = city; }\n    public Address(Address other) { this.city = other.city; }\n    public String getCity() { return city; }\n    public void setCity(String city) { this.city = city; }\n}\nclass MovedPerson {\n    private final String name;\n    private final Address originalAddress;\n    private Address movedTo;\n    public MovedPerson(String name, Address original, Address movedTo) {\n        this.name = name; this.originalAddress = original; this.movedTo = movedTo;\n    }\n    public MovedPerson(MovedPerson other) {\n        this.name = other.name; this.originalAddress = other.originalAddress; this.movedTo = other.movedTo;\n    }\n    public Address getOriginalAddress() { return originalAddress; }\n    public Address getMovedToAddress() { return movedTo; }\n    public void moveTo(Address a) { movedTo = a; }\n    public String getName() { return name; }\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Address orig = new Address(\"Sarajevo\"), to = new Address(\"Mostar\");\n        MovedPerson p = new MovedPerson(\"Emina\", orig, to);\n        orig.setCity(\"X\"); to.setCity(\"Y\");\n        if (!\"Sarajevo\".equals(p.getOriginalAddress().getCity()) || !\"Mostar\".equals(p.getMovedToAddress().getCity()))\n            throw new AssertionError(\"constructor must store copies\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS constructor\");\n        p.getOriginalAddress().setCity(\"Hacked\");\n        p.getMovedToAddress().setCity(\"Hacked\");\n        if (!\"Sarajevo\".equals(p.getOriginalAddress().getCity())) throw new AssertionError(\"final field leaked through getter: final != immutable\");\n        if (!\"Mostar\".equals(p.getMovedToAddress().getCity())) throw new AssertionError(\"movedTo leaked through getter\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS getters\");\n        MovedPerson q = new MovedPerson(p);\n        q.moveTo(new Address(\"Tuzla\"));\n        if (!\"Mostar\".equals(p.getMovedToAddress().getCity())) throw new AssertionError(\"copy shares state with original\");\n        Address z = new Address(\"Zenica\");\n        p.moveTo(z); z.setCity(\"Bihac\");\n        if (!\"Zenica\".equals(p.getMovedToAddress().getCity())) throw new AssertionError(\"moveTo must store a copy\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS copy constructor + moveTo\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Address {\n    private String city;\n    public Address(String city) { this.city = city; }\n    public Address(Address other) { this.city = other.city; }\n    public String getCity() { return city; }\n    public void setCity(String city) { this.city = city; }\n}\nclass MovedPerson {\n    private final String name;\n    private final Address originalAddress;\n    private Address movedTo;\n    public MovedPerson(String name, Address original, Address movedTo) {\n        this.name = name;\n        this.originalAddress = new Address(original);\n        this.movedTo = new Address(movedTo);\n    }\n    public MovedPerson(MovedPerson other) {\n        this(other.name, other.originalAddress, other.movedTo);   // the 3-arg constructor copies\n    }\n    public Address getOriginalAddress() { return new Address(originalAddress); }\n    public Address getMovedToAddress() { return new Address(movedTo); }\n    public void moveTo(Address a) { movedTo = new Address(a); }\n    public String getName() { return name; }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."}]},{"id":"06","title":"W6 · Inheritance (ch. 7)","slides":"Week 6 · 70 slides","md":"# 06 — Week 6 · Inheritance (Savitch ch. 7, 70 slides)\n\n_Ingested 15.11.2026 from lectures/Week6_SavichJava_chap7_Inheritance_ko.pptx (PDF copy; slides 2, 6, 10, 25, 33–36, 38, 52, 66 rendered and read from the images). Pearson deck for Savitch ch. 7 \"updated by Dr. Kazeem Oladejo, October 2025\". His additions: the **Course Assessment slide (Graded Lab One)**, the **Device / DoorDevice / ThermostatDevice** home-automation example (slides 6–11, 25, 30) replacing Savitch's Employee code in the first half, the \"Q/Ans\" in-class questions (slides 13, 14, 22, 65) and the \"Next Class\" slide (Quiz 1). Slides 21/29 and 25/30 are duplicates. Errors found and checked with JDK 26 are in Section 12._\n\n> **Instructor items (Dr. Oladejo) — high priority**\n> - **Graded Lab One, this week (slide 2): 5 points = 4 practical + 1 theory.** Lab topic: **Inheritance I** (Inheritance II comes after the midterm, as a bridge to polymorphism and abstract classes).\n> - **Next class (slide 70): Exception handling + debugging (hours 1–2), and QUIZ 1 in hour 2.**\n> - In-class Q&As on the slides (likely quiz/exam material — learn the answers word for word):\n>   - Slide 13: *Why can't an ancestor be used wherever a descendant can?* — **Because the derived class may have specifications that are not in the parent class.**\n>   - Slide 14: *What is a method signature?* — **The method's name plus its parameter list. Return type and access modifiers are NOT included.**\n>   - Slide 22: *Why can't a derived-class instance variable be an argument to `super`?* — **Order of initialization: the superclass constructor must run before the subclass's own instance variables are initialized or ready.**\n>   - Slide 65: *Why does `instanceof` return true for a descendant?* — **Because the descendant is a type of the ancestor.**\n> - DEMO \"Device Inheritance\" (slide 11) — the Device code below is his.\n\n## 1. Vocabulary (slides 3–5, 28)\n\n- **Inheritance:** create a new class from an existing one. The new class gets all instance variables and methods, and can add more (code reuse without copying).\n- Synonyms: **base = super = parent = ancestor** class; **derived = sub = child = descendant** class.\n- Syntax: `class DoorDevice extends Device { ... }`. Java allows **one** direct parent (single inheritance).\n- Pitfall (slide 28): the **superclass** is *more general*, more inclusive, less complex. The **subclass** is *more specialized*, less inclusive, more complex. Don't swap them.\n\n## 2. The Device example (slides 6–11, instructor's own)\n\nUML (slide 6): `Device {model, serialNumber, status()}` ← IS-A ← `DoorDevice {status(), openClose()}` and `ThermostatDevice {status(), setTemp(int t)}`.\n\n```java\nclass Device {\n    protected String model;\n    protected String serialNumber;\n    public Device(String model, String serialNumber) { this.model = model; this.serialNumber = serialNumber; }\n    public String status() { return \"Generic device status.\"; }\n}\nclass DoorDevice extends Device {\n    private boolean isOpen;\n    public DoorDevice(String model, String serialNumber) {\n        super(model, serialNumber);        // parent part first\n        this.isOpen = false;\n    }\n    @Override\n    public String status() { return isOpen ? \"Door is open.\" : \"Door is closed.\"; }\n    public void openClose() { isOpen = !isOpen; System.out.println(\"Door toggled. \" + status()); }\n}\nclass ThermostatDevice extends Device {\n    private int temperature;\n    public ThermostatDevice(String model, String serialNumber) { this(model, serialNumber, 20); }  // default 20\n    public ThermostatDevice(String model, String serialNumber, int temp) {\n        super(model, serialNumber);        // first construct the parent, then the child\n        this.temperature = temp;\n    }\n    @Override\n    public String status() { return \"Current temperature is \" + temperature + \" C.\"; }\n    public void setTemp(int t) { temperature = t; }   // shown in the UML (slide 6); slide 10 cuts off with \"…\"\n}\n```\nVerified run:\n```java\nDevice d = new DoorDevice(\"D1\", \"S1\");\nSystem.out.println(d.status());                 // Door is closed.   (the object's own version runs)\nDoorDevice door = new DoorDevice(\"D2\", \"S2\");\ndoor.openClose();                               // Door toggled. Door is open.\nSystem.out.println(new ThermostatDevice(\"T\", \"S3\").status());  // Current temperature is 20 C.\n// d.openClose();   // COMPILE ERROR: the variable's type Device has no openClose()\n```\n\n## 3. What a derived class inherits (slides 12, 41–42, 46–48, 56)\n\n- All public/protected instance variables and methods; it can **add** new ones and **override** inherited ones. Unchanged methods are **not** rewritten in the child (slide 12).\n- **Private** instance variables are inherited (they exist inside the object) but **cannot be accessed by name** in the derived class — use the parent's public getters/setters (slide 46). Reason (slide 47): otherwise anyone could break privacy just by writing a subclass.\n- **Private methods are effectively not inherited** (slide 48): the child cannot call them; they can only run indirectly through a public parent method that calls them.\n- **Constructors are not inherited** — the child calls them with `super(...)`.\n- **Static variables are inherited** (slide 56); access modifiers mean the same thing for them.\n\n## 4. Overriding (slides 14–19)\n\n- **Override** = same **signature** (name + parameter list) in the derived class. Put `@Override` above it; the compiler then checks it really overrides.\n- **Signature** = name + parameter types. **Not** the return type, **not** the access modifier (slide 14).\n- **Return type** must stay the same — except a class type may become a **descendant** class: **covariant return type** (Java 5.0+, slides 15–16):\n  ```java\n  class BaseClass    { public Employee       getSomeone(int someKey) { ... } }\n  class DerivedClass extends BaseClass { public HourlyEmployee getSomeone(int someKey) { ... } }  // OK\n  ```\n- **Access** may become **more permissive**, never more restrictive (slides 17–18): `public` in base → `private` in derived is a compile error (\"attempting to assign weaker access privileges\").\n  Order: `private` < package (no modifier) < `protected` < `public`.\n- **Overriding vs overloading (slide 19):**\n\n| | Overriding | Overloading |\n|---|---|---|\n| Parameters | exactly the same number and types | different parameter list |\n| Where | derived class redefines an inherited method | same class or derived class |\n| Original method | replaced for objects of the derived class | **still inherited** and callable |\n| Example | `status()` in DoorDevice | `equals(Employee)` next to inherited `equals(Object)` |\n\n## 5. `final` (slide 20)\n\n- `final` **method** → cannot be overridden in a derived class.\n- `final` **class** → cannot be extended (e.g. `String`).\n- (Week 5 reminder: `final` **variable** → cannot be reassigned.)\n\n## 6. Constructors: `super(...)` and `this(...)` (slides 21–25, 29–30)\n\n```java\npublic DerivedClass(int p1, int p2, double p3) {\n    super(p1, p2);          // call the base-class constructor\n    instanceVariable = p3;\n}\n```\nRules:\n1. Call the parent constructor with **`super`**, never with the parent's class name.\n2. `super(...)` must be the **first action** in the constructor (see Errata/JDK note).\n3. A derived-class instance variable **cannot** be an argument to `super` (slide 22, order of initialization). javac: \"reference to v may only appear after an explicit constructor invocation\".\n4. No `super(...)` written → Java inserts `super()` (the **no-argument** constructor). If the parent has **no** no-arg constructor → **compile error**. So always call `super` explicitly (slide 23).\n5. **`this(...)`** calls another constructor of the **same** class. Same placement rule: first action.\n6. You cannot write both `this(...)` and `super(...)` in one constructor. Use `this(...)` first; the constructor it calls must then call `super(...)` first (slide 24).\n7. Typical use: a shorter constructor passes defaults to the explicit-value constructor (`this(model, serialNumber, 20)`, slide 25).\n\nConstructor order (verified):\n```java\nclass A { A() { System.out.println(\"A()\"); } }\nclass B extends A { B() { this(1); System.out.println(\"B()\"); } B(int x) { System.out.println(\"B(int)\"); } }\nclass C extends B { C() { System.out.println(\"C()\"); } }\nnew C();   // prints A()  B(int)  B()  C()   — parent parts are always built first\n```\n\n## 7. An object has more than one type; is-a vs has-a (slides 13, 26–27, 37–45)\n\n- A derived object **is a** base object: a `ThermostatDevice` is a `Device`. It has the type of **every ancestor**.\n- So it can be assigned to an ancestor variable and passed where an ancestor parameter is expected: `Device d = new DoorDevice(...)`.\n- **Not the other way:** `DoorDevice x = new Device(...)` does not compile. The ancestor lacks the child's extra members (slide 13).\n- Employee hierarchy (slide 38, Display 7.1): Employee → HourlyEmployee (FullTimeHourlyEmployee, PartTimeEmployee), SalariedEmployee (TechnicalStaff → Engineer, Technician, ClericalStaff; Executive).\n- **\"is a\"** = inheritance (`HourlyEmployee` is an `Employee`). **\"has a\"** = **composition**: a class has an instance variable of a class type (`Employee` has an `Address`). Both are used together: HourlyEmployee *is an* Employee and *has an* Address (slides 43–45).\n\n## 8. Access modifiers (slides 49–55)\n\n| Modifier | Own class | Same package | Derived class in another package | Any other class |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `public` | yes | yes | yes | yes |\n| `protected` | yes | yes | yes (only through its own/inherited members — see below) | no |\n| none (package / default / friendly) | yes | yes | **no** | no |\n| `private` | yes | no | no | no |\n\n- `protected` is **weak** protection: anyone can subclass to get at it. Instance variables should normally **not** be protected (slide 49) — note his own `Device` uses protected fields anyway.\n- **Package access is more restricted than protected** (slide 51).\n- **Default package** (slide 53): classes in the current directory with no `package` line are all in one unnamed package, so they share package access.\n- Display 7.9 (slide 52): A has `public v1, protected v2, package v3, private v4`. B (same package): v1 v2 v3. C extends A (same package): v1 v2 v3. D extends A (other package): v1 v2. E (other package, unrelated): v1 only. Nobody else sees v4.\n- **Protected restriction (slides 54–55):** B extends A, **different packages**, A has `protected int n`. In B: `n` ✓ and `new B().n` ✓, but `new A().n` ✗ (\"n has protected access in A\" — verified). Same package → no problem.\n\n## 9. `super.method()` (slides 57–58)\n\n```java\npublic String toString() { return (super.toString() + \"$\" + wageRate); }   // e.g. \"Ann$8.5\"\n```\n- `super.m()` calls the **direct parent's** version of an overridden method.\n- `super.super.toString()` is **ILLEGAL** (compile error). You cannot skip a generation.\n\n## 10. Class `Object`; `equals`, `toString`, `getClass` (slides 59–69)\n\n- Every class descends from `Object` (package `java.lang`, auto-imported). A class with no `extends` extends `Object`.\n- A parameter of type `Object` accepts any object.\n- Inherited from Object: `equals`, `toString` (and `getClass`, `hashCode`…). Override `equals`/`toString` — library classes expect it. Default `toString` prints e.g. `Device@4961f6af`.\n- **`equals(Employee other)` OVERLOADS, it does not override** `equals(Object)` (slide 62). Collections call `equals(Object)`, so `list.contains(...)` silently uses the wrong one (verified: `false`).\n- The right `equals` (slides 63–64):\n```java\npublic boolean equals(Object otherObject) {\n    if (otherObject == null) return false;\n    else if (getClass() != otherObject.getClass()) return false;\n    else {\n        Employee otherEmployee = (Employee) otherObject;\n        return name.equals(otherEmployee.name) && hireDate.equals(otherEmployee.hireDate);\n    }\n}\n```\n  Steps: null check → same exact class → cast → compare every field.\n- **`instanceof` vs `getClass()` (slides 65–69):**\n  - `x instanceof Employee` is true for an Employee **or any descendant**.\n  - `a.getClass() == b.getClass()` is true only if both were created with **the same class** in `new`. It is more exact.\n  - `getClass()` is `final` in Object — cannot be overridden.\n  - With `instanceof` in both classes' `equals`, `e.equals(h)` is **true** but `h.equals(e)` is **false** → `equals` is not symmetric. `getClass()` avoids this. (Verified.)\n\n## 11. The EnhancedStringTokenizer (slides 31–36, Display 7.7)\n\n- Extends `java.util.StringTokenizer` to remember tokens returned so far.\n- Inherits `countTokens()` unchanged; **overrides** `nextToken()` and `nextToken(String delimiters)` (each calls `super.nextToken(...)`, stores the token in `a[count++]`); **adds** `tokensSoFar()` which returns a copy array of length `count`.\n- Constructor: `super(theString); a = new String[countTokens()]; count = 0;` — `countTokens()` can be called because `super` already ran.\n- Point: library classes can be extended by inheritance.\n- **Trap (verified, see Errata):** the inherited `nextToken(String)` internally calls `nextToken()`, which is the overridden one — so the textbook class records tokens twice. A base-class method that calls an overridable method always runs the subclass's override.\n\n## 12. Errata and clarifications (verified with JDK 26.0.2)\n\n| Slide | Slide says | Correct | How verified |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 17–18 | access \"can be changed from private in the base class to public\" for an overridden method | A private method is **not inherited**, so a public method with the same name in the child is a **new** method, not an override. Adding `@Override` gives \"does not override or implement a method from a supertype\". The public → private direction is a real compile error. | javac |\n| 25, 30 | `this(model, serialNumber, 20)` | Missing `;` → compile error. Slide 10 has it right: `this(model, serialNumber, 20);` | read |\n| 22, 24 | `super`/`this` must \"always be the first action\" | True for the textbook and Java ≤ 24, and the answer to give in exams. Since **JDK 25** (flexible constructor bodies) statements that do not use `this` may come before `super(...)`; JDK 26 compiled `if (k < 0) throw ...; v = k; super(k*2);`. Reading a field or calling an instance method before `super` is still illegal, which is what slide 22 means. | javac + ran it |\n| 24 | \"The same restrictions … apply to the this constructor i.e. call super first!\" | Means: `this(...)` must be first, like `super(...)`. You cannot call both. | — |\n| 34, 35 callouts | \"super.nextTokens\" | Method is `super.nextToken` (no s). | read |\n| 33–36 (Display 7.7) | `nextToken(String delimiters)` overrides and records the token | **Real bug (verified):** the JDK's `StringTokenizer.nextToken(String)` sets the delimiters and then calls `nextToken()`. By dynamic binding that runs the **overridden** `nextToken()`, which records the token, and then `nextToken(String)` records it again. `new EnhancedStringTokenizer(\"a b c d\").nextToken(\" \")` → `tokensSoFar()` = `[a, a]`; two `nextToken(\" \")` calls on `\"a b\"` throw `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException: Index 2`. Second problem: the array is sized once by `countTokens()`, but new delimiters can yield more tokens (`\"a,b c,d\"`: 2 by spaces, 3 by commas). Fix in `exercises-java/w06.md` E6. Good exam lesson: a base-class method that calls an overridable method runs **your** override. | ran it |\n| 66 | `!(OtherObject instanceof Employee)` | Parameter is `otherObject` (lower-case o); as written it does not compile. | read |\n| 66–67 | `new Employee(\"Joe\", new Address())`, then \"same name and hire date\" | His version passes an `Address`, but the `equals` on slide 64 compares `hireDate`. Savitch's original uses `new Date()`. The conclusion (testH true, testE false) holds. | ran an equivalent |\n| 7 vs 8 | common properties \"Name, Serial Number\" | Code uses `model`, `serialNumber`. | read |\n| 20 | \"It the modifier final\" | \"If the modifier final\". | — |\n\n## Likely exam questions\n\n1. **What is printed?** `Device d = new ThermostatDevice(\"T\",\"1\"); System.out.println(d.status());` → `Current temperature is 20 C.` (the object's override runs).\n2. **Does it compile?** `Device d = new DoorDevice(\"D\",\"1\"); d.openClose();` → **No**: `Device` has no `openClose()`.\n3. **Constructor order:** classes A ← B ← C each print in their no-arg constructor; `new C()` → `A B C`.\n4. **Find the bug:**\n   ```java\n   class Base { Base(int x) { } }\n   class Sub extends Base { Sub() { } }\n   ```\n   → compile error: `Sub()` implicitly calls `super()`, and `Base` has no no-arg constructor. Fix: `Sub() { super(0); }`.\n5. **Find the bug:** `class P { public void go() {} } class Q extends P { void go() {} }` → weaker access (public → package). Error.\n6. **Override or overload?** `public boolean equals(Employee e)` in Employee → overload. Write the overriding version (Section 10).\n7. **Legal?** `super.super.toString()` → no.\n8. **Theory:** difference between `instanceof` and `getClass()` in `equals`; why `getClass()` keeps `equals` symmetric.\n9. **Theory:** protected vs package access; which is more restrictive? → package.\n10. **Write a class:** `SmartLight extends Device` with a `brightness` field, a 2-arg constructor using `this(..., 100)`, a 3-arg one using `super`, and an overriding `status()`.\n11. **In-class questions** from slides 13, 14, 22, 65 (see the box at the top).\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"What is inheritance?","a":"Creating a new (derived) class from an existing (base) class; the derived class automatically gets the base's instance variables and methods and can add more."},{"q":"Give the synonyms for base class and derived class.","a":"Base = super = parent = ancestor; derived = sub = child = descendant."},{"q":"Which keyword makes a class a derived class?","a":"`extends`, e.g. `class DoorDevice extends Device`."},{"q":"Which is more general: the superclass or the subclass?","a":"The superclass (more general and inclusive, less complex); the subclass is more specialized."},{"q":"What is a method signature (slide 14)?","a":"The method name plus its parameter list; the return type and access modifiers are not part of it."},{"q":"What is overriding?","a":"Redefining an inherited method in the derived class with exactly the same signature."},{"q":"Overriding vs overloading?","a":"Overriding keeps the same parameter list and replaces the method; overloading uses a different parameter list and the original method is still inherited."},{"q":"What is a covariant return type?","a":"An overriding method may return a descendant of the original class return type (e.g. Employee → HourlyEmployee); allowed since Java 5.0."},{"q":"Can an overriding method be more restrictive in access (public → private)?","a":"No — compile error; access may only become more permissive."},{"q":"What does `final` on a method mean?","a":"It cannot be overridden in a derived class."},{"q":"What does `final` on a class mean?","a":"It cannot be used as a base class (cannot be extended)."},{"q":"How does a derived constructor call the base constructor?","a":"With `super(args);` as its first action — never with the base class name."},{"q":"What happens if a derived constructor has no `super(...)` call?","a":"Java calls the base's no-argument constructor automatically; compile error if there is none."},{"q":"Why can't a derived-class instance variable be an argument to `super` (slide 22)?","a":"Order of initialization: the superclass constructor must run before the subclass's own instance variables are initialized."},{"q":"What does `this(...)` inside a constructor do?","a":"Calls another constructor of the same class; it must be the first action."},{"q":"If you need both `this(...)` and `super(...)`, what do you do?","a":"Call `this(...)` first; the constructor it calls then calls `super(...)` as its first action."},{"q":"In `new C()` where C extends B extends A, in what order do the constructor bodies finish?","a":"A, then B, then C — the parent part is built first."},{"q":"Why can an ancestor not be used where a descendant is expected (slide 13)?","a":"Because the derived class may have specifications that are not in the parent class."},{"q":"Is `Device d = new DoorDevice(\"D\",\"1\");` legal? Is `d.openClose()`?","a":"Yes; no — Device has no openClose()."},{"q":"\"Is a\" vs \"has a\"?","a":"Is-a = inheritance (HourlyEmployee is an Employee); has-a = composition, an instance variable of a class type (Employee has an Address)."},{"q":"Can a derived class access a private instance variable of its base class by name?","a":"No — only through the base class's public accessor/mutator methods."},{"q":"Are private methods inherited?","a":"Effectively no — the child cannot call them; they run only indirectly through a public base method."},{"q":"Where can a protected member be accessed by name?","a":"In its own class, in any derived class, and in any class in the same package."},{"q":"Which is more restrictive: package access or protected?","a":"Package access."},{"q":"What is the default package?","a":"The unnamed package of all classes in the current directory that declare no package; they share package access."},{"q":"B extends A in a different package; A has protected n. Can a method in B read `new A().n`?","a":"No — only n as B's own inherited member (n, or n of a B object)."},{"q":"Are static variables inherited?","a":"Yes, with the same meaning of public/private/protected/package."},{"q":"How do you call the parent's version of an overridden toString?","a":"`super.toString()`."},{"q":"Is `super.super.toString()` legal?","a":"No — super reaches only the direct parent."},{"q":"What is the ancestor of every Java class?","a":"`Object` (in java.lang, imported automatically)."},{"q":"Why is `public boolean equals(Employee other)` wrong?","a":"It overloads instead of overriding `equals(Object)`, so code that calls equals(Object) (e.g. collections) ignores it."},{"q":"List the steps of a correct equals(Object).","a":"Return false if null; return false if getClass() differs; cast; compare every instance variable."},{"q":"instanceof vs getClass()?","a":"instanceof is true for the class and all descendants; getClass()==getClass() is true only for exactly the same class."},{"q":"What goes wrong with instanceof-based equals in Employee and HourlyEmployee?","a":"e.equals(h) is true but h.equals(e) is false — equals stops being symmetric."},{"q":"Why does Display 7.7's EnhancedStringTokenizer record a token twice after nextToken(\" \")?","a":"The JDK's nextToken(String) calls nextToken(), which by dynamic binding is the overriding version that already records it."},{"q":"What are the CS105 week-6 lab and next-class announcements?","a":"Graded Lab One (5 pts: 4 practical + 1 theory) on Inheritance I this week; next class exception handling + debugging and QUIZ 1 in hour 2."}],"figs":[{"n":1,"src":"slides/W6s001.jpg"},{"n":2,"src":"slides/W6s002.jpg"},{"n":3,"src":"slides/W6s003.jpg"},{"n":4,"src":"slides/W6s004.jpg"},{"n":5,"src":"slides/W6s005.jpg"},{"n":6,"src":"slides/W6s006.jpg"},{"n":7,"src":"slides/W6s007.jpg"},{"n":8,"src":"slides/W6s008.jpg"},{"n":9,"src":"slides/W6s009.jpg"},{"n":10,"src":"slides/W6s010.jpg"},{"n":11,"src":"slides/W6s011.jpg"},{"n":12,"src":"slides/W6s012.jpg"},{"n":13,"src":"slides/W6s013.jpg"},{"n":14,"src":"slides/W6s014.jpg"},{"n":15,"src":"slides/W6s015.jpg"},{"n":16,"src":"slides/W6s016.jpg"},{"n":17,"src":"slides/W6s017.jpg"},{"n":18,"src":"slides/W6s018.jpg"},{"n":19,"src":"slides/W6s019.jpg"},{"n":20,"src":"slides/W6s020.jpg"},{"n":21,"src":"slides/W6s021.jpg"},{"n":22,"src":"slides/W6s022.jpg"},{"n":23,"src":"slides/W6s023.jpg"},{"n":24,"src":"slides/W6s024.jpg"},{"n":25,"src":"slides/W6s025.jpg"},{"n":26,"src":"slides/W6s026.jpg"},{"n":27,"src":"slides/W6s027.jpg"},{"n":28,"src":"slides/W6s028.jpg"},{"n":29,"src":"slides/W6s029.jpg"},{"n":30,"src":"slides/W6s030.jpg"},{"n":31,"src":"slides/W6s031.jpg"},{"n":32,"src":"slides/W6s032.jpg"},{"n":33,"src":"slides/W6s033.jpg"},{"n":34,"src":"slides/W6s034.jpg"},{"n":35,"src":"slides/W6s035.jpg"},{"n":36,"src":"slides/W6s036.jpg"},{"n":37,"src":"slides/W6s037.jpg"},{"n":38,"src":"slides/W6s038.jpg"},{"n":39,"src":"slides/W6s039.jpg"},{"n":40,"src":"slides/W6s040.jpg"},{"n":41,"src":"slides/W6s041.jpg"},{"n":42,"src":"slides/W6s042.jpg"},{"n":43,"src":"slides/W6s043.jpg"},{"n":44,"src":"slides/W6s044.jpg"},{"n":45,"src":"slides/W6s045.jpg"},{"n":46,"src":"slides/W6s046.jpg"},{"n":47,"src":"slides/W6s047.jpg"},{"n":48,"src":"slides/W6s048.jpg"},{"n":49,"src":"slides/W6s049.jpg"},{"n":50,"src":"slides/W6s050.jpg"},{"n":51,"src":"slides/W6s051.jpg"},{"n":52,"src":"slides/W6s052.jpg"},{"n":53,"src":"slides/W6s053.jpg"},{"n":54,"src":"slides/W6s054.jpg"},{"n":55,"src":"slides/W6s055.jpg"},{"n":56,"src":"slides/W6s056.jpg"},{"n":57,"src":"slides/W6s057.jpg"},{"n":58,"src":"slides/W6s058.jpg"},{"n":59,"src":"slides/W6s059.jpg"},{"n":60,"src":"slides/W6s060.jpg"},{"n":61,"src":"slides/W6s061.jpg"},{"n":62,"src":"slides/W6s062.jpg"},{"n":63,"src":"slides/W6s063.jpg"},{"n":64,"src":"slides/W6s064.jpg"},{"n":65,"src":"slides/W6s065.jpg"},{"n":66,"src":"slides/W6s066.jpg"},{"n":67,"src":"slides/W6s067.jpg"},{"n":68,"src":"slides/W6s068.jpg"},{"n":69,"src":"slides/W6s069.jpg"},{"n":70,"src":"slides/W6s070.jpg"}],"exercises":[{"id":"e1","lang":"java","title":"SmartLight extends Device","level":"warm","blurb":"Dr. Oladejo's `Device` class is given (do not change it). Finish `SmartLight extends Device`:","prompt":"Dr. Oladejo's `Device` class is given (do not change it). Finish `SmartLight extends Device`:\n- constructor `SmartLight(String model, String serialNumber, int brightness)` must call `super(model, serialNumber)` and store `brightness`;\n- override `status()` (use `@Override`) to return `\"Light at 70%.\"` for brightness 70.","starter":"class Device {\n    protected String model;\n    protected String serialNumber;\n    public Device(String model, String serialNumber) { this.model = model; this.serialNumber = serialNumber; }\n    public String status() { return \"Generic device status.\"; }\n}\nclass SmartLight extends Device {\n    private int brightness;\n    public SmartLight(String model, String serialNumber, int brightness) {\n        super(model, serialNumber);\n        // TODO store brightness\n    }\n    // TODO override status()\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        SmartLight s = new SmartLight(\"Hue\", \"SN-7\", 70);\n        if (!\"Hue\".equals(s.model) || !\"SN-7\".equals(s.serialNumber)) throw new AssertionError(\"super(model, serialNumber) not called correctly\");\n        Device d = s;   // a SmartLight IS A Device\n        if (!\"Light at 70%.\".equals(d.status())) throw new AssertionError(\"status() through a Device variable gave: \" + d.status());\n        System.out.println(\"PASS override runs through a Device reference\");\n        if (!\"Light at 5%.\".equals(new SmartLight(\"x\", \"y\", 5).status())) throw new AssertionError(\"brightness not stored\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS brightness\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Device {\n    protected String model;\n    protected String serialNumber;\n    public Device(String model, String serialNumber) { this.model = model; this.serialNumber = serialNumber; }\n    public String status() { return \"Generic device status.\"; }\n}\nclass SmartLight extends Device {\n    private int brightness;\n    public SmartLight(String model, String serialNumber, int brightness) {\n        super(model, serialNumber);\n        this.brightness = brightness;\n    }\n    @Override\n    public String status() { return \"Light at \" + brightness + \"%.\"; }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e2","lang":"java","title":"ThermostatDevice with this(...)","level":"warm","blurb":"Write the two-argument constructor of `ThermostatDevice` so it calls the three-argument one with `this(model, serialNumber, 20)` (slide 25 — note the semicolon)","prompt":"Write the two-argument constructor of `ThermostatDevice` so it calls the three-argument one with `this(model, serialNumber, 20)` (slide 25 — note the semicolon). Also add `setTemp(int t)` from the UML on slide 6.","starter":"class Device {\n    protected String model;\n    protected String serialNumber;\n    public Device(String model, String serialNumber) { this.model = model; this.serialNumber = serialNumber; }\n    public String status() { return \"Generic device status.\"; }\n}\nclass ThermostatDevice extends Device {\n    private int temperature;\n    public ThermostatDevice(String model, String serialNumber) {\n        super(model, serialNumber);\n    }\n    public ThermostatDevice(String model, String serialNumber, int temp) {\n        super(model, serialNumber);\n        this.temperature = temp;\n    }\n    @Override\n    public String status() { return \"Current temperature is \" + temperature + \" C.\"; }\n    public void setTemp(int t) { /* TODO */ }\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        ThermostatDevice t = new ThermostatDevice(\"Nest\", \"SN-1\");\n        if (!\"Current temperature is 20 C.\".equals(t.status())) throw new AssertionError(\"default temperature should be 20, got: \" + t.status());\n        if (!\"SN-1\".equals(t.serialNumber)) throw new AssertionError(\"serial number lost\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS default 20 via this(...)\");\n        t.setTemp(23);\n        if (!\"Current temperature is 23 C.\".equals(t.status())) throw new AssertionError(\"setTemp does not work: \" + t.status());\n        System.out.println(\"PASS setTemp\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Device {\n    protected String model;\n    protected String serialNumber;\n    public Device(String model, String serialNumber) { this.model = model; this.serialNumber = serialNumber; }\n    public String status() { return \"Generic device status.\"; }\n}\nclass ThermostatDevice extends Device {\n    private int temperature;\n    public ThermostatDevice(String model, String serialNumber) {\n        this(model, serialNumber, 20);\n    }\n    public ThermostatDevice(String model, String serialNumber, int temp) {\n        super(model, serialNumber);\n        this.temperature = temp;\n    }\n    @Override\n    public String status() { return \"Current temperature is \" + temperature + \" C.\"; }\n    public void setTemp(int t) { temperature = t; }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e3","lang":"java","title":"Override equals, don't overload it","level":"core","blurb":"The starter's `equals(Employee)` **overloads** `Object.equals(Object)` (slide 62), so an `ArrayList` cannot find an equal employee. Replace it with a correct **","prompt":"The starter's `equals(Employee)` **overloads** `Object.equals(Object)` (slide 62), so an `ArrayList` cannot find an equal employee. Replace it with a correct **override** `public boolean equals(Object otherObject)` following slide 64: null → false; different `getClass()` → false; cast; compare `name` and `id`.","starter":"class Employee {\n    private String name;\n    private int id;\n    public Employee(String name, int id) { this.name = name; this.id = id; }\n    public boolean equals(Employee other) {\n        return other != null && name.equals(other.name) && id == other.id;\n    }\n}","test":"import java.util.ArrayList;\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Employee a = new Employee(\"Joe\", 7);\n        Object b = new Employee(\"Joe\", 7);\n        if (!a.equals(b)) throw new AssertionError(\"a.equals(Object b) is false: equals is overloaded, not overridden\");\n        ArrayList<Employee> list = new ArrayList<>();\n        list.add(new Employee(\"Ann\", 1));\n        if (!list.contains(new Employee(\"Ann\", 1))) throw new AssertionError(\"ArrayList.contains uses equals(Object)\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS equals(Object) overridden\");\n        if (a.equals(null)) throw new AssertionError(\"equals(null) must be false\");\n        if (a.equals(\"Joe\")) throw new AssertionError(\"an Employee must not equal a String\");\n        if (a.equals(new Employee(\"Joe\", 8))) throw new AssertionError(\"different id must not be equal\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS null / other class / different field\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Employee {\n    private String name;\n    private int id;\n    public Employee(String name, int id) { this.name = name; this.id = id; }\n    @Override\n    public boolean equals(Object otherObject) {\n        if (otherObject == null) return false;\n        else if (getClass() != otherObject.getClass()) return false;\n        else {\n            Employee other = (Employee) otherObject;\n            return name.equals(other.name) && id == other.id;\n        }\n    }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e4","lang":"java","title":"toString with super.toString()","level":"core","blurb":"`Employee.name` is **private**, so `HourlyEmployee` cannot use it by name (slide 46). Override `HourlyEmployee.toString()` to return the parent's text followed ","prompt":"`Employee.name` is **private**, so `HourlyEmployee` cannot use it by name (slide 46). Override `HourlyEmployee.toString()` to return the parent's text followed by `\"$\"` and the wage rate, using `super.toString()` (slide 57). Example: `Ann$8.5`.","starter":"class Employee {\n    private String name;\n    public Employee(String name) { this.name = name; }\n    public String getName() { return name; }\n    @Override\n    public String toString() { return name; }\n}\nclass HourlyEmployee extends Employee {\n    private double wageRate;\n    public HourlyEmployee(String name, double wageRate) {\n        super(name);\n        this.wageRate = wageRate;\n    }\n    // TODO override toString\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Employee e = new HourlyEmployee(\"Ann\", 8.5);\n        if (!\"Ann$8.5\".equals(e.toString())) throw new AssertionError(\"expected Ann$8.5 but got \" + e);\n        System.out.println(\"PASS \" + e);\n        if (!\"Bo$12.0\".equals(String.valueOf(new HourlyEmployee(\"Bo\", 12)))) throw new AssertionError(\"expected Bo$12.0\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS second object\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Employee {\n    private String name;\n    public Employee(String name) { this.name = name; }\n    public String getName() { return name; }\n    @Override\n    public String toString() { return name; }\n}\nclass HourlyEmployee extends Employee {\n    private double wageRate;\n    public HourlyEmployee(String name, double wageRate) {\n        super(name);\n        this.wageRate = wageRate;\n    }\n    @Override\n    public String toString() { return super.toString() + \"$\" + wageRate; }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e5","lang":"java","title":"Covariant return type","level":"core","blurb":"`Shape.copy()` returns a `Shape`. Override `copy()` in `Circle` with the **covariant return type** `Circle` (slides 15–16) so it returns a new `Circle` with the","prompt":"`Shape.copy()` returns a `Shape`. Override `copy()` in `Circle` with the **covariant return type** `Circle` (slides 15–16) so it returns a new `Circle` with the same radius. Add a copy constructor `Circle(Circle other)` and use it.","starter":"class Shape {\n    public Shape copy() { return new Shape(); }\n    public double area() { return 0; }\n}\nclass Circle extends Shape {\n    private double radius;\n    public Circle(double radius) { this.radius = radius; }\n    @Override\n    public double area() { return Math.PI * radius * radius; }\n    // TODO: copy constructor + covariant copy()\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Shape s = new Circle(2);\n        Shape t = s.copy();\n        if (!(t instanceof Circle)) throw new AssertionError(\"copy() of a Circle must return a Circle, got \" + t.getClass().getName());\n        if (t == s) throw new AssertionError(\"copy() must return a new object\");\n        if (Math.abs(t.area() - s.area()) > 1e-9) throw new AssertionError(\"radius not copied\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS Circle.copy()\");\n        try {\n            Class<?> rt = Circle.class.getDeclaredMethod(\"copy\").getReturnType();\n            if (rt != Circle.class) throw new AssertionError(\"declare the return type as Circle (covariant), not \" + rt.getSimpleName());\n        } catch (NoSuchMethodException e) { throw new AssertionError(\"Circle must declare copy()\"); }\n        System.out.println(\"PASS covariant return type\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Shape {\n    public Shape copy() { return new Shape(); }\n    public double area() { return 0; }\n}\nclass Circle extends Shape {\n    private double radius;\n    public Circle(double radius) { this.radius = radius; }\n    public Circle(Circle other) { this.radius = other.radius; }\n    @Override\n    public double area() { return Math.PI * radius * radius; }\n    @Override\n    public Circle copy() { return new Circle(this); }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e6","lang":"java","title":"Fix the EnhancedStringTokenizer","level":"hard","blurb":"The textbook's `EnhancedStringTokenizer` (Display 7.7, slides 33–36) has **two bugs** (both verified on JDK 26):","prompt":"The textbook's `EnhancedStringTokenizer` (Display 7.7, slides 33–36) has **two bugs** (both verified on JDK 26):\n1. **Double recording.** Inside the JDK, `StringTokenizer.nextToken(String delim)` sets the delimiters and then calls `nextToken()`. Because of dynamic binding that call runs **our overridden** `nextToken()`, which already stores the token — then `nextToken(String)` stores it a second time. `new EnhancedStringTokenizer(\"a b c d\").nextToken(\" \")` leaves `tokensSoFar()` = `[a, a]`; on `\"a b\"` two calls of `nextToken(\" \")` crash with `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException`.\n2. **Fixed-size array.** It is sized once with `countTokens()`, but changing the delimiters can produce more tokens than that (`\"a,b c,d\"` has 2 tokens by spaces, 3 by commas).\n\nKeep the design (extends `StringTokenizer`, overrides both `nextToken` methods, adds `tokensSoFar()`). Make each token recorded **exactly once** whether or not the library calls `nextToken()` internally (hint: a boolean flag), and let the storage grow (e.g. double the array).","starter":"class EnhancedStringTokenizer extends java.util.StringTokenizer {   // fully qualified: an import line would have to sit above the test's Main\n    private String[] a;\n    private int count;\n    public EnhancedStringTokenizer(String theString) {\n        super(theString);\n        a = new String[countTokens()];\n        count = 0;\n    }\n    public String nextToken() {\n        String token = super.nextToken();\n        a[count] = token;\n        count++;\n        return token;\n    }\n    public String nextToken(String delimiters) {\n        String token = super.nextToken(delimiters);\n        a[count] = token;\n        count++;\n        return token;\n    }\n    public String[] tokensSoFar() {\n        String[] arrayToReturn = new String[count];\n        for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) arrayToReturn[i] = a[i];\n        return arrayToReturn;\n    }\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        EnhancedStringTokenizer t = new EnhancedStringTokenizer(\"one two three\");\n        t.nextToken(); t.nextToken();\n        if (!java.util.Arrays.equals(t.tokensSoFar(), new String[]{\"one\", \"two\"})) throw new AssertionError(\"tokensSoFar wrong\");\n        if (t.countTokens() != 1) throw new AssertionError(\"countTokens should be inherited unchanged\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS normal use\");\n        EnhancedStringTokenizer w = new EnhancedStringTokenizer(\"a b\");\n        String first, second;\n        try { first = w.nextToken(\" \"); second = w.nextToken(\" \"); }\n        catch (ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException e) { throw new AssertionError(\"nextToken(String) records each token twice (it calls your nextToken() internally): \" + e.getMessage()); }\n        if (!java.util.Arrays.equals(w.tokensSoFar(), new String[]{\"a\", \"b\"}))\n            throw new AssertionError(\"expected [a, b] but tokensSoFar() = \" + java.util.Arrays.toString(w.tokensSoFar()));\n        System.out.println(\"PASS each token recorded once\");\n        EnhancedStringTokenizer u = new EnhancedStringTokenizer(\"a,b c,d\");   // 2 tokens with the default delimiters\n        String r;\n        try {\n            r = u.nextToken(\",\") + \"|\" + u.nextToken() + \"|\" + u.nextToken();\n        } catch (ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException e) {\n            throw new AssertionError(\"crashed after changing delimiters: \" + e.getMessage());\n        }\n        if (!\"a|b c|d\".equals(r)) throw new AssertionError(\"tokens were \" + r);\n        if (u.tokensSoFar().length != 3) throw new AssertionError(\"tokensSoFar should hold 3 tokens\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS survives delimiter change\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class EnhancedStringTokenizer extends java.util.StringTokenizer {   // fully qualified: an import line would have to sit above the test's Main\n    private String[] a;\n    private int count;\n    private boolean recording = true;   // off while nextToken(String) runs, because the JDK calls nextToken() inside it\n    public EnhancedStringTokenizer(String theString) {\n        super(theString);\n        a = new String[Math.max(1, countTokens())];\n        count = 0;\n    }\n    private void store(String token) {\n        if (count == a.length) {\n            String[] bigger = new String[a.length * 2];\n            for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) bigger[i] = a[i];\n            a = bigger;\n        }\n        a[count] = token;\n        count++;\n    }\n    @Override\n    public String nextToken() {\n        String token = super.nextToken();\n        if (recording) store(token);\n        return token;\n    }\n    @Override\n    public String nextToken(String delimiters) {\n        String token;\n        recording = false;\n        try { token = super.nextToken(delimiters); }\n        finally { recording = true; }\n        store(token);\n        return token;\n    }\n    public String[] tokensSoFar() {\n        String[] arrayToReturn = new String[count];\n        for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) arrayToReturn[i] = a[i];\n        return arrayToReturn;\n    }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e7","lang":"java","title":"Symmetric equals in a hierarchy","level":"hard","blurb":"The starter uses `instanceof` in both classes, so `e.equals(h)` is true but `h.equals(e)` is false (slides 65–67). Rewrite both `equals` methods with `getClass(","prompt":"The starter uses `instanceof` in both classes, so `e.equals(h)` is true but `h.equals(e)` is false (slides 65–67). Rewrite both `equals` methods with `getClass()` (slide 64). `HourlyEmployee.equals` must call `super.equals(otherObject)` for the inherited part, then compare `wageRate`.","starter":"class Employee {\n    private String name;\n    public Employee(String name) { this.name = name; }\n    @Override\n    public boolean equals(Object otherObject) {\n        if (otherObject == null) return false;\n        if (!(otherObject instanceof Employee)) return false;\n        return name.equals(((Employee) otherObject).name);\n    }\n}\nclass HourlyEmployee extends Employee {\n    private double wageRate;\n    public HourlyEmployee(String name, double wageRate) { super(name); this.wageRate = wageRate; }\n    @Override\n    public boolean equals(Object otherObject) {\n        if (otherObject == null) return false;\n        if (!(otherObject instanceof HourlyEmployee)) return false;\n        HourlyEmployee o = (HourlyEmployee) otherObject;\n        return super.equals(o) && wageRate == o.wageRate;\n    }\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Employee e = new Employee(\"Joe\");\n        HourlyEmployee h = new HourlyEmployee(\"Joe\", 8.5);\n        if (e.equals(h) != h.equals(e)) throw new AssertionError(\"not symmetric: e.equals(h)=\" + e.equals(h) + \", h.equals(e)=\" + h.equals(e));\n        if (e.equals(h)) throw new AssertionError(\"an Employee and an HourlyEmployee must not be equal\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS symmetric\");\n        if (!h.equals(new HourlyEmployee(\"Joe\", 8.5))) throw new AssertionError(\"equal hourly employees\");\n        if (h.equals(new HourlyEmployee(\"Joe\", 9.0))) throw new AssertionError(\"different wageRate\");\n        if (h.equals(new HourlyEmployee(\"Ann\", 8.5))) throw new AssertionError(\"different name\");\n        if (!e.equals(new Employee(\"Joe\")) || e.equals(null)) throw new AssertionError(\"Employee.equals basics\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS field comparisons\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Employee {\n    private String name;\n    public Employee(String name) { this.name = name; }\n    @Override\n    public boolean equals(Object otherObject) {\n        if (otherObject == null) return false;\n        if (getClass() != otherObject.getClass()) return false;\n        return name.equals(((Employee) otherObject).name);\n    }\n}\nclass HourlyEmployee extends Employee {\n    private double wageRate;\n    public HourlyEmployee(String name, double wageRate) { super(name); this.wageRate = wageRate; }\n    @Override\n    public boolean equals(Object otherObject) {\n        if (!super.equals(otherObject)) return false;   // null check, same class, same name\n        HourlyEmployee o = (HourlyEmployee) otherObject;\n        return wageRate == o.wageRate;\n    }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."}]},{"id":"07","title":"W7 · Exception Handling (ch. 9)","slides":"Week 7 · 41 slides","md":"# 07 — Week 7 · Exception Handling (Savitch ch. 9, 41 slides)\n\n_Ingested 15.11.2026 from lectures/Week7_Savich_chap9_Exception_ko.pptx (PDF copy in lectures/), Savitch Absolute Java 6e ch. 9 slides by Williams/Mock, updated by Dr. Kazeem Oladejo, November 2026. Code slides 14, 22–25 and the hierarchy diagram (slide 37) are images; they were rendered and read. Every code example below was compiled and run on JDK 26._\n\n> **Instructor items in this deck**\n> - **Slide 2, Course Assessment:** **Week 7 = Quiz 1 (7 overall points)** · **Week 8 = Midterm Exam** · Week 9 = no lecture (Lab: Inheritance II) · **Week 10 = Polymorphism & Abstract Classes + Graded Lab 2 (10 overall points)**.\n> - **Slide 13, in-class question (red):** \"Where does the exception come from in the code?\" (demo `WholeNumExceptionDemo.java`). **His answer: `nextInt()`.** Learn this pattern: `Scanner.nextInt()` throws `InputMismatchException` when the token is not a whole number.\n\n## 1. The idea (slides 3–4)\n\n- **Throwing an exception** = signalling that something unusual happened. Library code or your own code does this.\n- **Handling an exception** = the code somewhere else that deals with it.\n- Most exceptions represent an error condition. The normal path (\"nothing unusual\") goes in the `try` block.\n\n## 2. try–throw–catch (slides 5–8)\n\n```java\ntry {\n    // basic algorithm; may throw\n    if (bad) throw new Exception(\"why\");   // throw = statement; the value is always an exception OBJECT\n    // skipped if the throw above runs\n} catch (Exception e) {                    // exception handler; e = the thrown object\n    System.out.println(e.getMessage());\n}\n```\n\n- When an exception is thrown, the rest of the `try` block is **abandoned**. Control jumps to the first matching `catch`.\n- A `catch` block looks like a method with one parameter, but it is not a method.\n- The thrown object is plugged in for the catch parameter. Running the catch block = **catching / handling** the exception.\n- `throw new ExceptionClassName(args);` — `throw` is followed by an object, usually created with `new` on the spot.\n- If no exception is thrown, all catch blocks are skipped and execution continues after them.\n\n## 3. Library exceptions and Scanner (slides 9–14)\n\n- Often the library throws, not you. `keyboard.nextInt()` throws **`InputMismatchException`** if the user types `forty two`.\n- `InputMismatchException` is in **`java.util`** → `import java.util.InputMismatchException;`\n- It descends from `RuntimeException` (via `NoSuchElementException`), so it is **unchecked**: you do not have to catch or declare it, but you may.\n- **Exception-controlled loop** (slide 12, Display 9.1 on slide 14): repeat until the risky line succeeds.\n\n```java\nScanner keyboard = new Scanner(System.in);\nint number = 0;              // \"to keep compiler happy\"\nboolean done = false;\nwhile (!done) {\n    try {\n        System.out.println(\"Enter a whole number:\");\n        number = keyboard.nextInt();\n        done = true;         // only reached if nextInt() did not throw\n    } catch (InputMismatchException e) {\n        keyboard.nextLine(); // discard the bad token, else infinite loop\n        System.out.println(\"Not a correctly written whole number.\");\n        System.out.println(\"Try again.\");\n    }\n}\nSystem.out.println(\"You entered \" + number);\n```\n\nGotcha: without `keyboard.nextLine()` in the catch, the bad token stays in the input, `nextInt()` fails again, and the loop never ends.\n\n## 4. Exception classes and getMessage (slides 15–19, 26)\n\n- Every predefined exception class has (1) a constructor taking one `String`, and (2) `getMessage()` returning that string.\n- Your own exception classes should behave the same.\n- The two key facts about an exception object: its **type** (class) and its **message**.\n- Library examples: `IOException`, `FileNotFoundException`, `NoSuchMethodException`. Most need an import (`import java.io.IOException;`). `Exception` itself is in `java.lang`, so no import.\n- No-arg constructor of a library exception → `getMessage()` returns **`null`**. `toString()` (what `println(e)` shows) gives `ClassName: message`, e.g. `java.lang.Exception: boom`.\n\n| Common exception | Package | Checked? | Typical cause |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `Exception` | java.lang | checked | base class you extend |\n| `IOException` | java.io | checked | general I/O failure |\n| `FileNotFoundException` | java.io | checked (subclass of IOException) | file cannot be opened/created |\n| `InputMismatchException` | java.util | unchecked | `nextInt()` on non-int |\n| `ArithmeticException` | java.lang | unchecked | integer `/ 0` (message `/ by zero`) |\n| `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException` | java.lang | unchecked | bad index |\n| `NullPointerException` | java.lang | unchecked | method call on `null` |\n| `NumberFormatException` | java.lang | unchecked | `Integer.parseInt(\"4x\")` |\n| `ClassCastException` | java.lang | unchecked | bad downcast |\n\n## 5. Defining your own exception class (slides 20–28)\n\nDisplay 9.4 (slide 22):\n\n```java\npublic class DivisionByZeroException extends Exception {\n    public DivisionByZeroException() {\n        super(\"Division by Zero!\");          // default message\n    }\n    public DivisionByZeroException(String message) {\n        super(message);                       // pass message to Exception\n    }\n}\n```\n\nRules (slides 21, 27, 28):\n- Must extend an existing exception class (usually `Exception`, unless a more specific one fits).\n- **Constructors are the most important members.** Define at least two:\n  - a `String` constructor whose body begins with `super(message)`;\n  - a no-arg constructor whose body begins with `super(\"some default string\")`.\n- This **preserves `getMessage()`**, which is inherited and returns the string given to `super`.\n- Extend `Exception` → checked. Extend `RuntimeException` → unchecked.\n\nUsing it (Display 9.5, slides 23–25):\n\n```java\ntry {\n    int numerator = keyboard.nextInt();\n    int denominator = keyboard.nextInt();\n    if (denominator == 0)\n        throw new DivisionByZeroException();\n    double quotient = numerator / (double) denominator;\n    System.out.println(numerator + \"/\" + denominator + \" = \" + quotient);\n} catch (DivisionByZeroException e) {\n    System.out.println(e.getMessage());   // prints: Division by Zero!\n    secondChance();\n}\nSystem.out.println(\"End of program.\");\n```\n\n`secondChance()` (slide 25) handles a second zero **without** throwing: it prints \"I cannot do division by zero.\" and calls `System.exit(0)`. Book's annotation: *sometimes it is better to handle an exceptional case without throwing an exception.*\n\n## 6. Multiple catch blocks and catch order (slides 29–31)\n\n- One `try` can throw different exception types on different runs, but **at most one per execution** (a throw ends the try block).\n- Each `catch` catches only its own class **and its subclasses**.\n- Catch blocks are checked **top to bottom**; the **first match** runs, the rest are skipped.\n- **Pitfall: catch the more specific exception first.** If `catch (IOException e)` comes before `catch (FileNotFoundException e)`, the second is unreachable and javac refuses: `exception FileNotFoundException has already been caught` (verified).\n\n```java\ntry { ... }\ncatch (FileNotFoundException e) { ... }   // subclass first\ncatch (IOException e) { ... }             // then superclass\ncatch (Exception e) { ... }               // most general last\n```\n\n## 7. throws clause and the Catch-or-Declare rule (slides 32–35, 39)\n\n- A method that may throw a checked exception but does not catch it must **declare** it: `public void aMethod() throws AnException, AnotherException`.\n- If a method throws and does not catch, the method ends **immediately**; the exception goes to the caller.\n- **Catch or Declare Rule** (checked exceptions): either (1) catch it in the same method, or (2) declare it with `throws` and \"pass the buck\" to the caller.\n- Ultimately some method should catch it. If every method up to `main` only declares it, it is never caught:\n  - non-GUI program: terminates with an error message naming the exception class (`Exception in thread \"main\" java.lang.Exception: lost` + stack trace; exit code 1, verified);\n  - GUI program: may keep running in an unreliable state.\n- Compiler errors you should recognise (verified):\n  - `throw new Exception(\"x\");` in a method with no `throws` and no catch → `unreported exception Exception; must be caught or declared to be thrown`.\n  - catching a checked exception that the try block cannot throw (e.g. `IOException` around `int x = 1;`) → `exception IOException is never thrown in body of corresponding try statement`.\n\n## 8. Checked vs unchecked (slides 36–37)\n\nHierarchy (Display 9.11, slide 37):\n\n```\nThrowable\n├── Exception                ← checked (catch or declare)\n│   ├── RuntimeException     ← UNCHECKED, with all its subclasses\n│   └── IOException, your own `extends Exception` classes, ... ← checked\n└── Error                    ← UNCHECKED (serious JVM problems, e.g. OutOfMemoryError)\n```\n\n- **Checked** = subject to Catch-or-Declare; the compiler enforces it. `Throwable`, `Exception`, and every subclass of `Exception` **except the `RuntimeException` branch**.\n- **Unchecked** = `RuntimeException` and its subclasses, plus `Error` and its subclasses. No catch/throws required (allowed, though).\n- Rule of thumb: unchecked = programmer bug (bad index, null, bad cast) or catastrophic; checked = something outside your control you must plan for (missing file).\n\n## 9. throws clause when overriding (slide 38)\n\n- An overriding method may list **the same** checked exceptions as the base method, **a subset**, or **subclasses** of them. It may list none.\n- It may **not add** new checked exceptions. Verified: base `void n()` and derived `void n() throws IOException` → `n() in B cannot override n() in A`.\n- Adding an **unchecked** exception to the clause is allowed (verified with `IllegalStateException`).\n\n## 10. finally (slides 40–41)\n\n```java\ntry { ... }\ncatch (ExceptionClass1 e) { ... }\ncatch (ExceptionClassN e) { ... }\nfinally { /* runs in all cases */ }\n```\n\nThree cases (slide 41):\n1. try finishes normally → finally runs.\n2. exception thrown and caught → catch runs, then finally runs.\n3. exception thrown and **not** caught here → finally runs, then the method ends and the exception goes to the caller.\n\nExtra facts (verified, not on slides):\n- finally runs even when the try block executes `return`: `try { return 1; } finally { print(\"fin\"); }` prints `fin` and then returns 1.\n- A `return` inside finally overrides a pending return **and swallows** a pending exception (bad practice; javac warns).\n- `System.exit(0)` inside try stops the JVM: **finally does NOT run**.\n- `continue`/`break` in a catch inside a loop still run finally first.\n\nTracing template (verified):\n\n```java\nstatic String t(int k) {\n    String s = \"\";\n    try {\n        s += \"A\";\n        if (k == 1) throw new IllegalStateException();\n        if (k == 2) throw new Exception();\n        s += \"B\";\n    } catch (IllegalStateException e) { s += \"C\"; }\n      catch (Exception e)             { s += \"D\"; }\n      finally                         { s += \"F\"; }\n    return s + \"E\";\n}\n// t(0) = \"ABFE\", t(1) = \"ACFE\", t(2) = \"ADFE\"\n```\n\n## 11. Modern Java extras (beyond the slides, useful in labs)\n\n- **Multi-catch** (Java 7): `catch (FileNotFoundException | InputMismatchException e)`. The types must not be subclass-related.\n- **try-with-resources** (Java 7): `try (Scanner in = new Scanner(new File(\"a.txt\"))) { ... }` closes the resource automatically, even on an exception. See section 08.\n\n## 12. Errata (slides vs. real Java)\n\n| Slide | What it says | What is correct | How verified |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 8 | A catch block \"can have at most one parameter\" | It must have **exactly one** parameter. `catch ()` is a syntax error. | javac |\n| 13 vs 14 | Demo file named `WholeNumExceptionDemo.java` | The code on slide 14 (Display 9.1) is class `InputMismatchExceptionDemo`. Same program, different name. | slide images |\n| 17 | \"`Exception` is the root class for all exceptions; every exception class is a descendant of `Exception`\" | The root is **`Throwable`**. `Error` and its subclasses are throwable but not descendants of `Exception` (slide 37 shows this correctly). | `Error.class.getSuperclass()` = Throwable |\n| 36 | \"`Throwable`, `Exception`, and **all descendants of `Exception`** are checked\" | All descendants **except `RuntimeException` and its subclasses**, which are unchecked (the same slide's last bullet and slide 37 say so). | `throw new IllegalStateException()` compiles with no `throws` |\n| 38 | Derived class must list the same exceptions or a subset; \"may not add any\" | Correct for **checked** exceptions. It may also list **subclasses** of the base's exceptions, and may add **unchecked** ones. | javac (Over.java: `throws FileNotFoundException, IllegalStateException` overriding `throws IOException` compiles) |\n\n## Likely exam questions\n\n1. **What is printed?**\n   ```java\n   try { System.out.println(\"A\"); throw new Exception(\"B\"); }\n   catch (Exception e) { System.out.println(e.getMessage()); }\n   finally { System.out.println(\"C\"); }\n   System.out.println(\"D\");\n   ```\n   **A:** `A`, `B`, `C`, `D` on four lines.\n2. **What does `t(0)`, `t(1)`, `t(2)` return** for the tracing template in §10? **A:** `ABFE`, `ACFE`, `ADFE`.\n3. **What is printed?**\n   ```java\n   int count = 0;\n   for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\n       try { if (i == 1) throw new RuntimeException(); count += 10; }\n       catch (RuntimeException e) { count += 1; continue; }\n       finally { count += 100; }\n   }\n   System.out.println(count);\n   ```\n   **A:** `321` (110 + 101 + 110; finally runs even on `continue`).\n4. **What is printed?**\n   ```java\n   try {\n       try { throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"in\"); }\n       catch (NullPointerException e) { System.out.println(\"NPE\"); }\n       finally { System.out.println(\"F1\"); }\n       System.out.println(\"X\");\n   } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) { System.out.println(\"outer \" + e.getMessage()); }\n   ```\n   **A:** `F1` then `outer in`. The inner catch does not match; inner finally runs; `X` is skipped.\n5. **Find the bug:** `catch (Exception e) {...} catch (InputMismatchException e) {...}` **A:** Compile error: the second catch is unreachable. Put the specific one first.\n6. **Find the bug:** `public void read() { throw new IOException(\"x\"); }` **A:** Checked exception neither caught nor declared. Add `throws IOException` or a try/catch.\n7. **Write** an exception class `NegativeNumberException` with a no-arg constructor (default message \"Negative number!\") and a String constructor. **A:** see §5 pattern: `extends Exception`, `super(\"Negative number!\")`, `super(message)`.\n8. **Short theory:** Difference between checked and unchecked exceptions? Give two examples of each. **A:** Checked must be caught or declared (`IOException`, `FileNotFoundException`); unchecked need not (`InputMismatchException`, `ArithmeticException`, any `RuntimeException`, any `Error`).\n9. **Short theory:** In the slide 13 demo, where does the exception come from? **A:** `nextInt()` (it throws `InputMismatchException`).\n10. **Short theory:** Name the three possible paths through try–catch–finally. **A:** slide 41 list in §10.\n11. **What does `new Exception().getMessage()` return?** **A:** `null`.\n12. **Why must the catch block in an exception-controlled loop call `keyboard.nextLine()`?** **A:** To discard the bad input; otherwise `nextInt()` throws again forever.\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"What is \"throwing an exception\"?","a":"Signalling that something unusual happened, with `throw new SomeException(...)`."},{"q":"What is \"handling an exception\"?","a":"Running a catch block that deals with the thrown exception object."},{"q":"What happens to the rest of a try block after a throw?","a":"It is skipped; control jumps to the first matching catch block."},{"q":"What does `throw` take as its operand?","a":"An object of an exception class, usually `new ExceptionClass(message)`."},{"q":"How many parameters does a catch block have?","a":"Exactly one (the slides say \"at most one\", but zero is a syntax error)."},{"q":"Which method throws InputMismatchException in the slide 13 demo?","a":"`nextInt()` of Scanner, when the input is not a whole number."},{"q":"Which package holds InputMismatchException?","a":"`java.util`."},{"q":"Is InputMismatchException checked or unchecked?","a":"Unchecked; it descends from RuntimeException."},{"q":"In an exception-controlled loop, why call `keyboard.nextLine()` in the catch?","a":"To discard the bad token; otherwise nextInt fails again forever."},{"q":"Two properties every predefined exception class has?","a":"A constructor taking a String, and `getMessage()` returning that string."},{"q":"What does `getMessage()` return for an exception built with no message?","a":"`null` for library exceptions; your own no-arg constructor should pass a default string to super."},{"q":"Which package is Exception in, and does it need an import?","a":"`java.lang`; no import needed."},{"q":"What must a programmer-defined exception class extend?","a":"An existing exception class, usually `Exception`."},{"q":"Which two constructors should every custom exception have?","a":"A no-arg one calling `super(\"default message\")` and a String one calling `super(message)`."},{"q":"Why call `super(message)` in a custom exception constructor?","a":"So the inherited `getMessage()` returns that message."},{"q":"If a custom exception extends RuntimeException, is it checked?","a":"No, it is unchecked."},{"q":"How many exceptions can one execution of a try block throw?","a":"At most one; a throw ends the try block."},{"q":"In what order are catch blocks examined?","a":"Top to bottom; the first matching type runs, the rest are skipped."},{"q":"Why must a subclass exception be caught before its superclass?","a":"Otherwise the subclass catch is unreachable and javac reports \"has already been caught\"."},{"q":"What is a throws clause?","a":"A declaration in the method header that the method may throw an exception it does not catch, e.g. `throws IOException`."},{"q":"How do you declare two exceptions in a throws clause?","a":"Separate them with commas: `throws AnException, AnotherException`."},{"q":"State the Catch or Declare Rule.","a":"A checked exception must be caught in the same method or declared in that method's throws clause."},{"q":"What does declaring an exception do with the responsibility?","a":"It passes it to the calling method (\"pass the buck\")."},{"q":"Which classes are checked exceptions?","a":"Throwable, Exception and its subclasses, except RuntimeException and its subclasses."},{"q":"Which classes are unchecked?","a":"RuntimeException and its subclasses, and Error and its subclasses."},{"q":"What is the root class of everything that can be thrown?","a":"`Throwable` (not Exception; slide 17 says Exception)."},{"q":"Can an overriding method add a new checked exception to its throws clause?","a":"No; it may keep the same ones, a subset, or subclasses of them."},{"q":"What happens if an exception is never caught in a console program?","a":"The program ends with an error message naming the exception class and a stack trace."},{"q":"When does a finally block run?","a":"Always after try (and any catch), whether or not an exception was thrown or caught."},{"q":"What happens with an exception that no catch in the method matches, when there is a finally?","a":"finally runs, then the method ends and the exception propagates to the caller."},{"q":"Does finally run if the try block calls `System.exit(0)`?","a":"No; the JVM stops immediately."},{"q":"Does finally run if the try block executes `return`?","a":"Yes, finally runs before the method actually returns."},{"q":"What does `System.out.println(new Exception(\"boom\"))` print?","a":"`java.lang.Exception: boom`."},{"q":"In secondChance() (slide 25), how is a second zero denominator handled?","a":"Without an exception: it prints a message and calls System.exit(0)."}],"figs":[{"n":1,"src":"slides/W7e001.jpg"},{"n":2,"src":"slides/W7e002.jpg"},{"n":3,"src":"slides/W7e003.jpg"},{"n":4,"src":"slides/W7e004.jpg"},{"n":5,"src":"slides/W7e005.jpg"},{"n":6,"src":"slides/W7e006.jpg"},{"n":7,"src":"slides/W7e007.jpg"},{"n":8,"src":"slides/W7e008.jpg"},{"n":9,"src":"slides/W7e009.jpg"},{"n":10,"src":"slides/W7e010.jpg"},{"n":11,"src":"slides/W7e011.jpg"},{"n":12,"src":"slides/W7e012.jpg"},{"n":13,"src":"slides/W7e013.jpg"},{"n":14,"src":"slides/W7e014.jpg"},{"n":15,"src":"slides/W7e015.jpg"},{"n":16,"src":"slides/W7e016.jpg"},{"n":17,"src":"slides/W7e017.jpg"},{"n":18,"src":"slides/W7e018.jpg"},{"n":19,"src":"slides/W7e019.jpg"},{"n":20,"src":"slides/W7e020.jpg"},{"n":21,"src":"slides/W7e021.jpg"},{"n":22,"src":"slides/W7e022.jpg"},{"n":23,"src":"slides/W7e023.jpg"},{"n":24,"src":"slides/W7e024.jpg"},{"n":25,"src":"slides/W7e025.jpg"},{"n":26,"src":"slides/W7e026.jpg"},{"n":27,"src":"slides/W7e027.jpg"},{"n":28,"src":"slides/W7e028.jpg"},{"n":29,"src":"slides/W7e029.jpg"},{"n":30,"src":"slides/W7e030.jpg"},{"n":31,"src":"slides/W7e031.jpg"},{"n":32,"src":"slides/W7e032.jpg"},{"n":33,"src":"slides/W7e033.jpg"},{"n":34,"src":"slides/W7e034.jpg"},{"n":35,"src":"slides/W7e035.jpg"},{"n":36,"src":"slides/W7e036.jpg"},{"n":37,"src":"slides/W7e037.jpg"},{"n":38,"src":"slides/W7e038.jpg"},{"n":39,"src":"slides/W7e039.jpg"},{"n":40,"src":"slides/W7e040.jpg"},{"n":41,"src":"slides/W7e041.jpg"}],"exercises":[{"id":"e1","lang":"java","title":"A proper custom exception class","level":"warm","blurb":"Slides 22 and 28 say a programmer-defined exception needs **two constructors** that both start with `super(...)`, so the inherited `getMessage()` keeps working.","prompt":"Slides 22 and 28 say a programmer-defined exception needs **two constructors** that both start with `super(...)`, so the inherited `getMessage()` keeps working.\n\nWrite `class InvalidAgeException extends Exception` with:\n- `InvalidAgeException()` → message `\"Invalid age!\"`\n- `InvalidAgeException(String message)` → message `message`\n\nThe starter compiles but its String constructor throws the message away.","starter":"class InvalidAgeException extends Exception {\n    public InvalidAgeException() {\n        super(\"Invalid age!\");\n    }\n    public InvalidAgeException(String message) {\n        super();   // BUG: message is lost\n    }\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n        Exception a = new InvalidAgeException();\n        if (!\"Invalid age!\".equals(a.getMessage()))\n            throw new AssertionError(\"no-arg constructor: expected 'Invalid age!' but got \" + a.getMessage());\n        System.out.println(\"PASS default message\");\n        Exception b = new InvalidAgeException(\"Age -3 is negative\");\n        if (!\"Age -3 is negative\".equals(b.getMessage()))\n            throw new AssertionError(\"String constructor: expected 'Age -3 is negative' but got \" + b.getMessage());\n        System.out.println(\"PASS custom message\");\n        if (b instanceof RuntimeException)\n            throw new AssertionError(\"InvalidAgeException should be CHECKED (extend Exception, not RuntimeException)\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS checked exception\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class InvalidAgeException extends Exception {\n    public InvalidAgeException() {\n        super(\"Invalid age!\");\n    }\n    public InvalidAgeException(String message) {\n        super(message);\n    }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e2","lang":"java","title":"Parse or fall back","level":"warm","blurb":"`Integer.parseInt` throws the unchecked `NumberFormatException` for text like `\"4x\"`.","prompt":"`Integer.parseInt` throws the unchecked `NumberFormatException` for text like `\"4x\"`.\n\nWrite `static int parseOr(String s, int fallback)` in class `Parse`: return the parsed int, or `fallback` if `s` is not a valid int (also if `s` is `null`; `Integer.parseInt(null)` throws `NumberFormatException` too).\n\nExamples: `parseOr(\"42\", 0)` → 42, `parseOr(\" 7 \", -1)` → -1 (spaces are not trimmed), `parseOr(\"4x\", -1)` → -1.","starter":"class Parse {\n    static int parseOr(String s, int fallback) {\n        return Integer.parseInt(s);\n    }\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        if (Parse.parseOr(\"42\", 0) != 42) throw new AssertionError(\"parseOr(\\\"42\\\", 0) should be 42\");\n        if (Parse.parseOr(\"-15\", 0) != -15) throw new AssertionError(\"parseOr(\\\"-15\\\", 0) should be -15\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS valid numbers\");\n        int r;\n        try { r = Parse.parseOr(\"4x\", -1); }\n        catch (NumberFormatException e) { throw new AssertionError(\"parseOr(\\\"4x\\\", -1) let NumberFormatException escape; catch it\"); }\n        if (r != -1) throw new AssertionError(\"parseOr(\\\"4x\\\", -1) should be -1, got \" + r);\n        if (Parse.parseOr(\" 7 \", -1) != -1) throw new AssertionError(\"parseOr(\\\" 7 \\\", -1) should be -1\");\n        if (Parse.parseOr(\"\", 99) != 99) throw new AssertionError(\"parseOr(\\\"\\\", 99) should be 99\");\n        if (Parse.parseOr(null, 5) != 5) throw new AssertionError(\"parseOr(null, 5) should be 5\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS fallbacks\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Parse {\n    static int parseOr(String s, int fallback) {\n        try {\n            return Integer.parseInt(s);\n        } catch (NumberFormatException e) {\n            return fallback;\n        }\n    }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e3","lang":"java","title":"Bank account with a checked exception","level":"core","blurb":"Write two classes:","prompt":"Write two classes:\n- `class InsufficientFundsException extends Exception` with a no-arg constructor (message `\"Insufficient funds\"`) and a `String` constructor.\n- `class Account` with `Account(double balance)`, `double getBalance()` and\n  `void withdraw(double amount) throws InsufficientFundsException`:\n  - `amount <= 0` → throw `new IllegalArgumentException(\"Amount must be positive\")` (unchecked; balance unchanged);\n  - `amount > balance` → throw `new InsufficientFundsException(\"Balance \" + balance + \", requested \" + amount)` (balance unchanged);\n  - otherwise subtract.\n\nExample: balance 100.0, `withdraw(150.0)` → message `Balance 100.0, requested 150.0`.","starter":"class InsufficientFundsException extends Exception {\n    public InsufficientFundsException() { super(\"Insufficient funds\"); }\n    public InsufficientFundsException(String message) { super(message); }\n}\n\nclass Account {\n    private double balance;\n    public Account(double balance) { this.balance = balance; }\n    public double getBalance() { return balance; }\n    public void withdraw(double amount) throws InsufficientFundsException {\n        balance = balance - amount;   // no checks yet\n    }\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n        Account a = new Account(100.0);\n        a.withdraw(30.0);\n        if (a.getBalance() != 70.0) throw new AssertionError(\"after withdraw(30) balance should be 70.0, got \" + a.getBalance());\n        System.out.println(\"PASS normal withdraw\");\n        Account b = new Account(100.0);\n        boolean thrown = false;\n        try { b.withdraw(150.0); }\n        catch (InsufficientFundsException e) {\n            thrown = true;\n            if (!\"Balance 100.0, requested 150.0\".equals(e.getMessage()))\n                throw new AssertionError(\"wrong message: \" + e.getMessage());\n        }\n        if (!thrown) throw new AssertionError(\"withdraw(150) on balance 100 must throw InsufficientFundsException\");\n        if (b.getBalance() != 100.0) throw new AssertionError(\"balance must not change when the withdrawal fails, got \" + b.getBalance());\n        System.out.println(\"PASS insufficient funds\");\n        for (double bad : new double[]{0.0, -5.0}) {\n            thrown = false;\n            try { b.withdraw(bad); }\n            catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {\n                thrown = true;\n                if (!\"Amount must be positive\".equals(e.getMessage())) throw new AssertionError(\"wrong message: \" + e.getMessage());\n            }\n            if (!thrown) throw new AssertionError(\"withdraw(\" + bad + \") must throw IllegalArgumentException\");\n        }\n        if (b.getBalance() != 100.0) throw new AssertionError(\"balance must not change on a bad amount\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS bad amounts\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class InsufficientFundsException extends Exception {\n    public InsufficientFundsException() { super(\"Insufficient funds\"); }\n    public InsufficientFundsException(String message) { super(message); }\n}\n\nclass Account {\n    private double balance;\n    public Account(double balance) { this.balance = balance; }\n    public double getBalance() { return balance; }\n    public void withdraw(double amount) throws InsufficientFundsException {\n        if (amount <= 0)\n            throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Amount must be positive\");\n        if (amount > balance)\n            throw new InsufficientFundsException(\"Balance \" + balance + \", requested \" + amount);\n        balance = balance - amount;\n    }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e4","lang":"java","title":"Exception-controlled loop without the keyboard","level":"core","blurb":"Slide 14 loops until `nextInt()` succeeds. Do the same on any `Scanner`.","prompt":"Slide 14 loops until `nextInt()` succeeds. Do the same on any `Scanner`.\n\nWrite `static int firstInt(java.util.Scanner in)` in class `Reader`:\n- try `in.nextInt()`; on `InputMismatchException` discard the bad token with `in.next()` and try again;\n- if the input runs out (`NoSuchElementException`), return `-1`.\n\nHint: `InputMismatchException` is a subclass of `NoSuchElementException`, so catch order matters.\n\nExamples: tokens `\"abc 4x 42 7\"` → 42 (and a second call returns 7); `\"x y\"` → -1.","starter":"class Reader {\n    static int firstInt(java.util.Scanner in) {\n        return in.nextInt();\n    }\n}","test":"import java.util.Scanner;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner s = new Scanner(\"abc 4x 42 7\");\n        int r;\n        try { r = Reader.firstInt(s); }\n        catch (RuntimeException e) { throw new AssertionError(\"firstInt let \" + e.getClass().getSimpleName() + \" escape on \\\"abc 4x 42 7\\\"\"); }\n        if (r != 42) throw new AssertionError(\"first call should return 42, got \" + r);\n        if (Reader.firstInt(s) != 7) throw new AssertionError(\"second call should return 7 (the next int)\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS skips bad tokens\");\n        int end;\n        try { end = Reader.firstInt(s); }\n        catch (RuntimeException e) { throw new AssertionError(\"at end of input return -1 instead of throwing \" + e.getClass().getSimpleName()); }\n        if (end != -1) throw new AssertionError(\"at end of input expected -1, got \" + end);\n        if (Reader.firstInt(new Scanner(\"x y\")) != -1) throw new AssertionError(\"\\\"x y\\\" has no int: expected -1\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS end of input\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Reader {\n    static int firstInt(java.util.Scanner in) {\n        while (true) {\n            try {\n                return in.nextInt();\n            } catch (java.util.InputMismatchException e) {   // subclass first\n                in.next();                          // discard the bad token\n            } catch (java.util.NoSuchElementException e) {   // input ran out\n                return -1;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e5","lang":"java","title":"finally always runs","level":"core","blurb":"Write `static boolean attempt(Runnable task, java.util.List<String> log)` in class `Tracker`:","prompt":"Write `static boolean attempt(Runnable task, java.util.List<String> log)` in class `Tracker`:\n- add `\"start\"` to the log, then run `task.run()`;\n- if it finishes, return `true`;\n- if it throws a `RuntimeException e`, add `\"fail: \" + e.getClass().getSimpleName()` and return `false`;\n- in **every** case add `\"end\"` as the last entry (use `finally`).\n\nExample: a task doing `1/0` gives log `[start, fail: ArithmeticException, end]` and returns false.","starter":"class Tracker {\n    static boolean attempt(Runnable task, java.util.List<String> log) {\n        log.add(\"start\");\n        try {\n            task.run();\n            log.add(\"end\");\n            return true;\n        } catch (RuntimeException e) {\n            log.add(\"fail: \" + e.getClass().getSimpleName());\n            return false;\n        }\n    }\n}","test":"import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        List<String> log = new ArrayList<>();\n        boolean ok = Tracker.attempt(() -> {}, log);\n        if (!ok || !log.equals(List.of(\"start\", \"end\"))) throw new AssertionError(\"successful task: expected true and [start, end], got \" + ok + \" \" + log);\n        System.out.println(\"PASS success path\");\n        log = new ArrayList<>();\n        ok = Tracker.attempt(() -> { int z = 0; int q = 1 / z; }, log);\n        if (ok || !log.equals(List.of(\"start\", \"fail: ArithmeticException\", \"end\")))\n            throw new AssertionError(\"1/0 task: expected false and [start, fail: ArithmeticException, end], got \" + ok + \" \" + log);\n        log = new ArrayList<>();\n        ok = Tracker.attempt(() -> { int[] a = new int[1]; a[3] = 1; }, log);\n        if (ok || !log.equals(List.of(\"start\", \"fail: ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException\", \"end\")))\n            throw new AssertionError(\"bad index task: got \" + ok + \" \" + log);\n        System.out.println(\"PASS failure path\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Tracker {\n    static boolean attempt(Runnable task, java.util.List<String> log) {\n        log.add(\"start\");\n        try {\n            task.run();\n            return true;\n        } catch (RuntimeException e) {\n            log.add(\"fail: \" + e.getClass().getSimpleName());\n            return false;\n        } finally {\n            log.add(\"end\");\n        }\n    }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e6","lang":"java","title":"Division demo, method version","level":"hard","blurb":"Rebuild Display 9.5 (slides 22–25) as testable methods. Write:","prompt":"Rebuild Display 9.5 (slides 22–25) as testable methods. Write:\n- `class DivisionByZeroException extends Exception` exactly as on slide 22 (default message `\"Division by Zero!\"`, plus a String constructor);\n- `class Divider` with\n  - `static double divide(int a, int b) throws DivisionByZeroException` — throws when `b == 0`, else returns `a / (double) b`;\n  - `static String safeDivide(String a, String b)` — parses both strings, calls `divide`, and returns:\n    - `a + \"/\" + b + \" = \" + quotient` on success (use the parsed ints, e.g. `\"7/2 = 3.5\"`),\n    - the exception's message if it is a `DivisionByZeroException`,\n    - `\"Bad number: \" + text` (the first string that fails to parse) on `NumberFormatException`.","starter":"class DivisionByZeroException extends Exception {\n    public DivisionByZeroException() { super(\"Division by Zero!\"); }\n    public DivisionByZeroException(String message) { super(message); }\n}\n\nclass Divider {\n    static double divide(int a, int b) throws DivisionByZeroException {\n        return a / (double) b;\n    }\n    static String safeDivide(String a, String b) {\n        return \"\";\n    }\n}","test":"public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n        if (Divider.divide(7, 2) != 3.5) throw new AssertionError(\"divide(7,2) should be 3.5\");\n        boolean thrown = false;\n        try { Divider.divide(5, 0); }\n        catch (DivisionByZeroException e) { thrown = \"Division by Zero!\".equals(e.getMessage()); }\n        if (!thrown) throw new AssertionError(\"divide(5,0) must throw DivisionByZeroException with message 'Division by Zero!'\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS divide\");\n        String r = Divider.safeDivide(\"7\", \"2\");\n        if (!\"7/2 = 3.5\".equals(r)) throw new AssertionError(\"safeDivide(\\\"7\\\",\\\"2\\\") expected '7/2 = 3.5' got '\" + r + \"'\");\n        r = Divider.safeDivide(\"9\", \"0\");\n        if (!\"Division by Zero!\".equals(r)) throw new AssertionError(\"safeDivide(\\\"9\\\",\\\"0\\\") expected 'Division by Zero!' got '\" + r + \"'\");\n        r = Divider.safeDivide(\"nine\", \"3\");\n        if (!\"Bad number: nine\".equals(r)) throw new AssertionError(\"safeDivide(\\\"nine\\\",\\\"3\\\") expected 'Bad number: nine' got '\" + r + \"'\");\n        r = Divider.safeDivide(\"4\", \"x2\");\n        if (!\"Bad number: x2\".equals(r)) throw new AssertionError(\"safeDivide(\\\"4\\\",\\\"x2\\\") expected 'Bad number: x2' got '\" + r + \"'\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS safeDivide\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class DivisionByZeroException extends Exception {\n    public DivisionByZeroException() { super(\"Division by Zero!\"); }\n    public DivisionByZeroException(String message) { super(message); }\n}\n\nclass Divider {\n    static double divide(int a, int b) throws DivisionByZeroException {\n        if (b == 0)\n            throw new DivisionByZeroException();\n        return a / (double) b;\n    }\n    static String safeDivide(String a, String b) {\n        String current = a;\n        try {\n            int x = Integer.parseInt(a);\n            current = b;\n            int y = Integer.parseInt(b);\n            return x + \"/\" + y + \" = \" + divide(x, y);\n        } catch (DivisionByZeroException e) {\n            return e.getMessage();\n        } catch (NumberFormatException e) {\n            return \"Bad number: \" + current;\n        }\n    }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e7","lang":"java","title":"Translate and propagate","level":"hard","blurb":"Validation code often turns a low-level exception into a meaningful checked one and lets the caller decide.","prompt":"Validation code often turns a low-level exception into a meaningful checked one and lets the caller decide.\n\nWrite:\n- `class AgeException extends Exception` with a no-arg constructor (message `\"Bad age\"`) and a String constructor;\n- `class Ages` with\n  - `static int parseAge(String s) throws AgeException`:\n    `\"Not a number: \" + s` if `Integer.parseInt` fails; `\"Out of range: \" + n` if `n < 0` or `n > 150`; otherwise return `n`.\n  - `static int countValid(String[] items, java.util.List<String> errors)`: calls `parseAge` for each item, returns how many were valid, and appends each failure's `getMessage()` to `errors` in order.\n\nExample: `{\"20\", \"abc\", \"-4\", \"150\", \"151\"}` → returns 2, errors `[Not a number: abc, Out of range: -4, Out of range: 151]`.","starter":"class AgeException extends Exception {\n    public AgeException() { super(\"Bad age\"); }\n    public AgeException(String message) { super(message); }\n}\n\nclass Ages {\n    static int parseAge(String s) throws AgeException {\n        return Integer.parseInt(s);\n    }\n    static int countValid(String[] items, java.util.List<String> errors) {\n        return items.length;\n    }\n}","test":"import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n        if (Ages.parseAge(\"37\") != 37) throw new AssertionError(\"parseAge(\\\"37\\\") should be 37\");\n        String msg = null;\n        try { Ages.parseAge(\"abc\"); } catch (AgeException e) { msg = e.getMessage(); }\n        catch (NumberFormatException e) { throw new AssertionError(\"parseAge must turn NumberFormatException into AgeException\"); }\n        if (!\"Not a number: abc\".equals(msg)) throw new AssertionError(\"parseAge(\\\"abc\\\") message expected 'Not a number: abc', got \" + msg);\n        msg = null;\n        try { Ages.parseAge(\"200\"); } catch (AgeException e) { msg = e.getMessage(); }\n        if (!\"Out of range: 200\".equals(msg)) throw new AssertionError(\"parseAge(\\\"200\\\") message expected 'Out of range: 200', got \" + msg);\n        System.out.println(\"PASS parseAge\");\n        List<String> errors = new ArrayList<>();\n        int n = Ages.countValid(new String[]{\"20\", \"abc\", \"-4\", \"150\", \"151\"}, errors);\n        if (n != 2) throw new AssertionError(\"countValid should return 2, got \" + n);\n        if (!errors.equals(List.of(\"Not a number: abc\", \"Out of range: -4\", \"Out of range: 151\")))\n            throw new AssertionError(\"errors list wrong: \" + errors);\n        System.out.println(\"PASS countValid\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class AgeException extends Exception {\n    public AgeException() { super(\"Bad age\"); }\n    public AgeException(String message) { super(message); }\n}\n\nclass Ages {\n    static int parseAge(String s) throws AgeException {\n        int n;\n        try {\n            n = Integer.parseInt(s);\n        } catch (NumberFormatException e) {\n            throw new AgeException(\"Not a number: \" + s);\n        }\n        if (n < 0 || n > 150)\n            throw new AgeException(\"Out of range: \" + n);\n        return n;\n    }\n    static int countValid(String[] items, java.util.List<String> errors) {\n        int valid = 0;\n        for (String item : items) {\n            try {\n                parseAge(item);\n                valid++;\n            } catch (AgeException e) {\n                errors.add(e.getMessage());\n            }\n        }\n        return valid;\n    }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."}]},{"id":"08","title":"W7 · File I/O (ch. 10)","slides":"Week 7 · 36 slides","md":"# 08 — Week 7 · File I/O (Savitch ch. 10, 36 slides)\n\n_Ingested 15.11.2026 from lectures/Week7_Savich_chap10_FileIO_ko.pptx (PDF copy in lectures/), Savitch Absolute Java 6e ch. 10 slides by Williams/Mock, updated by Dr. Kazeem Oladejo, November 2026. Code slides 20–23 (Display 10.3, Scanner) and 27–29 (Display 10.7, BufferedReader) are images; they were rendered and read. The deck covers **text files only**: binary files appear only as a definition on slide 5 (no ObjectInputStream/ObjectOutputStream, no Serializable). Every code example below was compiled and run on JDK 26._\n\n> **Instructor items in this deck**\n> - **Slide 13, DEMO:** \"Writing to the file: QuickFoxVsLazyDog.txt\" (live demo of PrintWriter; code not on the slide).\n> - **Slide 36, Next Class:** **Week 10 = Polymorphism and Abstract Classes — \"refresh Inheritance ahead of class!\"** (Weeks 7–9: Quiz 1, Midterm, Lab; see 07 header.)\n> - This deck shares Week 7 with the exceptions deck (07): file I/O is the main *use* of checked exceptions, so expect them combined in questions.\n\n## 1. Streams (slides 2–3)\n\n- A **stream** is an object that lets data flow between a program and a device or file.\n- **Input stream:** data flows *into* the program (`System.in`, keyboard; or a file).\n- **Output stream:** data flows *out of* the program (`System.out`, screen; or a file).\n\n## 2. Text files vs binary files (slides 4–5)\n\n| | Text file | Binary file |\n|---|---|---|\n| Meant for | humans; editable in a text editor | programs only |\n| Content | characters (slides say \"ASCII\") | raw binary data |\n| Portability | same on all computers | usually machine/language specific; **Java binary files are platform independent** |\n| Advantage | portable, readable | more efficient to process |\n\n## 3. Writing a text file with PrintWriter (slides 6–11, 14)\n\nImports (all in `java.io`):\n\n```java\nimport java.io.PrintWriter;\nimport java.io.FileOutputStream;\nimport java.io.FileNotFoundException;\n```\n\nPattern the slides teach (declare outside the try, initialise to `null`):\n\n```java\nPrintWriter outputStream = null;\ntry {\n    outputStream = new PrintWriter(new FileOutputStream(\"stuff.txt\"));\n} catch (FileNotFoundException e) {\n    System.out.println(\"Error opening the file stuff.txt.\");\n    System.exit(0);\n}\noutputStream.println(\"The quick brown fox\");\noutputStream.print(\"jumps over \");\noutputStream.println(\"the lazy dog.\");\noutputStream.close();          // ALWAYS close (see Errata: unclosed = lost data)\n```\n\nKey facts:\n- `print`, `println`, `printf` work exactly like `System.out`'s, but go to the file.\n- Opening for writing **erases** an existing file's contents. A missing file is **created** empty.\n- Opening can throw **`FileNotFoundException`** (checked). For output it really means \"the file could not be created\" (e.g. the folder does not exist). For input it means \"no such file\".\n- **Close** the stream when done: it flushes buffered text to disk and releases resources.\n- **A file has two names** (slide 14): the real OS file name (`\"stuff.txt\"`, a String, used once to connect) and the **stream variable name** (`outputStream`, used everywhere in the program).\n- **File names** (slide 12) follow OS rules, not Java identifier rules; the `.txt` suffix means nothing to Java.\n\n## 4. IOException and friends (slides 15–16)\n\n- `IOException` is the root of many I/O exceptions; `FileNotFoundException extends IOException`.\n- They are all **checked** → catch or declare.\n- From `Scanner`, by contrast: `NoSuchElementException`, `InputMismatchException`, `IllegalStateException` are **unchecked**.\n\n| Exception | When (verified) | Checked? |\n|---|---|---|\n| `FileNotFoundException` | open a missing file for reading; cannot create a file for writing | yes |\n| `IOException` | `BufferedReader.readLine()/read()/close()` may throw it | yes |\n| `NoSuchElementException` | `scanner.nextInt()` / `nextLine()` past the end of the file | no |\n| `InputMismatchException` | `nextInt()` when the next token is not an int | no |\n| `IllegalStateException` | using a Scanner after `close()` (\"Scanner closed\") | no |\n\n## 5. Pitfall: a try block is a block (slides 10, 17)\n\nA variable declared **inside** a try block is local to it. Using it after the block → compile error `cannot find symbol`.\n\nDeclaring it outside **without** initialising also fails: `variable o might not have been initialized` (the compiler sees that the catch path leaves it unset). That is why the slides write `PrintWriter outputStream = null;` / `Scanner inputStream = null;`.\n\n## 6. Appending (slide 18)\n\nPass `true` as the second argument of **FileOutputStream**:\n\n```java\noutputStream = new PrintWriter(new FileOutputStream(\"log.txt\", true));\n```\n\nNew text goes after the old text. Without `true` (or with `false`) the file is overwritten. Verified: writing `one`, then appending `two=2` and `three` gives three lines.\n\n## 7. Reading a text file with Scanner (slides 19–23)\n\nReplace `System.in` with a stream connected to the file. All Scanner methods (`nextInt`, `nextLine`, `hasNextInt`, ...) behave the same.\n\n```java\nimport java.util.Scanner;\nimport java.io.FileInputStream;\nimport java.io.FileNotFoundException;\n\nScanner inputStream = null;\ntry {\n    inputStream = new Scanner(new FileInputStream(\"morestuff.txt\"));\n} catch (FileNotFoundException e) {\n    System.out.println(\"File morestuff.txt was not found\");\n    System.out.println(\"or could not be opened.\");\n    System.exit(0);\n}\nint n1 = inputStream.nextInt();\nint n2 = inputStream.nextInt();\nint n3 = inputStream.nextInt();\ninputStream.nextLine();                 // \"To go to the next line\"\nString line = inputStream.nextLine();\nSystem.out.println(n1 + \", \" + n2 + \", \" + n3);   // 1, 2, and 3 on the slide\nSystem.out.println(line);\ninputStream.close();\n```\n\nFile `morestuff.txt` on slide 22:\n```\n1 2\n3 4\nEat my shorts.\n```\nOutput: `1, 2, and 3` and `Eat my shorts.`\n\n**Trace this carefully (exam trap):** after reading `3`, the scanner sits just after `3` on line 2. `nextLine()` returns the **rest of line 2**, which is `\" 4\"`; that `4` is thrown away. The next `nextLine()` returns `Eat my shorts.` (verified).\n\nLoop to end of file with Scanner: `while (in.hasNextLine()) ...` or `while (in.hasNextInt()) sum += in.nextInt();`. Reading past the end throws `NoSuchElementException`.\n\n## 8. Reading a text file with BufferedReader (slides 24–31)\n\nImports: `BufferedReader`, `FileReader`, `FileNotFoundException`, `IOException` (all java.io).\n\n```java\ntry {\n    BufferedReader inputStream = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(\"morestuff2.txt\"));\n    String line = inputStream.readLine();\n    System.out.println(\"The first line read from the file is:\");\n    System.out.println(line);\n    line = inputStream.readLine();\n    System.out.println(\"The second line read from the file is:\");\n    System.out.println(line);\n    inputStream.close();\n} catch (FileNotFoundException e) {        // subclass first\n    System.out.println(\"File morestuff2.txt was not found\");\n    System.out.println(\"or could not be opened.\");\n} catch (IOException e) {                  // superclass second\n    System.out.println(\"Error reading from morestuff2.txt.\");\n}\n```\n\nWith `morestuff2.txt` = `1 2 3` / `Jack jump over` / `the candle stick.` the output shows lines 1 and 2 only (`1 2 3`, `Jack jump over`).\n\n- **Two methods only:** `readLine()` returns a `String`; `read()` returns one character **as an `int`** → cast: `char next = (char) inputStream.read();`\n- **No number-reading methods.** Read a line, then convert: `Integer.parseInt(line)`, `Double.parseDouble(line)`. Several numbers on a line: split into tokens (`StringTokenizer` on the slide, or `line.split(\" \")`), then parse each.\n- **End of file** (slide 31): `readLine()` returns **`null`**; `read()` returns **`-1`**.\n- Two catches are needed because `FileReader` throws `FileNotFoundException` and `readLine()` throws `IOException`. The order must be subclass first.\n\nStandard loops (verified):\n\n```java\nString line;\nwhile ((line = br.readLine()) != null) { ... }   // every line\n\nint c;\nwhile ((c = br.read()) != -1) { char ch = (char) c; ... }   // every character, including '\\n'\n```\n\n## 9. Scanner vs BufferedReader (summary)\n\n| | Scanner | BufferedReader |\n|---|---|---|\n| Wrap | `new Scanner(new FileInputStream(name))` | `new BufferedReader(new FileReader(name))` |\n| Read numbers | `nextInt()`, `nextDouble()` | no; `readLine()` + `Integer.parseInt` |\n| Read a line | `nextLine()` | `readLine()` |\n| One char | no | `read()` returns int, cast to char |\n| End of file | `hasNextLine()`/`hasNextInt()` false; `next...()` throws NoSuchElementException | `readLine()` → null, `read()` → -1 |\n| Checked exceptions | only when opening (FileNotFoundException) | opening + every read (IOException) |\n| Package | java.util (Scanner) | java.io |\n\n## 10. Path names (slides 32–35)\n\n- A plain file name means \"in the directory where the program is run\".\n- **Full (absolute) path:** from the root. **Relative path:** from the program's directory.\n- UNIX: `\"/user/sallyz/data/data.txt\"`.\n- Windows: `C:\\dataFiles\\goodData\\data.txt` must be written `\"C:\\\\dataFiles\\\\goodData\\\\data.txt\"` in Java, because `\\` starts an escape sequence. (Java also accepts forward slashes on Windows: `\"C:/dataFiles/goodData/data.txt\"`.)\n\n## 11. Errata and modern-Java notes\n\n| Slide | What it says | What is correct | How verified |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 11 | \"If the program does not close the file before the program ends, Java will close it automatically\" | **Unflushed text is lost.** A `PrintWriter` that is never closed (or flushed) leaves the file **empty**. Always `close()` (or `flush()`). | Program writes `println(\"hello\")` without close → file size 0 bytes; with `flush()` → 2 bytes |\n| 7 | \"The class PrintWriter has no constructor that takes a file name\" | True in old Java only. Since Java 5, `new PrintWriter(\"out.txt\")` works (truncates the file, throws FileNotFoundException). The slide's `new PrintWriter(new FileOutputStream(name))` still works and is needed for **append** mode. | javac/java |\n| 25 | \"Like PrintWriter and **Scanner**, BufferedReader has no constructor that takes a file name\" | `new Scanner(\"data.txt\")` compiles, but it scans the **string \"data.txt\" itself**, not the file. Use `new Scanner(new File(\"data.txt\"))` or `new Scanner(new FileInputStream(\"data.txt\"))`. Classic bug. | `new Scanner(\"data.txt\").next()` returned `data.txt` |\n| 4 | Text files \"can also be called ASCII files\" | Modern Java writes text in the platform default charset, which is **UTF-8** since JDK 18. ASCII is a subset of it. | `Charset.defaultCharset()` = UTF-8 |\n| 26 | \"readLIne\" | typo for `readLine` | — |\n| 30 | Use `StringTokenizer` for several numbers on a line | Still works but is a legacy class; `line.split(\" \")` (or `split(\"\\\\s+\")`) is the modern way. | both run |\n\nBeyond the slides: **try-with-resources** closes files automatically, even if an exception is thrown:\n\n```java\ntry (PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(new FileOutputStream(\"a.txt\", true))) {\n    out.println(\"appended line\");\n}   // out.close() called here automatically\n```\n\n## Likely exam questions\n\n1. **What is printed?** File `morestuff.txt` holds `1 2` / `3 4` / `Eat my shorts.`; code reads `nextInt()` three times, then `nextLine()` twice and prints the second line. **A:** `Eat my shorts.` (the first `nextLine()` returns `\" 4\"`).\n2. **What is in `log.txt`?** Write `one` (normal open), then open with `new FileOutputStream(\"log.txt\", true)` and `println(\"two\")`, then open with `new FileOutputStream(\"log.txt\")` and `println(\"three\")`. **A:** only `three` (the last open overwrites).\n3. **What does `readLine()` return at end of file? What does `read()` return?** **A:** `null`; `-1`.\n4. **Find the bug:**\n   ```java\n   try { PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(new FileOutputStream(\"a.txt\")); }\n   catch (FileNotFoundException e) { System.exit(0); }\n   out.println(\"hi\");\n   ```\n   **A:** `out` is local to the try block → `cannot find symbol`. Declare `PrintWriter out = null;` before the try.\n5. **Find the bug:** catch `IOException` before `FileNotFoundException`. **A:** compile error, the second catch is unreachable; swap them.\n6. **Find the bug:** `Scanner in = new Scanner(\"scores.txt\"); int x = in.nextInt();` **A:** scans the text \"scores.txt\", throws InputMismatchException; use `new Scanner(new File(\"scores.txt\"))`.\n7. **Write a method** `int sumFile(String name) throws IOException` that adds one integer per line using BufferedReader. **A:**\n   ```java\n   static int sumFile(String name) throws IOException {\n       BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(name));\n       int sum = 0;\n       String line;\n       while ((line = br.readLine()) != null)\n           sum += Integer.parseInt(line.trim());\n       br.close();\n       return sum;\n   }\n   ```\n8. **Short theory:** Why does a program get `FileNotFoundException` when *writing*? **A:** The file could not be created (missing folder, no permission).\n9. **Short theory:** Text vs binary files: two differences. **A:** Text is human-readable and portable; binary is read by programs and more efficient. Java binary files are platform independent.\n10. **Short theory:** What are the two names of a file? **A:** The OS file name and the stream (variable) name.\n11. **How do you write the Windows path `C:\\data\\a.txt` as a Java string?** **A:** `\"C:\\\\data\\\\a.txt\"`.\n12. **Why should the program call `close()`?** **A:** To flush buffered output to disk and free resources; without it the text may never reach the file.\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"What is a stream?","a":"An object that lets data flow between a program and an I/O device or file."},{"q":"Input stream vs output stream?","a":"Input: data flows into the program. Output: data flows out of the program."},{"q":"Which stream connects to the keyboard, and which to the screen?","a":"`System.in` (keyboard), `System.out` (screen)."},{"q":"What is a text file?","a":"A file of characters meant to be read by humans and editable with a text editor."},{"q":"What is a binary file?","a":"A file of binary data meant to be read by programs; more efficient to process than text."},{"q":"What is special about Java binary files?","a":"They are platform independent, unlike most binary files."},{"q":"Which class writes to a text file in the slides?","a":"`PrintWriter`, with print, println and printf."},{"q":"How does the slide open a text file for writing?","a":"`new PrintWriter(new FileOutputStream(\"name.txt\"))`."},{"q":"What happens if you open an existing file for writing (no append)?","a":"Its old contents are erased."},{"q":"What happens if you open a non-existent file for writing?","a":"A new empty file is created."},{"q":"What exception can opening a file throw, and is it checked?","a":"`FileNotFoundException`; checked (subclass of IOException)."},{"q":"What does FileNotFoundException mean when opening a file for output?","a":"The file could not be created."},{"q":"Why declare the PrintWriter variable outside the try block and set it to null?","a":"A variable declared inside the block is local to it; null avoids \"might not have been initialized\"."},{"q":"How do you open a text file for appending?","a":"`new PrintWriter(new FileOutputStream(\"name.txt\", true))`."},{"q":"What happens to unwritten output if a PrintWriter is never closed?","a":"It is lost; the file can stay empty (the slide's claim of automatic closing is wrong)."},{"q":"What are the two names of a file in a program?","a":"The real OS file name and the stream variable name."},{"q":"Does the \".txt\" suffix mean anything to Java?","a":"No; the file name is just a String following OS rules."},{"q":"What is the root class of many I/O exceptions?","a":"`IOException`."},{"q":"Name three unchecked exceptions Scanner can throw.","a":"NoSuchElementException, InputMismatchException, IllegalStateException."},{"q":"How do you read a text file with Scanner?","a":"`new Scanner(new FileInputStream(\"name.txt\"))` (or `new Scanner(new File(...))`)."},{"q":"What does `new Scanner(\"data.txt\")` read?","a":"The literal string \"data.txt\", not the file."},{"q":"File \"1 2 / 3 4 / Eat my shorts.\": after three nextInt() calls, what does nextLine() return?","a":"\" 4\", the rest of line 2."},{"q":"How do you open a file with BufferedReader?","a":"`new BufferedReader(new FileReader(\"name.txt\"))`."},{"q":"Which two reading methods does BufferedReader have?","a":"`readLine()` (a String) and `read()` (one char as an int)."},{"q":"How do you get a char from BufferedReader's read()?","a":"Cast: `char next = (char) inputStream.read();`"},{"q":"How do you read a number with BufferedReader?","a":"readLine(), then Integer.parseInt or Double.parseDouble."},{"q":"How can you split a line holding several numbers?","a":"StringTokenizer (slides) or `line.split(\" \")`, then parse each token."},{"q":"What does readLine() return at end of file?","a":"`null`."},{"q":"What does read() return at end of file?","a":"`-1`."},{"q":"Which checked exceptions does the BufferedReader demo catch, and in what order?","a":"FileNotFoundException first, then IOException."},{"q":"Where does Java look for a plain file name like \"data.txt\"?","a":"In the directory where the program is run."},{"q":"Full path vs relative path?","a":"Full starts at the root directory; relative starts at the program's directory."},{"q":"How do you write C:\\dataFiles\\data.txt as a Java string?","a":"\"C:\\\\dataFiles\\\\data.txt\" (each backslash doubled)."},{"q":"What does try-with-resources do?","a":"Closes the resource automatically at the end of the try, even after an exception."}],"figs":[{"n":1,"src":"slides/W7f001.jpg"},{"n":2,"src":"slides/W7f002.jpg"},{"n":3,"src":"slides/W7f003.jpg"},{"n":4,"src":"slides/W7f004.jpg"},{"n":5,"src":"slides/W7f005.jpg"},{"n":6,"src":"slides/W7f006.jpg"},{"n":7,"src":"slides/W7f007.jpg"},{"n":8,"src":"slides/W7f008.jpg"},{"n":9,"src":"slides/W7f009.jpg"},{"n":10,"src":"slides/W7f010.jpg"},{"n":11,"src":"slides/W7f011.jpg"},{"n":12,"src":"slides/W7f012.jpg"},{"n":13,"src":"slides/W7f013.jpg"},{"n":14,"src":"slides/W7f014.jpg"},{"n":15,"src":"slides/W7f015.jpg"},{"n":16,"src":"slides/W7f016.jpg"},{"n":17,"src":"slides/W7f017.jpg"},{"n":18,"src":"slides/W7f018.jpg"},{"n":19,"src":"slides/W7f019.jpg"},{"n":20,"src":"slides/W7f020.jpg"},{"n":21,"src":"slides/W7f021.jpg"},{"n":22,"src":"slides/W7f022.jpg"},{"n":23,"src":"slides/W7f023.jpg"},{"n":24,"src":"slides/W7f024.jpg"},{"n":25,"src":"slides/W7f025.jpg"},{"n":26,"src":"slides/W7f026.jpg"},{"n":27,"src":"slides/W7f027.jpg"},{"n":28,"src":"slides/W7f028.jpg"},{"n":29,"src":"slides/W7f029.jpg"},{"n":30,"src":"slides/W7f030.jpg"},{"n":31,"src":"slides/W7f031.jpg"},{"n":32,"src":"slides/W7f032.jpg"},{"n":33,"src":"slides/W7f033.jpg"},{"n":34,"src":"slides/W7f034.jpg"},{"n":35,"src":"slides/W7f035.jpg"},{"n":36,"src":"slides/W7f036.jpg"}],"exercises":[{"id":"e1","lang":"java","title":"Write lines, and close!","level":"warm","blurb":"Write `static void writeLines(String fileName, String[] lines) throws FileNotFoundException` in class `Writer1` that writes each string on its own line using `n","prompt":"Write `static void writeLines(String fileName, String[] lines) throws FileNotFoundException` in class `Writer1` that writes each string on its own line using `new PrintWriter(new FileOutputStream(fileName))` (slide 8).\n\nThe starter forgets `close()`. Run it and look: the file stays **empty** (slide 11's \"Java will close it automatically\" is wrong; see notes 08 Errata).","starter":"class Writer1 {\n    static void writeLines(String fileName, String[] lines) throws FileNotFoundException {\n        PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(new FileOutputStream(fileName));\n        for (String line : lines)\n            out.println(line);\n    }\n}","test":"import java.io.*;\nimport java.util.*;\nimport java.nio.file.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n        Writer1.writeLines(\"w1.txt\", new String[]{\"The quick brown fox\", \"jumps over\", \"the lazy dog.\"});\n        List<String> got = Files.readAllLines(Path.of(\"w1.txt\"));\n        if (!got.equals(List.of(\"The quick brown fox\", \"jumps over\", \"the lazy dog.\")))\n            throw new AssertionError(\"file content wrong (did you close the PrintWriter?): \" + got);\n        System.out.println(\"PASS three lines written\");\n        Writer1.writeLines(\"w1.txt\", new String[]{\"only\"});\n        got = Files.readAllLines(Path.of(\"w1.txt\"));\n        if (!got.equals(List.of(\"only\"))) throw new AssertionError(\"opening without append must overwrite: \" + got);\n        System.out.println(\"PASS overwrite\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Writer1 {\n    static void writeLines(String fileName, String[] lines) throws FileNotFoundException {\n        PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(new FileOutputStream(fileName));\n        for (String line : lines)\n            out.println(line);\n        out.close();   // flushes the buffer to disk\n    }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e2","lang":"java","title":"Count lines with BufferedReader","level":"warm","blurb":"Write `static int countLines(String fileName) throws IOException` in class `Counter` using `BufferedReader`/`FileReader`. Loop until `readLine()` returns `null`","prompt":"Write `static int countLines(String fileName) throws IOException` in class `Counter` using `BufferedReader`/`FileReader`. Loop until `readLine()` returns `null` (slide 31). An empty file has 0 lines.","starter":"class Counter {\n    static int countLines(String fileName) throws IOException {\n        BufferedReader in = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(fileName));\n        int n = 0;\n        if (in.readLine() != null) n++;\n        in.close();\n        return n;\n    }\n}","test":"import java.io.*;\nimport java.util.*;\nimport java.nio.file.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n        Files.writeString(Path.of(\"c2.txt\"), \"1 2 3\\nJack jump over\\nthe candle stick.\\n\");\n        int n = Counter.countLines(\"c2.txt\");\n        if (n != 3) throw new AssertionError(\"morestuff2-style file has 3 lines, got \" + n);\n        Files.writeString(Path.of(\"c2e.txt\"), \"\");\n        if (Counter.countLines(\"c2e.txt\") != 0) throw new AssertionError(\"empty file should have 0 lines\");\n        Files.writeString(Path.of(\"c2b.txt\"), \"a\\n\\nb\");\n        if (Counter.countLines(\"c2b.txt\") != 3) throw new AssertionError(\"\\\"a\\\\n\\\\nb\\\" has 3 lines (blank line counts, last line has no newline)\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS countLines\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Counter {\n    static int countLines(String fileName) throws IOException {\n        BufferedReader in = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(fileName));\n        int n = 0;\n        while (in.readLine() != null)\n            n++;\n        in.close();\n        return n;\n    }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e3","lang":"java","title":"Append mode","level":"core","blurb":"Write `static void log(String fileName, String message) throws FileNotFoundException` in class `Logger` that **adds** `message` as a new line at the end of the ","prompt":"Write `static void log(String fileName, String message) throws FileNotFoundException` in class `Logger` that **adds** `message` as a new line at the end of the file, keeping old content (slide 18: `new FileOutputStream(fileName, true)`). If the file does not exist yet, it is created.","starter":"class Logger {\n    static void log(String fileName, String message) throws FileNotFoundException {\n        PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(new FileOutputStream(fileName));\n        out.println(message);\n        out.close();\n    }\n}","test":"import java.io.*;\nimport java.util.*;\nimport java.nio.file.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n        Files.deleteIfExists(Path.of(\"app.log\"));\n        Logger.log(\"app.log\", \"started\");\n        Logger.log(\"app.log\", \"working\");\n        Logger.log(\"app.log\", \"stopped\");\n        List<String> got = Files.readAllLines(Path.of(\"app.log\"));\n        if (!got.equals(List.of(\"started\", \"working\", \"stopped\")))\n            throw new AssertionError(\"expected [started, working, stopped] (append!), got \" + got);\n        System.out.println(\"PASS append\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Logger {\n    static void log(String fileName, String message) throws FileNotFoundException {\n        PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(new FileOutputStream(fileName, true));\n        out.println(message);\n        out.close();\n    }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e4","lang":"java","title":"Sum every int in a file with Scanner","level":"core","blurb":"Write `static int sumInts(String fileName) throws FileNotFoundException` in class `Summer`. Open the file with `new Scanner(new FileInputStream(fileName))` (sli","prompt":"Write `static int sumInts(String fileName) throws FileNotFoundException` in class `Summer`. Open the file with `new Scanner(new FileInputStream(fileName))` (slide 19) and add **all** integers, across all lines, using `hasNextInt()`. Close the scanner.\n\nExample: file `1 2\\n3 4\\n10\\n` → 20.","starter":"class Summer {\n    static int sumInts(String fileName) throws FileNotFoundException {\n        Scanner in = new Scanner(new FileInputStream(fileName));\n        int sum = in.nextInt() + in.nextInt();\n        in.close();\n        return sum;\n    }\n}","test":"import java.io.*;\nimport java.util.*;\nimport java.nio.file.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n        Files.writeString(Path.of(\"s4.txt\"), \"1 2\\n3 4\\n10\\n\");\n        int s = Summer.sumInts(\"s4.txt\");\n        if (s != 20) throw new AssertionError(\"1+2+3+4+10 = 20, got \" + s);\n        Files.writeString(Path.of(\"s4b.txt\"), \"  -5\\n\\n7   8\\n\");\n        if (Summer.sumInts(\"s4b.txt\") != 10) throw new AssertionError(\"-5+7+8 = 10 (ignore extra spaces and blank lines)\");\n        Files.writeString(Path.of(\"s4c.txt\"), \"\");\n        int e;\n        try { e = Summer.sumInts(\"s4c.txt\"); }\n        catch (NoSuchElementException ex) { throw new AssertionError(\"empty file: return 0, do not read past the end\"); }\n        if (e != 0) throw new AssertionError(\"empty file should sum to 0\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS sumInts\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Summer {\n    static int sumInts(String fileName) throws FileNotFoundException {\n        Scanner in = new Scanner(new FileInputStream(fileName));\n        int sum = 0;\n        while (in.hasNextInt())\n            sum += in.nextInt();\n        in.close();\n        return sum;\n    }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e5","lang":"java","title":"Missing file? Use a fallback","level":"core","blurb":"Write `static String firstLineOr(String fileName, String fallback)` in class `Safe`. It returns the first line of the file read with `BufferedReader`. It must *","prompt":"Write `static String firstLineOr(String fileName, String fallback)` in class `Safe`. It returns the first line of the file read with `BufferedReader`. It must **not** declare any exception:\n- file missing → `FileNotFoundException` → return `fallback`;\n- file empty (`readLine()` gives `null`) → return `fallback`;\n- any other `IOException` → return `fallback`.\n\nRemember the catch order: `FileNotFoundException` is a subclass of `IOException`.","starter":"class Safe {\n    static String firstLineOr(String fileName, String fallback) {\n        try {\n            BufferedReader in = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(fileName));\n            String line = in.readLine();\n            in.close();\n            return line;\n        } catch (IOException e) {\n            throw new RuntimeException(e);\n        }\n    }\n}","test":"import java.io.*;\nimport java.util.*;\nimport java.nio.file.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n        Files.writeString(Path.of(\"f5.txt\"), \"Eat my shorts.\\nsecond\\n\");\n        if (!\"Eat my shorts.\".equals(Safe.firstLineOr(\"f5.txt\", \"?\"))) throw new AssertionError(\"should return the first line\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS existing file\");\n        Files.deleteIfExists(Path.of(\"nope5.txt\"));\n        String r;\n        try { r = Safe.firstLineOr(\"nope5.txt\", \"MISSING\"); }\n        catch (RuntimeException e) { throw new AssertionError(\"missing file must return the fallback, not throw \" + e); }\n        if (!\"MISSING\".equals(r)) throw new AssertionError(\"missing file: expected MISSING, got \" + r);\n        Files.writeString(Path.of(\"e5.txt\"), \"\");\n        if (!\"EMPTY\".equals(Safe.firstLineOr(\"e5.txt\", \"EMPTY\"))) throw new AssertionError(\"empty file: expected the fallback\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS fallbacks\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Safe {\n    static String firstLineOr(String fileName, String fallback) {\n        try {\n            BufferedReader in = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(fileName));\n            String line = in.readLine();\n            in.close();\n            return line == null ? fallback : line;\n        } catch (FileNotFoundException e) {   // subclass first\n            return fallback;\n        } catch (IOException e) {\n            return fallback;\n        }\n    }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e6","lang":"java","title":"Count one character with read()","level":"core","blurb":"Write `static int countChar(String fileName, char target) throws IOException` in class `CharCount`. Read the file **one character at a time** with `BufferedRead","prompt":"Write `static int countChar(String fileName, char target) throws IOException` in class `CharCount`. Read the file **one character at a time** with `BufferedReader.read()`, which returns an `int` and `-1` at the end (slides 26, 31). Count how often `target` occurs.","starter":"class CharCount {\n    static int countChar(String fileName, char target) throws IOException {\n        BufferedReader in = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(fileName));\n        String line = in.readLine();\n        in.close();\n        int n = 0;\n        for (char c : line.toCharArray())\n            if (c == target) n++;\n        return n;\n    }\n}","test":"import java.io.*;\nimport java.util.*;\nimport java.nio.file.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n        Files.writeString(Path.of(\"c6.txt\"), \"banana\\nbandana\\n\");\n        int a = CharCount.countChar(\"c6.txt\", 'a');\n        if (a != 6) throw new AssertionError(\"'a' occurs 6 times in banana+bandana, got \" + a);\n        if (CharCount.countChar(\"c6.txt\", '\\n') != 2) throw new AssertionError(\"read() also returns newline characters: expected 2\");\n        if (CharCount.countChar(\"c6.txt\", 'z') != 0) throw new AssertionError(\"'z' does not occur\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS countChar\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class CharCount {\n    static int countChar(String fileName, char target) throws IOException {\n        BufferedReader in = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(fileName));\n        int n = 0;\n        int c;\n        while ((c = in.read()) != -1)\n            if ((char) c == target) n++;\n        in.close();\n        return n;\n    }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e7","lang":"java","title":"Copy and transform","level":"hard","blurb":"Write `static int copyUpper(String inName, String outName) throws IOException` in class `Copier`:","prompt":"Write `static int copyUpper(String inName, String outName) throws IOException` in class `Copier`:\n- read `inName` line by line with `BufferedReader`;\n- write each line in UPPER CASE to `outName` with `PrintWriter` (overwrite mode);\n- skip lines that are empty after `trim()`;\n- return the number of lines written; close both streams.","starter":"class Copier {\n    static int copyUpper(String inName, String outName) throws IOException {\n        BufferedReader in = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(inName));\n        PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(new FileOutputStream(outName));\n        String line = in.readLine();\n        out.println(line);\n        in.close();\n        out.close();\n        return 1;\n    }\n}","test":"import java.io.*;\nimport java.util.*;\nimport java.nio.file.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n        Files.writeString(Path.of(\"in7.txt\"), \"jack jump over\\n\\n   \\nthe candle stick.\\n\");\n        Files.writeString(Path.of(\"out7.txt\"), \"OLD CONTENT\\nOLD\\nOLD\\nOLD\\n\");\n        int n = Copier.copyUpper(\"in7.txt\", \"out7.txt\");\n        List<String> got = Files.readAllLines(Path.of(\"out7.txt\"));\n        if (!got.equals(List.of(\"JACK JUMP OVER\", \"THE CANDLE STICK.\")))\n            throw new AssertionError(\"out7.txt should be [JACK JUMP OVER, THE CANDLE STICK.], got \" + got);\n        if (n != 2) throw new AssertionError(\"should return 2 lines written, got \" + n);\n        System.out.println(\"PASS copyUpper\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Copier {\n    static int copyUpper(String inName, String outName) throws IOException {\n        BufferedReader in = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(inName));\n        PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(new FileOutputStream(outName));\n        int written = 0;\n        String line;\n        while ((line = in.readLine()) != null) {\n            if (line.trim().isEmpty()) continue;\n            out.println(line.toUpperCase());\n            written++;\n        }\n        in.close();\n        out.close();\n        return written;\n    }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."},{"id":"e8","lang":"java","title":"Grade report with bad lines","level":"hard","blurb":"Input file: one student per line, `name score` (e.g. `Ana 72`). Write `static int report(String inName, String outName) throws IOException` in class `Grades`:","prompt":"Input file: one student per line, `name score` (e.g. `Ana 72`). Write `static int report(String inName, String outName) throws IOException` in class `Grades`:\n- for each valid line write `name: PASS` (score ≥ 50) or `name: FAIL` to `outName`, in input order;\n- a line is **bad** if it does not have exactly two tokens (split on whitespace after `trim()`) or the score is not an int (`NumberFormatException`); skip it;\n- blank lines are ignored (not bad);\n- return the number of bad lines.\n\nExample: `Ana 72`, `Bo 49`, `Cem x`, ``, `Dino 50 extra`, `Eda 50` → output `Ana: PASS`, `Bo: FAIL`, `Eda: PASS`; returns 2.","starter":"class Grades {\n    static int report(String inName, String outName) throws IOException {\n        BufferedReader in = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(inName));\n        PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(new FileOutputStream(outName));\n        String line;\n        while ((line = in.readLine()) != null) {\n            String[] p = line.split(\" \");\n            out.println(p[0] + \": \" + (Integer.parseInt(p[1]) >= 50 ? \"PASS\" : \"FAIL\"));\n        }\n        in.close();\n        out.close();\n        return 0;\n    }\n}","test":"import java.io.*;\nimport java.util.*;\nimport java.nio.file.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n        Files.writeString(Path.of(\"g8.txt\"), \"Ana 72\\nBo 49\\nCem x\\n\\nDino 50 extra\\nEda 50\\n\");\n        int bad;\n        try { bad = Grades.report(\"g8.txt\", \"r8.txt\"); }\n        catch (RuntimeException e) { throw new AssertionError(\"bad lines must be skipped, not crash: \" + e); }\n        List<String> got = Files.readAllLines(Path.of(\"r8.txt\"));\n        if (!got.equals(List.of(\"Ana: PASS\", \"Bo: FAIL\", \"Eda: PASS\")))\n            throw new AssertionError(\"report content wrong: \" + got);\n        if (bad != 2) throw new AssertionError(\"expected 2 bad lines (Cem x, Dino 50 extra), got \" + bad);\n        System.out.println(\"PASS report\");\n        Files.writeString(Path.of(\"g8b.txt\"), \"  Zed   90  \\n\");\n        if (Grades.report(\"g8b.txt\", \"r8b.txt\") != 0 || !Files.readAllLines(Path.of(\"r8b.txt\")).equals(List.of(\"Zed: PASS\")))\n            throw new AssertionError(\"extra spaces around tokens must be tolerated\");\n        System.out.println(\"PASS whitespace\");\n    }\n}","solution":"class Grades {\n    static int report(String inName, String outName) throws IOException {\n        BufferedReader in = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(inName));\n        PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(new FileOutputStream(outName));\n        int bad = 0;\n        String line;\n        while ((line = in.readLine()) != null) {\n            line = line.trim();\n            if (line.isEmpty()) continue;\n            String[] p = line.split(\"\\\\s+\");\n            if (p.length != 2) { bad++; continue; }\n            try {\n                int score = Integer.parseInt(p[1]);\n                out.println(p[0] + \": \" + (score >= 50 ? \"PASS\" : \"FAIL\"));\n            } catch (NumberFormatException e) {\n                bad++;\n            }\n        }\n        in.close();\n        out.close();\n        return bad;\n    }\n}","hint":"Read the test (the Main class): every check names the method, the input and the value it expects."}]}],"deliverables":[]};
